window.DEEP={"1":{"h":["Al-Fatihah was revealed in Makkah early in the Prophet's ﷺ mission. Many scholars count it as the first complete surah revealed, though not the first verses. It became the foundation of the daily prayer from the start, and a Muslim recites it at least seventeen times a day.","It has many names, each showing a different role: Umm al-Kitab (the Mother of the Book), al-Sab' al-Mathani (the Seven Oft-Repeated), al-Shifa' (the Cure) and al-Ruqyah (the Healing Recitation). The last name comes from an authentic report in Bukhari: a Companion recited it over a man stung by a scorpion, and the Prophet ﷺ approved.","In a hadith qudsi (Muslim 395), Allah says He has divided the prayer between Himself and His servant into two halves. When the servant says 'All praise is for Allah', Allah replies, 'My servant has praised Me.' The surah is a conversation, not only a recitation."],"s":[[1,4,"Praise of the Lord","Allah is introduced through His names: Lord of all worlds, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful, and Master of the Day of Judgment."],[5,5,"The covenant","The turning point: 'You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help.' The servant moves from praising Allah to speaking to Him directly."],[6,7,"The request","The one request Allah teaches us to make every day: guidance to the straight path of those He has blessed."]],"n":{"1":"Scholars differ on whether the Bismillah is the first verse of Al-Fatihah. In the common Kufan count, followed in most printed copies, it is verse 1.","2":"Al-hamd is praise that includes love and gratitude. 'Lord of the worlds' (Rabb al-'alamin) means He creates, sustains and nurtures everything that exists.","3":"Al-Rahman is mercy vast enough to cover all creation; al-Rahim is mercy that reaches the believers specially. Mercy is mentioned before judgment.","4":"There are two authentic readings: Maliki (Master of) and Maaliki (King of). Both are correct and together they complete the meaning.","5":"In Arabic, placing 'You alone' (iyyaka) first gives the sense of exclusivity. The plural 'we' teaches that worship is communal: every believer prays with the whole ummah.","6":"We ask for guidance in every prayer, even after becoming Muslim, because guidance is needed at every step and never finished.","7":"Those Allah has blessed are named in 4:69: the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs and the righteous. The two paths to avoid are knowledge without action and action without knowledge."},"l":[["Begin everything with Allah's name",1,"The Bismillah turns ordinary actions into worship."],["Praise before asking",2,"The surah teaches the manners of du'a: first praise Allah, then ask."],["Worship and help go together",5,"We need Allah's help even to worship Him."],["Guidance is a daily need",6,"No one is beyond needing to ask for guidance."]],"r":["When you next pray, pause after each verse of Al-Fatihah. What is Allah replying to you?","Which of the two wrong paths, knowing without doing or doing without knowing, am I more at risk of?"],"k":[]},"2":{"h":["Al-Baqarah was the first major surah revealed in Madinah, beginning soon after the Hijrah (622 CE), though its verses came over several years. Its final verses on interest (riba) are among the last revelations of the Quran.","In Madinah the Muslims became a community for the first time, living alongside three Jewish tribes and a group of hypocrites. The surah answers this new situation: it describes three types of people, addresses the Children of Israel at length, changes the direction of prayer (qibla) from Jerusalem to the Ka'bah in the second year after the Hijrah, and sets down laws for fasting, Hajj, family, war and trade.","Its name comes from the story of the cow (verses 67–73): the Children of Israel were told to sacrifice a cow and kept asking needless questions. The lesson is to obey Allah sincerely without looking for excuses. The Prophet ﷺ said Satan flees from a house in which Al-Baqarah is recited (Muslim)."],"s":[[1,5,"The God-conscious","Five qualities of those guided by the Book: belief in the unseen, prayer, giving, faith in all revelation and certainty of the Hereafter."],[6,7,"Those who refuse","Some hearts are sealed by their own persistent rejection."],[8,20,"The hypocrites","Thirteen verses on hypocrisy, with two parables: a fire that goes out, and a rainstorm with thunder and lightning."],[21,29,"A call to all humanity","Worship your Lord who created you. A challenge: produce a single surah like it."],[30,39,"Adam, the angels and Iblis","Allah appoints a khalifah on earth, teaches Adam the names, and Iblis refuses out of pride."],[40,74,"Blessings on the Children of Israel","The rescue from Pharaoh, manna and quails, the golden calf, and the story of the cow."],[75,103,"Broken covenants","Altering scripture, rejecting prophets, and the story of Harut and Marut in Babylon."],[104,121,"Claims and answers","Abrogation, exclusive claims to Paradise, and the truth that east and west belong to Allah."],[122,141,"The religion of Ibrahim","Ibrahim and Isma'il raise the Ka'bah and pray for a messenger from their descendants. Their religion is pure submission."],[142,152,"The new qibla","The direction of prayer changes to the Ka'bah. The ummah is made a 'middle nation'."],[153,177,"Patience and true righteousness","Trials of fear and hunger, Safa and Marwah, lawful food, and the great verse defining righteousness (177)."],[178,203,"Laws of life and worship","Retribution, wills, the fasting of Ramadan, the nearness of Allah, fighting and Hajj."],[204,242,"Society and family","Two kinds of people, wine and gambling, orphans, marriage, divorce, nursing and the waiting period."],[243,260,"Faith and power","Talut, Jalut and Dawud; Ayat al-Kursi; no compulsion in religion; Ibrahim's proofs of resurrection."],[261,283,"Charity, interest and debt","The parable of the grain of seven ears, the prohibition of riba, and the longest verse in the Quran, on recording debts."],[284,286,"The seal of the surah","Faith in all prophets, and the believers' prayer: 'Our Lord, do not burden us beyond what we can bear.'"]],"n":{"2":"'This is the Book in which there is no doubt': the Quran opens its longest surah by describing itself.","3":"Belief in the unseen (al-ghayb) is listed first: Allah, angels, the Hereafter. Faith begins where the senses end.","23":"The challenge (tahaddi) to produce one surah like it was never met by the masters of Arabic.","30":"Khalifah means a successor or steward: humans are entrusted with the earth, not its owners.","34":"Iblis was not an angel but a jinn (18:50). His sin was arrogance: 'I am better than him.'","62":"Sincere believers among earlier communities who believed in Allah and the Last Day and did good have their reward.","67":"The cow story shows how needless questioning turned an easy command into a hard one.","102":"Harut and Marut were two angels sent as a test in Babylon. They warned everyone: 'We are only a trial, so do not disbelieve.'","115":"Revealed, according to Ibn 'Umar, about praying voluntary prayers on a mount in any direction it faced (Muslim).","124":"Ibrahim fulfilled every test, so Allah made him a leader (imam) for humanity.","143":"Ummatan wasatan: a balanced, just middle nation, witness over humanity.","144":"The Prophet ﷺ longed for the Ka'bah and kept looking to the sky; Allah granted his wish.","155":"Trials will come in five forms: fear, hunger, loss of wealth, lives and fruits.","156":"'Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi raji'un': we belong to Allah and to Him we return. The words of the patient.","158":"'A'ishah explained that some Ansar felt uneasy walking between Safa and Marwah, because idols once stood there; this verse reassured them (Bukhari).","177":"Righteousness (birr) is not facing east or west, but faith, generosity, prayer, keeping promises and patience.","183":"Fasting was prescribed in the second year after the Hijrah, 'as it was prescribed for those before you'.","186":"Placed in the middle of the fasting verses: 'I am near; I answer the call of the caller when he calls upon Me.'","187":"Before this verse, some Companions found it hard to eat after sleeping in the night of Ramadan. This brought ease.","219":"The first step in the gradual prohibition of wine: its harm is greater than its benefit.","255":"Ayat al-Kursi, the greatest verse in the Quran (Muslim 810), describes Allah's life, knowledge and power.","256":"'There is no compulsion in religion': faith must be chosen freely.","260":"Ibrahim asked to see how Allah gives life, not from doubt but 'so that my heart may be at rest'.","275":"Riba (interest) is declared unlawful, while trade is lawful.","281":"Ibn 'Abbas reported that this was the last verse revealed to the Prophet ﷺ.","282":"The longest verse of the Quran: write down debts, with witnesses.","286":"'Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity.' The surah ends with a prayer the believers are taught to make."},"l":[["Faith shows in action",177,"Righteousness joins belief with generosity, prayer and keeping promises."],["Do not make religion hard on yourself",67,"Sincere, simple obedience is better than looking for loopholes."],["Allah is near",186,"Talk to Him directly; He answers."],["Patience in loss",156,"Every loss returns to the One it came from."],["Honesty in money",282,"Recording debts and avoiding interest protect trust between people."]],"r":["Which of the five qualities in verses 3–4 is weakest in my life?","How do I respond to commands I don't fully understand: like Ibrahim, or like the people of the cow?","What would my life look like if I truly felt Allah is near (2:186)?"],"k":[261]},"3":{"h":["Ali 'Imran was revealed in Madinah, mostly in the third year after the Hijrah and the years following. Its first part, up to around verse 80, is linked by many scholars to the delegation of sixty Christians from Najran who came to debate the Prophet ﷺ about 'Isa. The Prophet ﷺ invited them to a mutual prayer-curse (mubahalah, verse 61); they declined and made a treaty.","Its second part reflects on the Battle of Uhud (3 AH). About 700 Muslims faced 3,000 Quraysh. The battle began well, but archers on the hill left their post to collect spoils, Khalid ibn al-Walid's cavalry attacked from behind, and around seventy Muslims were martyred, including the Prophet's ﷺ uncle Hamzah. The Prophet ﷺ himself was wounded, and a rumour spread that he had been killed.","Instead of blame, the surah offers healing: it explains why the setback happened, honours the martyrs as alive with their Lord, and teaches the Prophet ﷺ to deal gently with his Companions and keep consulting them."],"s":[[1,9,"The Book and its verses","Clear verses and ambiguous ones; the prayer of the firmly grounded in knowledge."],[10,32,"The true religion","The religion before Allah is Islam (submission). Love for Allah means following the Prophet ﷺ."],[33,63,"The family of 'Imran","The birth of Maryam, Zakariyya's prayer, the birth of 'Isa, his miracles, and the mubahalah."],[64,101,"A common word","An invitation to the People of the Book. Ibrahim was neither Jew nor Christian. The first House of worship is in Makkah."],[102,120,"Hold fast together","Hold firmly to the rope of Allah. You are the best community brought forth for humanity."],[121,148,"Lessons of Uhud, part 1","The memory of Badr, a warning about interest, the qualities of the pious, and 'Muhammad is only a messenger'."],[149,175,"Lessons of Uhud, part 2","Why the setback came, the gentleness of the Prophet ﷺ, and the martyrs alive with their Lord."],[176,189,"Every soul will taste death","This life is temporary enjoyment; the true reward comes on the Day of Resurrection."],[190,200,"The people of understanding","Reflection on creation, heartfelt prayer, Allah's answer, and a final call to patience."]],"n":{"7":"Muhkam verses are clear in meaning and form the foundation; mutashabih verses can bear more than one meaning. Those with deviation in their hearts chase the ambiguous ones.","8":"'Our Lord, do not let our hearts deviate after You have guided us': a du'a for steadfastness.","14":"The things people naturally love are listed honestly: spouses, children, gold, horses, cattle and land. Better is what is with Allah.","19":"'The religion before Allah is Islam': submission to Allah, the religion of every prophet.","26":"'You give sovereignty to whom You will and take it from whom You will.' Reported to have been revealed as a promise of the spread of Islam.","31":"The test of loving Allah: following His Messenger ﷺ.","37":"Zakariyya found fresh provision with young Maryam out of season. Her answer, 'It is from Allah', inspired his own prayer for a child.","59":"'Isa's creation without a father is like Adam's, created without father or mother.","61":"The mubahalah: the Prophet ﷺ came out with 'Ali, Fatimah, Hasan and Husayn (Muslim). The Najran delegation declined.","64":"The Prophet ﷺ quoted this verse in his letter to Heraclius, the Byzantine emperor (Bukhari).","92":"When this verse came, Abu Talhah gave away his most beloved garden, Bayruha' (Bukhari).","96":"Bakkah is another name for Makkah: the first House of worship established for humanity.","103":"The Aws and Khazraj tribes of Madinah had fought for generations. Islam made them brothers.","110":"'You are the best nation': conditional on enjoining good, forbidding evil and believing in Allah.","139":"Revealed after Uhud: 'Do not weaken and do not grieve, for you will be superior if you are believers.'","144":"Abu Bakr recited this verse on the day the Prophet ﷺ died, and people felt as if they had never heard it before (Bukhari).","159":"Gentleness is a mercy from Allah. Even after the mistakes at Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ is told to pardon them and consult them.","173":"'Hasbuna Allah wa ni'ma al-wakil': Allah is sufficient for us. Said when the Muslims were told a huge army was gathering against them.","185":"Every soul will taste death. Whoever is saved from the Fire and admitted to Paradise has truly succeeded.","190":"'A'ishah reported that the Prophet ﷺ wept at night when this verse was revealed (Ibn Hibban).","200":"The final command: be patient, outlast the enemy in patience, stand guard and fear Allah."},"l":[["Steadfast hearts need du'a",8,"Even guidance must be protected through prayer."],["Unity is a blessing",103,"Hold to Allah's rope together; division is the enemy's victory."],["Learn from defeat without despair",139,"Setbacks expose weaknesses so believers can grow."],["Lead with gentleness",159,"Harshness drives people away; mercy and consultation bind them."],["Reflect on creation",190,"The sky at night is a book for those who think."]],"r":["After a failure, do I look for someone to blame or for what I can learn, as Uhud teaches?","What 'most beloved thing' (3:92) could I give for Allah's sake?"],"k":[134,169]},"4":{"h":["An-Nisa was revealed in Madinah, mainly after the Battle of Uhud (3 AH) and over the following years. Uhud left many widows and orphans, and the young Muslim society needed clear rules to protect them. Reports in Abu Dawud and Tirmidhi say the inheritance verses came after the widow of Sa'd ibn al-Rabi', martyred at Uhud, complained that her husband's brother had taken all his wealth, leaving his two daughters nothing.","Before Islam, Arabian custom often denied women and children inheritance and treated women as property that could be inherited. An-Nisa gives women a fixed legal share, a right to their dowry (mahr), and protection from forced marriage and abuse.","The surah also deals with the hypocrites of Madinah, the duty of migration, the prayer of fear in battle, strict justice even against oneself, and a firm correction of beliefs about 'Isa."],"s":[[1,14,"Orphans and inheritance","One soul created all humanity. Protect orphans' wealth; Allah sets fixed shares of inheritance."],[15,42,"Marriage and family","Whom one may marry, the right to mahr, resolving conflict, and excellence toward parents, relatives and neighbours."],[43,57,"Purity and faith","Tayammum when water is unavailable, and the unforgivable sin of shirk."],[58,70,"Trusts and authority","Give trusts to those entitled; obey Allah, His Messenger and those in authority; refer disputes back to Allah and His Messenger."],[71,104,"Struggle, hypocrisy and migration","Defending the oppressed, exposing the hypocrites, the sanctity of a believer's life, migration, and prayer in fear."],[105,126,"Justice and the straight path","Do not defend the dishonest. Satan's false promises. Ibrahim as a model."],[127,134,"Women and reconciliation","Justice to women and orphans; reconciliation between spouses is best."],[135,152,"Stand firm for justice","Be witnesses for Allah even against yourselves; the hypocrites in the lowest depth."],[153,176,"The People of the Book","Broken covenants, 'Isa was not killed nor crucified, do not say 'Three', and the law of kalalah."]],"n":{"1":"All of humanity comes from a single soul. The surah on rights opens with our shared origin, and with honouring the ties of the womb.","3":"The permission to marry up to four is conditional on justice; if you fear injustice, then only one.","11":"The detailed inheritance shares, revealed (according to reports) after a widow pleaded for her daughters' rights.","19":"Forbids the pre-Islamic practice of inheriting women against their will, and commands living with them in kindness.","23":"The list of women one may not marry: a protection of family relations.","29":"'Do not kill yourselves': scholars include both suicide and destroying one another.","36":"Ten rights in one verse: Allah, parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, near and far neighbours, companions, travellers and those in your care.","41":"When Ibn Mas'ud recited up to this verse, the Prophet ﷺ said 'Enough' with tears in his eyes (Bukhari).","43":"Revealed when 'A'ishah's necklace was lost on a journey and there was no water. Tayammum (dry ablution) became a mercy (Bukhari).","58":"Reported to have been revealed when the key of the Ka'bah was returned to 'Uthman ibn Talhah after the conquest of Makkah.","59":"The foundation of Islamic authority: obey Allah, the Messenger, and those in charge; in disputes, return to the Quran and Sunnah.","69":"Those who obey will be with the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs and the righteous. This explains 'those You have blessed' in Al-Fatihah.","82":"'Do they not reflect on the Quran? If it were from other than Allah, they would find much contradiction in it.'","92":"Detailed rulings for accidental killing, showing the sanctity of every life.","97":"Those who stayed in oppression when they could have migrated are questioned: 'Was not Allah's earth spacious?'","105":"Reported to concern a Muslim, Tu'mah, who stole and blamed a Jew. The Quran defended the innocent Jew.","128":"'Reconciliation is best.' Sawdah offered to give her night to 'A'ishah to stay married to the Prophet ﷺ, and this verse is linked to that (Bukhari).","157":"'They did not kill him, nor did they crucify him; it was made to appear so to them.'","171":"'Do not say Three': 'Isa was a messenger of Allah and His word.","176":"Kalalah: one who dies without parents or children. The Companions said this was among the last verses revealed."},"l":[["Rights are a trust",58,"Fulfilling rights, especially of the weak, is worship."],["Justice begins at home",135,"Stand for justice even when it costs you."],["Reconciliation is best",128,"Preserve relationships through compromise."],["Reflect on the Quran",82,"Its consistency is a proof of its source."]],"r":["Of the ten rights in verse 36, which do I neglect most?","Have I ever defended someone I love when they were in the wrong?"],"k":[48,75,86,135]},"5":{"h":["Al-Ma'idah is among the last surahs revealed, in the final years of the Prophet's ﷺ life in Madinah. Verse 3, 'Today I have perfected your religion for you', was revealed on the Day of 'Arafah during the Farewell Pilgrimage (10 AH), on a Friday, as 'Umar confirmed (Bukhari).","By this time Islam had spread across Arabia. The surah gives final rulings on food, hunting during pilgrimage, ablution, theft, wills and wine, which was now completely forbidden. Its theme is 'fulfil your contracts': with Allah and with people.","It addresses Jews and Christians as communities who had received covenants. It recalls the Children of Israel refusing to enter the holy land, the first murder between the two sons of Adam, and ends with the table of food sent down for the disciples of 'Isa and 'Isa's testimony on the Day of Judgment."],"s":[[1,5,"Fulfil your covenants","Lawful and unlawful food, the sacred months, and the perfection of the religion."],[6,11,"Purity and justice","Ablution, and justice even toward those you dislike."],[12,26,"The covenant of Israel","Twelve leaders, a broken covenant, and refusal to enter the holy land, leading to forty years of wandering."],[27,40,"Sanctity of life","The two sons of Adam and the first murder. Whoever saves one life, it is as if he saved all humanity."],[41,50,"Judging by revelation","The Torah, the Gospel and the Quran each guide; judge by what Allah revealed."],[51,66,"Loyalty and the People of the Book","A people whom Allah loves and who love Him."],[67,86,"Deliver the message","The Prophet ﷺ is protected; Christians nearest in affection, whose eyes overflow with tears at the truth."],[87,108,"Lawful and unlawful","Do not forbid good things; wine and gambling are forbidden; hunting in ihram; testimony for wills."],[109,120,"'Isa and the table","The miracles of 'Isa, the table from heaven, and 'Isa's testimony that he only taught worship of Allah."]],"n":{"1":"'Fulfil your contracts' ('uqud): covenants with Allah, marriage, trade and every agreement.","3":"'Today I have perfected your religion for you, completed My favour upon you, and chosen Islam as your religion.'","6":"The verse of wudu' (ablution), ending: 'Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you.'","8":"'Do not let hatred of a people lead you to be unjust. Be just; that is nearer to piety.'","27":"The story of Habil and Qabil (Abel and Cain).","32":"'Whoever kills a soul... it is as if he killed all humanity; whoever saves one, it is as if he saved all humanity.'","48":"'For each of you We made a law and a way. Compete with one another in good.'","54":"Allah will bring a people He loves and who love Him, humble with believers, firm against disbelief.","67":"'Deliver what has been revealed to you.' After it, the Prophet ﷺ dismissed his bodyguards: 'Allah will protect you from people' (Tirmidhi).","83":"Linked by commentators to the Negus of Abyssinia and his companions, who wept when they heard the Quran.","90":"The final prohibition of wine, gambling, idols and divining arrows.","101":"Do not ask about things which, if made clear, would make matters hard for you.","110":"The miracles of 'Isa: speaking in the cradle, the clay bird, healing the blind and leper, and raising the dead, all 'by My permission'.","112":"The disciples asked for a table spread from heaven; the surah takes its name from it.","116":"'Did you say to people, take me and my mother as gods?' 'Isa replies, 'Glory be to You!'","118":"The Prophet ﷺ spent a whole night repeating this verse in prayer, weeping (Nasa'i)."},"l":[["Keep your word",1,"Every promise and contract is a religious duty."],["Justice toward enemies",8,"Hatred is no excuse for injustice."],["Every life is sacred",32,"Saving one life is like saving all humanity."],["Compete in goodness",48,"Differences between communities are a test; race toward good."]],"r":["Is there a promise I have not kept?","Do I let dislike of someone colour how fairly I treat them?"],"k":[2,31,82]},"6":{"h":["Al-An'am was revealed in Makkah, in the later years before the Hijrah. Some reports say it was revealed largely at one time, though scholars discuss the strength of these reports. Its subject is the foundation of faith: tawhid (Allah's oneness), prophethood and the Hereafter.","The Quraysh had invented religious rules about their livestock and crops: some animals dedicated to idols, some forbidden to women, some not to be ridden. The surah takes its name, 'The Cattle', from its refutation of these man-made laws, ending with the ten commandments of verses 151–153.","It is a surah of reasoning. Ibrahim's search among the star, moon and sun (verses 74–79) is presented as a model of thinking one's way to the Creator, and Allah's signs in seeds, dawn, stars and rain invite the reader to reflect."],"s":[[1,11,"The Creator and the deniers","Praise to Allah who created the heavens and earth, yet the disbelievers set up equals to Him."],[12,32,"Mercy and witness","Allah has prescribed mercy upon Himself. The deniers' regret at the Fire."],[33,55,"Consolation and the poor believers","They do not deny you but Allah's signs. Do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening."],[56,73,"The keys of the unseen","Only Allah knows the unseen; not a leaf falls without His knowledge."],[74,90,"Ibrahim's reasoning","Ibrahim turns from star, moon and sun to the Creator. Eighteen prophets named; follow their guidance."],[91,110,"Signs in nature","Allah splits the seed and the dawn; do not insult what others worship."],[111,135,"Following truth, not the majority","If you obeyed most people on earth, they would mislead you."],[136,153,"Pagan laws and the ten commandments","Refuting invented rules on crops and cattle; the commandments of Allah."],[154,165,"The straight path","My prayer, my sacrifice, my life and my death are for Allah, Lord of the worlds."]],"n":{"1":"The surah begins with praise for creation, light and darkness, as a direct answer to idolatry.","12":"'Your Lord has prescribed mercy upon Himself.' The Prophet ﷺ said Allah wrote: 'My mercy prevails over My anger' (Bukhari).","33":"Consolation: 'They do not call you a liar; it is the signs of Allah that the wrongdoers reject.'","38":"Animals and birds are communities like you.","52":"The Quraysh leaders asked the Prophet ﷺ to dismiss poor believers like Bilal before they would sit with him. This verse forbade it (Muslim).","59":"'With Him are the keys of the unseen; none knows them but Him. Not a leaf falls but He knows it.'","60":"Sleep is called a minor death: Allah takes souls by night and returns them by day.","76":"Ibrahim looks at the star and says 'This is my lord'. Many scholars see this as debating his people step by step.","79":"'I have turned my face toward He who created the heavens and earth, inclining to truth.' Recited in the opening supplication of prayer.","82":"Ibn Mas'ud reported the Companions were worried by 'wrongdoing'; the Prophet ﷺ explained it means shirk (Bukhari).","95":"Allah splits the seed and the date-stone, bringing life from death.","103":"Vision cannot grasp Him, but He grasps all vision.","116":"Truth is not decided by numbers.","151":"The ten commandments begin: do not associate anything with Him, be good to parents, do not kill your children for fear of poverty.","153":"'This is My straight path, so follow it.' The Prophet ﷺ drew one line and many lines branching from it to explain this (Ahmad).","160":"A good deed is rewarded tenfold; a bad deed only by its equal.","162":"'My prayer, my sacrifice, my life and my death are for Allah.' Recited in the opening of prayer."},"l":[["Think your way to faith",76,"Islam invites reason and reflection."],["Never look down on the poor",52,"Status in Allah's sight is by sincerity."],["Truth is not a vote",116,"Follow the truth even if few do."],["Dedicate your whole life",162,"Every part of life can be worship."]],"r":["What habits or 'rules' do I follow only because others do?","Where in nature have I seen Allah's signs this week?"],"k":[90,108,122,141,164,165]},"7":{"h":["Al-A'raf was revealed in Makkah in the later period, when the Quraysh's rejection had hardened. It is one of the longest Makkan surahs and a sister to Al-An'am.","It tells the history of humanity from beginning to end: the creation of Adam and Iblis's refusal, the warning to the 'children of Adam', and then a series of prophets (Nuh, Hud, Salih, Lut, Shu'ayb), each rejected by his people. The longest story is Musa's, told from his confrontation with Pharaoh to the golden calf and the Sabbath-breakers.","Its name comes from the Heights (al-A'raf), a place between Paradise and Hell where people whose good and bad deeds are balanced will wait, looking at both, hoping for mercy (verses 46–49)."],"s":[[1,10,"The Book and the warning","A Book sent down; do not follow other guardians. Many towns were destroyed."],[11,25,"Adam and Iblis","Iblis refuses to prostrate, Adam and his wife eat from the tree, repent and are sent to earth."],[26,58,"O children of Adam","Clothing of piety, adornment for prayer, the people of the Heights, and creation in six days."],[59,64,"Nuh","Nuh calls his people; they call him misguided; the flood."],[65,72,"Hud and 'Ad","Hud reminds 'Ad of Allah's blessings; they demand punishment."],[73,79,"Salih and Thamud","The she-camel as a sign; the arrogant leaders kill her."],[80,84,"Lut","An immorality no one had done before them."],[85,93,"Shu'ayb and Madyan","Give full measure; they threaten to expel him."],[94,102,"The pattern of history","Hardship to humble people, then ease to test them; most do not keep their covenant."],[103,171,"Musa, Pharaoh and the Children of Israel","The staff, the magicians who believe, the plagues, the sea, the calf, the seventy men, and the Sabbath-breakers."],[172,206,"The covenant of humanity","Every soul testified 'Yes, You are our Lord'; Allah's beautiful names; the Quran and how to listen."]],"n":{"12":"Iblis's reasoning: 'I am better than him; You created me from fire.' The first sin was arrogance.","23":"Adam's repentance: 'Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves.' The opposite of Iblis's response.","26":"Allah sent clothing to cover and to adorn, 'but the clothing of piety is best'.","31":"'Take your adornment at every mosque; eat and drink but do not waste.' Revealed when some pagans performed tawaf naked.","46":"The people of the Heights: their good and bad deeds are balanced; they hope to enter Paradise.","54":"Allah created the heavens and earth in six days, then rose over the Throne in a manner befitting His majesty.","56":"'Call upon Him in fear and hope; Allah's mercy is near to those who do good.'","59":"From Nuh to Shu'ayb, each prophet begins: 'O my people, worship Allah; you have no god but Him.'","96":"If the people of the towns had believed, Allah would have opened blessings from heaven and earth.","117":"Musa's staff swallowed the magicians' illusions.","120":"The magicians fell in prostration: the same scene as Ash-Shu'ara 26:46.","143":"Musa asked to see Allah; the mountain crumbled when Allah manifested to it, and Musa fell unconscious.","155":"Musa chose seventy men for the meeting with Allah, and prayed when they were seized by the earthquake.","157":"The unlettered Prophet ﷺ, described in the Torah and the Gospel, who relieves people of burdens and shackles.","163":"The Sabbath-breakers: fish came on the Sabbath, and some found tricks to break the law.","172":"Before birth, the descendants of Adam testified: 'Yes, You are our Lord.' This is the fitrah.","176":"The one who abandoned Allah's signs is compared to a dog that pants whether chased or left.","180":"'To Allah belong the most beautiful names, so call upon Him by them.'","199":"'Take what is easy, command what is right, and turn away from the ignorant': described by scholars as a summary of good character.","206":"The first place of prostration (sajdah) in the order of the Quran."},"l":[["Arrogance versus repentance",23,"Iblis argued; Adam repented. Choose Adam's way."],["Mercy is close to the good-doers",56,"Call on Allah in fear and hope."],["History repeats",94,"Ease and hardship are both tests."],["Good character in three words",199,"Pardon, enjoin good, turn from the ignorant."]],"r":["When I am corrected, do I respond like Iblis or like Adam?","Which of Allah's beautiful names do I call on most? Which could I learn next?"],"k":[156,179,204]},"8":{"h":["Al-Anfal was revealed in Madinah after the Battle of Badr, on 17 Ramadan in the second year after the Hijrah (624 CE). About 313 Muslims with few horses set out to intercept a Quraysh caravan led by Abu Sufyan. The caravan escaped, and instead they faced an army of around 1,000.","Against all expectation the Muslims won. Seventy Quraysh were killed, including Abu Jahl, and seventy captured. Afterward the Companions disagreed about the spoils of war (al-anfal), and the surah opens by saying the spoils belong to Allah and His Messenger.","The surah reads like a commentary on the battle: Allah's help through angels, sleep and rain, the throwing of dust, and the lesson that victory comes from Allah. It also covers the treatment of captives, treaties and the bond between the emigrants and the helpers."],"s":[[1,4,"Spoils and true believers","Spoils belong to Allah and His Messenger. True believers' hearts tremble at Allah's mention."],[5,19,"The Day of Badr","Some disliked going out; Allah sent angels, sleep, rain and steadfastness."],[20,28,"Respond to the call","Obey and respond to what gives you life; wealth and children are a test."],[29,40,"Plots and the criterion","The plot against the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah; Allah is the best of planners."],[41,54,"The Day of the Criterion","The fifth of the spoils; Satan deserted his allies at Badr."],[55,66,"Treaties, strength and peace","Prepare strength, but if they incline to peace, incline to it too."],[67,75,"Captives and brotherhood","Captives after Badr; the emigrants and helpers are allies of one another."]],"n":{"1":"The Companions disagreed about the spoils after Badr. The surah begins: fear Allah and set things right between you.","2":"True believers: their hearts tremble when Allah is mentioned, and their faith increases when His verses are recited.","11":"The night before Badr, Allah sent sleep for calm and rain to firm the sand under their feet.","17":"The Prophet ﷺ threw a handful of dust at the enemy: 'You did not throw when you threw, but Allah threw.'","30":"The Quraysh plotted to imprison, kill or expel the Prophet ﷺ on the night of the Hijrah. Allah is the best of planners.","33":"Allah would not punish them while the Prophet ﷺ was among them, nor while they seek forgiveness.","41":"A fifth of the spoils is for Allah and the Messenger, relatives, orphans, the poor and travellers.","48":"Satan made their deeds seem fair, then fled when he saw the angels.","60":"Prepare whatever strength you can to deter aggression.","63":"Allah united their hearts: if you had spent all that is on earth, you could not have done so.","67":"About the captives of Badr: a ransom was taken; this verse gently corrected the decision.","72":"The brotherhood of the Muhajirun and Ansar: those who believed, emigrated and those who gave shelter and support."},"l":[["Victory is from Allah",17,"Do your part, but never credit yourself alone."],["Disunity destroys strength",46,"Arguments drain a community's spirit."],["Change begins within",53,"Blessings remain while we remain grateful."],["Prefer peace",61,"Strength is for deterrence; peace is the aim."]],"r":["What 'calls me to life' (8:24) that I keep delaying?","Where do I need to set things right between me and someone else (8:1)?"],"k":[9,24,27,46,53,61]},"9":{"h":["At-Tawbah was revealed in Madinah in the ninth year after the Hijrah, among the final surahs. It is the only surah without the Bismillah. 'Ali is reported to have explained that the Bismillah is a declaration of safety, while this surah opens with a declaration of disavowal. 'Uthman reported that its content resembled Al-Anfal, and the Prophet ﷺ did not say whether they were one surah (Tirmidhi).","Its opening was announced during the Hajj of 9 AH, led by Abu Bakr, with 'Ali proclaiming it: treaty-breaking idolaters had four months, after which no idolater would perform Hajj and no one would circle the Ka'bah naked.","Most of the surah concerns the expedition to Tabuk against the Byzantines, in intense summer heat and at harvest time. The hypocrites made excuses and stayed behind. Three sincere believers, Ka'b ibn Malik, Murarah ibn al-Rabi' and Hilal ibn Umayyah, also stayed behind without excuse. They were boycotted for fifty days until their repentance was accepted (Bukhari), and the surah takes its name from that repentance."],"s":[[1,16,"The declaration of disavowal","Four months for treaty-breakers; those who honour treaties are honoured; asylum for those who seek it."],[17,29,"Who maintains the mosques","Only believers truly maintain Allah's mosques; nothing should be dearer than Allah and His Messenger."],[30,37,"Claims and the sacred months","The sacred months are four; do not wrong yourselves in them."],[38,59,"The call to Tabuk","'Go forth, light or heavy.' The cave of the Hijrah; the hypocrites' excuses."],[60,60,"The recipients of zakah","Eight categories who receive obligatory charity."],[61,80,"The hypocrites and the believers","Hypocrites mock and break promises; the believers are allies of one another."],[81,99,"Those who stayed behind","Those who rejoiced at staying behind, and the excused: the weak, the sick and those without means."],[100,110,"The forerunners and the Mosque of Harm","The first emigrants and helpers; a mosque built to cause division; the mosque founded on piety."],[111,118,"The bargain and the repentance","Allah has bought the believers' lives and wealth for Paradise. The repentance of the three is accepted."],[119,129,"Be with the truthful","Stay with the truthful; some should stay to learn religion; a Messenger who cares deeply for you."]],"n":{"1":"No Bismillah: the surah begins with a declaration of disavowal from treaty-breakers.","4":"Treaties with those who kept them were honoured until their end.","6":"If an idolater seeks protection, grant it so he may hear Allah's word, then escort him to safety.","24":"If parents, children, wealth, trade or homes are dearer to you than Allah, His Messenger and striving in His way, then wait.","36":"Twelve months, four of them sacred: Dhul-Qa'dah, Dhul-Hijjah, Muharram and Rajab.","40":"In the cave during the Hijrah: 'Do not grieve, Allah is with us.' Abu Bakr was 'the second of the two'.","100":"The forerunners among the Muhajirun and Ansar: Allah is pleased with them and they with Him.","103":"'Take from their wealth a charity to purify them, and pray for them; your prayer is reassurance for them.'","107":"Masjid al-Dirar, built by hypocrites to cause harm and division. The Prophet ﷺ had it demolished after Tabuk.","108":"'A mosque founded on piety from the first day', identified with Quba' and with the Prophet's ﷺ mosque.","118":"The three who stayed behind felt the earth narrow despite its vastness, until Allah accepted their repentance.","119":"Ka'b ibn Malik said truthfulness saved him; he resolved never to lie again (Bukhari).","122":"Not all should go out: a group should stay to gain understanding of religion and teach the others.","128":"'A Messenger has come to you from among yourselves: your suffering grieves him, he is anxious for you, kind and merciful.'","129":"'Hasbiya Allah': Allah is sufficient for me. Reported among the last verses revealed."},"l":[["Honesty brings rescue",119,"Ka'b's truthfulness, though painful, led to his forgiveness."],["Nothing dearer than Allah",24,"Check what truly comes first in your life."],["Repentance is always open",118,"Even after a serious failure, sincere repentance is accepted."],["Seek knowledge as service",122,"Learning religion is a duty to the community."]],"r":["Ka'b chose the truth over an easy excuse. When did I last make an excuse instead of owning a mistake?","Which of the things in verse 24 competes most with Allah in my heart?"],"k":[18,60,71,111]},"10":{"h":["Yunus was revealed in Makkah, likely in the late Makkan period. The Quraysh were astonished that revelation could come to 'a man from among them', and they demanded a different Quran or a miracle.","The surah answers with Allah's signs in the sun, moon, night and day, and with the reality of resurrection. It recalls Nuh, Musa and Pharaoh, and especially Yunus, whose people are the only nation in the Quran that believed after seeing the signs of punishment, and were spared (verse 98).","Its message to Makkah is clear and hopeful: it is not too late to believe."],"s":[[1,20,"The wonder of revelation","Why be amazed that a man among you is a warner? Allah created the heavens and earth, sun and moon."],[21,36,"Mercy, forgetfulness and proof","People call on Allah in a storm, then forget Him when safe."],[37,56,"The Quran's challenge","Bring a surah like it. Every nation has a messenger."],[57,70,"Healing for the hearts","An admonition, a healing and a mercy. The friends of Allah have no fear."],[71,93,"Nuh, Musa and Pharaoh","Nuh's challenge, the magicians, Musa's prayer, and Pharaoh's belief at the moment of drowning."],[94,109,"The people of Yunus","Only Yunus's people believed in time and were saved. No soul believes except by Allah's permission."]],"n":{"5":"The sun is given light (diya') and the moon reflected light (nur); their phases help count years.","12":"When harm touches a person he calls on Allah lying, sitting or standing, then passes on as if he never called.","22":"The ship in the storm: people sincerely call on Allah, then forget Him on land.","38":"The challenge again: produce one surah like it.","57":"The Quran is an admonition, a healing for what is in the hearts, guidance and mercy.","58":"'In the bounty of Allah and His mercy, let them rejoice; it is better than what they hoard.'","62":"The friends of Allah (awliya') have no fear and will not grieve: those who believe and are mindful of Him.","88":"Musa prayed against Pharaoh's wealth, which was being used to lead people astray.","90":"Pharaoh declared faith only when drowning; it was too late.","92":"Pharaoh's body was preserved as a sign for later generations.","98":"The people of Yunus believed before the punishment fell, and Allah lifted it from them."},"l":[["Do not forget Allah after the storm",22,"Gratitude in ease is the test of sincerity in hardship."],["The Quran heals hearts",57,"Bring your worries to it."],["It is never too late until it is",98,"Yunus's people turned in time; Pharaoh did not."]],"r":["Do I remember Allah as much after a crisis ends as during it?","What would 'rejoicing in Allah's mercy' (10:58) look like in my day?"],"k":[2,99]},"11":{"h":["Hud was revealed in Makkah in the later period, a time of grief and rising hostility. The Prophet ﷺ said that Hud and similar surahs had turned his hair grey (Tirmidhi). Scholars point especially to verse 112: 'Stand firm as you have been commanded.'","The surah follows Yunus closely and tells the stories of Nuh (with the most detailed account of the flood and his son who refused the ark), Hud, Salih, Ibrahim's guests, Lut, Shu'ayb and Musa. Each story shows the same truth: the messenger warns, the people refuse, and the believers are saved.","Its central message to the Prophet ﷺ and his followers is to stand firm, not to lean toward the wrongdoers, and to establish prayer."],"s":[[1,24,"A Book perfected","Worship only Allah and seek His forgiveness. Every creature's provision is on Allah."],[25,49,"Nuh and the flood","Nuh builds the ark; his son refuses to board and drowns; the waters recede."],[50,60,"Hud and 'Ad","Seek forgiveness and Allah will send rain. 'Ad deny and are destroyed."],[61,68,"Salih and Thamud","The she-camel and the mighty blast."],[69,83,"Ibrahim's guests and Lut","Angels bring news of Ishaq and Ya'qub, then destroy Lut's town."],[84,95,"Shu'ayb and Madyan","Honest measure; 'Does your prayer command you?'"],[96,123,"Musa and the lessons of history","Pharaoh leads his people into the Fire. Stand firm, pray, and be patient."]],"n":{"7":"'That He may test which of you is best in deeds.'","41":"'In the name of Allah is its sailing and its anchoring': the du'a for boarding a vessel.","42":"Nuh calls to his son: 'Come aboard with us.' He replies he will seek refuge on a mountain.","46":"'He is not of your family; his deed was unrighteous.' Faith, not blood, defines true family.","52":"Hud: 'Seek forgiveness of your Lord; He will send rain and add strength to your strength.'","71":"Sarah laughed at the news of a son in old age.","87":"Shu'ayb's people mocked him: 'Does your prayer command you to tell us how to spend our wealth?'","88":"'I only want reform as much as I can; my success is only from Allah.'","112":"'Stand firm as you have been commanded.' Ibn 'Abbas said no verse was harder on the Prophet ﷺ.","114":"Good deeds erase bad deeds. Revealed about a man who regretted kissing a woman (Bukhari).","120":"These stories are told to strengthen your heart."},"l":[["Faith defines family",46,"Love your family, but truth comes first."],["Steadfastness is the hardest command",112,"Consistency matters more than intensity."],["Good deeds erase bad",114,"Follow a slip with prayer and good."],["Seek forgiveness for blessings",52,"Istighfar opens rain and strength."]],"r":["Where do I need to 'stand firm as commanded' instead of finding a compromise?","What good deed could I do right after my next mistake?"],"k":[6,113]},"12":{"h":["Yusuf was revealed in Makkah toward the end of the Makkan period, often linked to the 'Year of Sorrow', when the Prophet ﷺ lost his wife Khadijah and his uncle Abu Talib. It is the only surah that tells one story from beginning to end, and Allah calls it 'the best of stories' (verse 3).","Some reports say the Quraysh, prompted by Jewish rabbis, asked the Prophet ﷺ about the Israelite who went from Canaan to Egypt. The detailed answer was a sign of revelation.","The story mirrors the Prophet's ﷺ own path: rejected by his own family, driven from home, tested, then raised to authority. When he entered Makkah in triumph years later, he forgave the Quraysh with Yusuf's words: 'No blame upon you today' (verse 92)."],"s":[[1,6,"The dream","Yusuf sees eleven stars, the sun and the moon prostrating; Ya'qub warns him to keep it secret."],[7,20,"The well","The brothers' jealousy, the well, the false blood on the shirt, and Ya'qub's beautiful patience."],[21,35,"The house of al-'Aziz","Temptation by the minister's wife; Yusuf seeks refuge in Allah; the women cut their hands; prison."],[36,42,"Calling to Allah in prison","Yusuf interprets two dreams and invites his fellow prisoners to tawhid."],[43,57,"From prison to power","The king's dream of seven cows; Yusuf's innocence declared; he is put in charge of the storehouses."],[58,82,"The brothers come to Egypt","Yusuf recognises them; the plan to keep Binyamin."],[83,101,"Reunion","Ya'qub's grief and hope; 'I am Yusuf'; the shirt restores sight; the dream fulfilled."],[102,111,"Lessons for the Prophet ﷺ","This is news of the unseen. In their stories is a lesson for people of understanding."]],"n":{"4":"Eleven stars (his brothers), the sun and the moon (his parents).","18":"'Beautiful patience' (sabrun jamil): patience without complaint to people.","23":"Yusuf's answer to temptation: 'I seek refuge in Allah.'","39":"In prison Yusuf preaches tawhid before interpreting dreams.","53":"'I do not acquit myself; the soul is ever inclined to evil, except whom my Lord has mercy on.'","55":"Yusuf asks for responsibility because he is 'a knowing guardian'.","87":"'Do not despair of the mercy of Allah.'","92":"'No blame upon you today.' The Prophet ﷺ repeated these words at the conquest of Makkah.","100":"Allah 'brought me out of prison', not mentioning the well, so as not to embarrass his brothers.","101":"Yusuf's du'a: 'Let me die as a Muslim and join me with the righteous.'"},"l":[["Patience without complaint",18,"Bring your pain to Allah, not to people."],["Chastity is strength",23,"Seek refuge in Allah from temptation."],["Never despair",87,"Ya'qub waited decades and never lost hope."],["Forgive when you have power",92,"Real strength is forgiveness."]],"r":["Who do I need to forgive with 'no blame upon you today'?","Which 'well' or 'prison' in my life might be part of a plan I can't yet see?"],"k":[3,24,33,86]},"13":{"h":["Ar-Ra'd is classified by some scholars as Makkan and by others as Madinan; its style and themes, Allah's signs and the certainty of the Hereafter, are typical of Makkah.","It takes its name from verse 13: the thunder glorifies Allah with His praise. The surah moves between the visible world (rain, gardens, lightning, rivers) and the unseen, showing that everything submits to Allah.","It contains one of the most quoted principles of the Quran: Allah does not change the condition of a people until they change what is in themselves (verse 11)."],"s":[[1,7,"Signs above and below","Allah raised the heavens without pillars, spread the earth, and placed in it rivers and fruits in pairs."],[8,18,"Allah's knowledge and power","He knows what every womb carries; the thunder glorifies Him; truth and falsehood are like water and foam."],[19,29,"Those who understand","The people of insight keep their covenants and repel evil with good. Hearts find rest in remembrance."],[30,43,"Messengers and their signs","Even if a Quran moved mountains, they would not believe. Every term has a decree."]],"n":{"4":"Plants watered by the same water yet differing in taste.","13":"The thunder glorifies Allah with His praise, and the angels too, out of awe.","17":"Truth is like water that benefits people and stays; falsehood is like foam that passes away.","22":"The people of insight are patient, pray, give in secret and openly, and repel evil with good.","28":"'Verily, in the remembrance of Allah do hearts find rest.'","31":"Even a Quran that moved mountains would not convince those determined to deny.","39":"Allah erases what He wills and confirms what He wills; with Him is the Mother of the Book."},"l":[["Change starts inside",11,"Transformation of a community begins with each self."],["Truth lasts",17,"Do not be impressed by noisy falsehood; it fades."],["Rest for the heart",28,"Remembrance of Allah is the true remedy for anxiety."]],"r":["What one change in myself might change my circumstances?","When my heart is restless, where do I go first?"],"k":[2,11]},"14":{"h":["Ibrahim was revealed in Makkah in the later period. It recalls Ibrahim, who settled his son Isma'il and wife Hajar in the barren valley of Makkah, and prayed for the city that was now rejecting his monotheism.","The surah describes the messengers' struggle against their peoples, the parable of the good word and the bad word, and the deep, moving prayer of Ibrahim (verses 35–41). It contrasts gratitude, which brings increase, with ingratitude, which brings loss.","It ends with a vivid description of the Day of Judgment, when the oppressors will be held to account."],"s":[[1,6,"Out of darkness into light","The Book brings people from darkness to light. Musa reminded his people of Allah's days."],[7,21,"Messengers and their peoples","If you are grateful, I will increase you. The messengers rely on Allah; Satan disowns his followers."],[22,34,"The good word and the bad","Satan's speech on Judgment Day; the good word is like a good tree; blessings beyond counting."],[35,41,"The prayer of Ibrahim","Ibrahim prays for Makkah's safety, for his descendants, and for forgiveness."],[42,52,"The Day of Judgment","Allah is not unaware of what the wrongdoers do; the earth will be changed to another earth."]],"n":{"1":"The purpose of the Book: to bring people out of darknesses into light.","22":"Satan's speech on the Last Day: 'I had no authority over you except that I invited you, and you responded.'","24":"The good word is like a good tree: firm roots, branches in the sky. The Prophet ﷺ compared it to the palm tree (Bukhari).","27":"Allah keeps the believers firm with the firm word in this life and the next, interpreted as the questioning in the grave (Bukhari).","37":"'I have settled some of my descendants in an uncultivated valley near Your Sacred House.' Hajar and Isma'il in Makkah.","41":"Ibrahim's prayer for forgiveness for himself, his parents and all believers, recited at the end of prayer by many Muslims."},"l":[["Gratitude multiplies blessings",7,"Thank Allah and see what increases."],["Be a good word",24,"Speech rooted in faith bears fruit for years."],["Pray for your descendants",40,"Ibrahim's prayers are still being answered."]],"r":["What three blessings could I thank Allah for right now?","What 'good word' could I plant this week?"],"k":[7,12,34,35,40]},"15":{"h":["Al-Hijr was revealed in Makkah, likely in the middle period, when the Muslims were few and openly mocked. Some reports place verse 94, 'Proclaim what you are commanded', as the turning point when the Prophet ﷺ began preaching openly.","Al-Hijr (the Rocky Tract) is the land of Thamud, north of Madinah, where houses are carved into rock (today's Mada'in Salih). The surah also tells of Iblis's refusal to bow to Adam and the angels who visited Ibrahim and Lut.","It contains a promise central to Muslim belief: Allah Himself will preserve the Quran (verse 9)."],"s":[[1,15,"Mockery and the preserved reminder","They call him mad. Allah sent down the Reminder and will guard it."],[16,25,"Signs in the heavens and earth","Constellations, the protected sky, and provision measured out."],[26,50,"Adam and Iblis","Created from clay; Iblis refuses and vows to mislead; the righteous in gardens."],[51,77,"Ibrahim's guests and Lut","Good news of a son; destruction of Lut's people."],[78,84,"The people of the Thicket and of al-Hijr","Their carved houses did not protect them."],[85,99,"Proclaim, and worship until certainty","The seven oft-repeated verses; turn away from the mockers; worship until death."]],"n":{"9":"'Indeed We sent down the Reminder, and indeed We will guard it.' A promise of preservation.","22":"The winds are 'fertilising', carrying clouds and pollen.","29":"Allah breathed into Adam from His spirit, a sign of human honour.","39":"Iblis vows to make wrong seem attractive to people on earth.","42":"Iblis has no authority over Allah's sincere servants.","87":"The seven oft-repeated verses: Al-Fatihah (Bukhari 4704).","94":"'Proclaim openly what you are commanded.'","97":"'We know your heart is distressed by what they say': Allah's comfort to the Prophet ﷺ.","99":"'Worship your Lord until certainty (death) comes to you.'"},"l":[["The Quran is protected",9,"Trust the text you read; Allah guards it."],["Mercy is announced first",49,"His forgiveness is mentioned before His punishment."],["Glorify when distressed",98,"The remedy for a tight chest is praise and prostration."]],"r":["When people's words hurt me, do I turn to glorifying Allah?","What would 'worship until certainty' mean for my daily habits?"],"k":[49,82]},"16":{"h":["An-Nahl was revealed in Makkah, mostly in the late period, though some verses near the end (such as 110 and 126–128) are thought by many scholars to be Madinan.","It is called the 'surah of blessings'. It lists Allah's gifts one by one: cattle, horses, rain, crops, the sea and its fish, stars for navigation, homes and clothing, and the bee that produces healing honey. Each is a reason for gratitude and a sign of the Creator.","It also contains verse 90, which 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ordered to be recited at the end of Friday sermons, a practice that continues across the Muslim world today."],"s":[[1,21,"The blessings of creation","Cattle, horses, rain, crops, the sea and stars: can the One who creates be like one who does not?"],[22,50,"The proud and the humble","The arrogant reject; everything in heaven and earth prostrates to Allah."],[51,77,"Allah's provision","Milk from between dung and blood, fruits, and the bee and its honey."],[78,100,"Justice, kindness and faithfulness","Allah commands justice, excellence and giving to relatives; keep your oaths."],[101,128,"Steadfastness and wisdom","Ibrahim as a nation in himself; call to Allah with wisdom; patience is with Allah's help."]],"n":{"43":"'Ask the people of knowledge if you do not know.'","58":"Condemnation of those who grieved at the birth of a daughter.","68":"Allah inspired the bee to build homes in mountains, trees and trellises.","69":"From the bee's belly comes a drink of varied colours, with healing for people.","90":"'Allah commands justice, excellence, and giving to relatives, and forbids indecency, wrong and oppression.'","92":"Do not be like the woman who unravels her yarn after spinning it strongly: do not break oaths.","106":"An exception for one forced to utter disbelief while his heart is firm, linked to 'Ammar ibn Yasir under torture.","120":"Ibrahim was a nation (ummah) by himself.","126":"Reported to have been revealed after Uhud: if you retaliate, do so equally, but patience is better."},"l":[["Count your blessings",18,"Gratitude grows when we notice."],["The whole of ethics in one verse",90,"Justice, excellence and generosity; no indecency or oppression."],["Invite with wisdom",125,"How we speak matters as much as what we say."],["A good life is promised",97,"Faith with good deeds brings a good life, for men and women alike."]],"r":["Which ordinary blessing (milk, honey, rain) have I never thanked Allah for?","Do I share my faith with wisdom and gentleness, or with argument?"],"k":[8,18,66,97,125,128]},"17":{"h":["Al-Isra was revealed in Makkah, about a year before the Hijrah. It opens with the Night Journey (al-Isra'), when the Prophet ﷺ was taken by night from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah to al-Masjid al-Aqsa in Jerusalem, and from there ascended through the heavens (the Mi'raj, described in An-Najm). The five daily prayers were prescribed on that night (Bukhari, Muslim).","When he told the Quraysh, many mocked him. Abu Bakr said, 'If he said it, then it is true', earning the title al-Siddiq. The surah is also called Bani Isra'il (the Children of Israel), because it recalls their two corruptions in the land and their punishments.","Verses 23–39 contain a set of commandments, a complete moral code: honour parents, give to relatives and the poor, avoid waste, do not kill children, do not approach adultery, protect orphans' wealth, give full measure, and do not walk arrogantly."],"s":[[1,8,"The Night Journey and Bani Isra'il","From Makkah to al-Aqsa; the two corruptions of the Children of Israel."],[9,22,"Guidance and responsibility","The Quran guides to what is most upright; every person's record is fastened to his neck."],[23,39,"The commandments","Kindness to parents, generosity without waste, honesty, humility."],[40,60,"Proofs and warnings","If there were other gods, they would seek a way to the Throne; everything glorifies Him."],[61,72,"Adam and Iblis; human dignity","Iblis's challenge; Allah has honoured the children of Adam."],[73,100,"Prayer, the Quran and the spirit","The times of prayer, the night prayer, the Quran as healing, and the question about the spirit."],[101,111,"Musa and the final word","Musa's nine signs; the Quran revealed in parts; praise to Allah who has no son or partner."]],"n":{"1":"Glory to Him who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed.","9":"This Quran guides to what is most upright.","13":"Every person's deeds are fastened to his neck; a book will be laid open.","15":"No one bears another's burden; Allah does not punish until a messenger is sent.","23":"'Do not say to them uff (a word of irritation), nor repel them, but speak to them a noble word.'","31":"Do not kill your children for fear of poverty; We provide for them and you.","32":"'Do not approach zina': not just the act, but what leads to it.","36":"Do not follow what you have no knowledge of; hearing, sight and heart will be questioned.","37":"Do not walk on earth arrogantly; you cannot split the earth or reach the mountains in height.","44":"Everything glorifies Him, but you do not understand their glorification.","70":"'We have honoured the children of Adam.'","78":"The times of prayer from the sun's decline to the darkness of night, and the Quran of dawn, which is witnessed.","79":"The night prayer (tahajjud) is an extra gift; perhaps Allah will raise you to a praised station.","81":"'Truth has come and falsehood has vanished.' The Prophet ﷺ recited it while destroying idols at the conquest of Makkah (Bukhari).","85":"'They ask you about the spirit. Say: the spirit is of my Lord's command, and you have been given little knowledge.'","110":"Recite neither too loud nor too quietly. Revealed while the Prophet ﷺ was hiding in Makkah (Bukhari)."},"l":[["Parents deserve gentleness",23,"Not even a sigh of irritation."],["Do not approach sin",32,"Guard the paths that lead to it."],["Speak only with knowledge",36,"Your hearing, sight and heart will be questioned."],["Night prayer raises you",79,"Quiet worship builds a high station."]],"r":["When did I last say 'uff' to my parents, in word or attitude?","What do I spread or repeat without knowing it is true (17:36)?"],"k":[24,26,82]},"18":{"h":["Al-Kahf was revealed in Makkah. According to a report in the sirah of Ibn Ishaq, the Quraysh sent two men to the Jewish rabbis of Madinah, who suggested three questions to test the Prophet ﷺ: about young men who disappeared in ancient times, a great traveller who reached the east and west, and the spirit.","The Prophet ﷺ said 'I will tell you tomorrow' without saying 'in sha' Allah', and revelation paused for about fifteen days. When it came, it answered the questions and taught: 'Never say of anything, I will do it tomorrow, without adding: if Allah wills' (verses 23–24).","The surah contains four stories of trial: faith (the youths of the cave), wealth (the owner of two gardens), knowledge (Musa and al-Khidr) and power (Dhul-Qarnayn). Scholars connect these to the trials of the Dajjal, and the Prophet ﷺ said that memorising its first ten verses protects from him (Muslim)."],"s":[[1,8,"The straight Book","Praise to Allah who sent down a Book without crookedness. The earth's adornment is a test."],[9,26,"The youths of the cave","Young believers flee persecution, sleep for 309 years, and wake as a sign."],[27,31,"Patience with the sincere","Keep yourself with those who call on their Lord morning and evening."],[32,44,"The two gardens","A rich man's pride destroys his garden; his poor friend's faith endures."],[45,59,"The reality of this world","Like rain on plants that soon become dry stubble; wealth and children are adornment."],[60,82,"Musa and al-Khidr","Musa seeks knowledge; the boat, the boy and the wall reveal Allah's hidden wisdom."],[83,98,"Dhul-Qarnayn","A just ruler who travels east and west and builds a barrier against Ya'juj and Ma'juj."],[99,110,"The final reminder","The greatest losers; if the sea were ink for Allah's words; do good and associate none with Him."]],"n":{"1":"The first ten verses protect from the Dajjal (Muslim 809).","10":"The youths' du'a: 'Our Lord, grant us mercy from Yourself and guide us rightly.'","13":"They were young men who believed, and Allah increased them in guidance.","18":"Their dog stretched its paws at the entrance: even an animal is honoured by good company.","22":"People argue about their number; 'My Lord knows best their number.'","24":"Always say 'in sha' Allah' when speaking of the future.","39":"'Ma sha' Allah, la quwwata illa billah': what you should say when admiring your blessings.","46":"Wealth and children are adornment; lasting good deeds are better with your Lord.","65":"Al-Khidr: a servant given mercy and knowledge from Allah.","66":"Musa, a great prophet, humbly asks to follow and learn.","79":"The boat was damaged to save it from a king seizing every good ship.","94":"Dhul-Qarnayn builds a barrier for a people threatened by Ya'juj and Ma'juj.","98":"'This is a mercy from my Lord': the powerful ruler credits Allah.","103":"The greatest losers are those whose effort is lost while they think they are doing well."},"l":[["Faith needs good company",28,"Stay close to those who remember Allah."],["Credit Allah for success",39,"Say ma sha' Allah when admiring what you have."],["Seek knowledge humbly",66,"Even Musa followed a teacher."],["Allah's plan is wiser",82,"What looks like harm may be protection."]],"r":["Which of the four trials (faith, wealth, knowledge, power) is my biggest test now?","When something bad happens, can I trust there may be a 'hidden treasure' behind it?"],"k":[7,28,82,109,110]},"19":{"h":["Maryam was revealed in Makkah, around the fifth year of the mission, before the first migration to Abyssinia. When the Quraysh sent envoys to the Negus (al-Najashi) to demand the return of the Muslim refugees, Ja'far ibn Abi Talib recited the opening of this surah. The Christian king wept until his beard was wet and refused to hand them over (Ahmad).","It is the only surah named after a woman, and Maryam is the only woman named in the Quran. It tells of the miraculous births of Yahya to the elderly Zakariyya and of 'Isa to Maryam, then the gentle way Ibrahim called his father, followed by mention of Musa, Isma'il and Idris.","The name al-Rahman (the Most Merciful) occurs sixteen times, more than in any other surah. Its tone is tender, and many verses end with a soft rhyme in '-iyya'."],"s":[[1,15,"Zakariyya and Yahya","An old man's quiet prayer for an heir, answered with Yahya."],[16,40,"Maryam and 'Isa","The angel's visit, the birth under the palm tree, and the baby speaking in the cradle."],[41,50,"Ibrahim and his father","Ibrahim's gentle words, 'O my father', and his father's harsh reply."],[51,65,"The prophets","Musa, Isma'il and Idris, who fell in prostration weeping when Allah's verses were recited."],[66,98,"Resurrection and the Most Merciful","Man asks, 'When I die, will I be raised?' The claim that al-Rahman has a son is enormous."]],"n":{"3":"'He called his Lord in a secret call.' Zakariyya's du'a was quiet and humble.","4":"'My bones have weakened and my head is aflame with white hair, but I was never disappointed in my prayers to You.'","23":"Labour pains drove her to a palm trunk: 'I wish I had died before this.'","25":"Shake the palm trunk and fresh dates will fall: effort alongside trust in Allah.","30":"The baby 'Isa's first words: 'I am the servant of Allah.'","36":"''Isa said: Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him.'","42":"Ibrahim's gentle 'O my father' (ya abati), repeated four times.","47":"Even when threatened, Ibrahim says: 'Peace be upon you; I will ask my Lord to forgive you.'","58":"When the verses of al-Rahman were recited, they fell in prostration weeping. A place of prostration.","71":"Everyone will pass over Hell; then the God-fearing are saved.","88":"'They say the Most Merciful has taken a son.' The heavens almost split at the claim.","96":"Al-Rahman will place love (wudd) for those who believe and do good."},"l":[["Pray quietly and persistently",4,"Zakariyya was never disappointed in his du'a."],["Combine effort and trust",25,"Maryam shook the tree; Allah dropped the dates."],["Call family with gentleness",42,"'O my father': respect even in disagreement."],["Faith brings love",96,"Allah places love in hearts for the righteous."]],"r":["Who in my family do I need to speak to with Ibrahim's gentleness?","Am I persistent in du'a, like Zakariyya, or do I give up?"],"k":[16]},"20":{"h":["Ta-Ha was revealed in Makkah, in the early-middle period, around the same time as Maryam. It is connected to the conversion of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. Setting out to kill the Prophet ﷺ, 'Umar learned that his sister Fatimah and her husband had become Muslim. He found them reading the opening of Ta-Ha, read it himself, and his heart softened; he went to the Prophet ﷺ and accepted Islam (Ibn Ishaq).","Its opening, 'We did not send down the Quran to you to cause you distress', reassured a Prophet ﷺ weighed down by his people's rejection.","Most of the surah is the story of Musa: the burning bush, his mission with Harun to Pharaoh, the magicians, the crossing of the sea and the golden calf made by al-Samiri. It ends with Adam's forgetting, Allah's forgiveness, and a warning about turning away from Allah's remembrance."],"s":[[1,8,"Not for distress","The Quran is a reminder for those who fear; the Most Merciful above the Throne."],[9,36,"Musa at the fire","'I am your Lord, remove your sandals.' Musa's du'a for an open chest and Harun as helper."],[37,76,"Musa's childhood and the magicians","Allah's care for baby Musa; the confrontation with Pharaoh; the magicians believe."],[77,98,"The sea and the calf","Crossing the sea; al-Samiri leads the people to worship a golden calf."],[99,114,"The Day and the Quran","Mountains blown to dust; 'My Lord, increase me in knowledge.'"],[115,135,"Adam, and turning away","Adam forgot and was forgiven; a narrow life for whoever turns from Allah's remembrance."]],"n":{"2":"'We have not sent down the Quran to you to cause you distress.'","12":"'I am your Lord, so remove your sandals; you are in the sacred valley of Tuwa.'","25":"Musa's du'a before a hard task: 'My Lord, expand my chest for me and ease my task.'","39":"Musa's mother placed him in a chest on the river: 'I cast upon you love from Me.'","44":"Speak to Pharaoh with gentle words. If gentleness is commanded toward Pharaoh, how much more toward others?","46":"'Do not fear; I am with you both, I hear and I see.'","72":"The magicians: 'Decree whatever you will; you can only decree for this worldly life.'","85":"Al-Samiri led them astray while Musa was away for forty nights.","94":"Harun: 'O son of my mother, do not seize me by my beard.' He feared division.","114":"'My Lord, increase me in knowledge.' The only thing the Prophet ﷺ was told to ask for more of.","132":"Command your family to pray and be patient in it."},"l":[["Gentleness even with tyrants",44,"Soft words open hearts."],["Ask for an open chest",25,"Before a hard task, make Musa's du'a."],["Always ask for more knowledge",114,"Learning never ends."],["Remembrance widens life",124,"Turning from Allah makes life narrow, whatever your wealth."]],"r":["Which 'Pharaoh' in my life do I need to speak to more gently?","Does my life feel narrow? How is my remembrance of Allah?"],"k":[14,115,124]},"21":{"h":["Al-Anbiya' was revealed in Makkah, in the middle period. Ibn Mas'ud listed it with Al-Isra, Al-Kahf, Maryam and Ta-Ha as among his earliest treasures (Bukhari 4994).","The Quraysh dismissed the Prophet ﷺ as a poet or sorcerer and the Hour as far away. The surah answers: 'Mankind's reckoning has drawn near, yet they turn away' (verse 1).","It then names sixteen prophets in quick succession: Musa, Harun, Ibrahim, Lut, Ishaq, Ya'qub, Nuh, Dawud, Sulayman, Ayyub, Isma'il, Idris, Dhul-Kifl, Yunus, Zakariyya and Yahya, with Maryam and 'Isa. Each was tested and each called on Allah, and Allah answered. They are 'one community' (verse 92), and Muhammad ﷺ is sent as 'a mercy to the worlds' (verse 107)."],"s":[[1,15,"The reckoning is near","People are heedless and call the Quran confused dreams."],[16,50,"Not for play","Creation has purpose; if there were other gods, heaven and earth would be ruined; every soul tastes death; the criterion given to Musa and Harun."],[51,73,"Ibrahim breaks the idols","Ibrahim smashes the idols and is thrown into the fire, which becomes cool and safe."],[74,91,"The prophets who called and were answered","Lut, Nuh, Dawud, Sulayman, Ayyub, Isma'il, Idris, Dhul-Kifl, Yunus, Zakariyya and Maryam."],[92,112,"One community, one mercy","All prophets are one ummah; the righteous will inherit the earth; a mercy to the worlds."]],"n":{"1":"'Mankind's reckoning has drawn near, while they turn away in heedlessness.'","30":"The heavens and earth were joined and We parted them; from water We made every living thing.","63":"Ibrahim points to the biggest idol: 'Ask them, if they can speak.'","83":"Ayyub's du'a: 'Adversity has touched me, and You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.'","87":"Yunus in the darkness of the whale: 'There is no god but You, glory be to You; I was among the wrongdoers.' The Prophet ﷺ said no Muslim prays with it except Allah answers (Tirmidhi).","89":"Zakariyya: 'My Lord, do not leave me alone, and You are the best of inheritors.'","92":"'This community of yours is one community, and I am your Lord, so worship Me.'"},"l":[["Life has purpose",16,"Nothing was created for play."],["Every trial has a du'a",87,"The prayer of Yunus is an answer for every darkness."],["Hasten to good",90,"The prophets were quick to good and humble in prayer."],["Be a mercy",107,"Follow the Prophet ﷺ by being a mercy to others."]],"r":["Which prophet's du'a in this surah speaks most to my situation?","How can I be a mercy to someone today?"],"k":[16,22,35,47,69,90,105,107]},"22":{"h":["Al-Hajj contains both Makkan and Madinan verses, and scholars differ on how to classify it. Its opening scenes of the Last Day are Makkan in style, while the permission to fight (verse 39) is widely held to be the first verse giving Muslims permission to defend themselves, revealed after the Hijrah.","It tells how Ibrahim was shown the site of the House and commanded to call humanity to pilgrimage, 'and they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel from every distant pass' (verse 27). It explains the rites of Hajj and their spirit.","In the Shafi'i school it has two places of prostration (verses 18 and 77), which is distinctive among surahs."],"s":[[1,24,"The earthquake of the Hour","Mothers forget their infants; the stages of creation as proof of resurrection; everything prostrates to Allah."],[25,37,"The House and the pilgrimage","Ibrahim calls people to Hajj; honour the symbols of Allah; it is piety that reaches Him."],[38,48,"Permission to defend","Those wronged are permitted to fight; if Allah did not repel some by others, places of worship would be destroyed."],[49,78,"Steadfast faith","Satan's insinuations; a fly cannot be created by the false gods; Allah chose you and placed no hardship in religion."]],"n":{"1":"'O people, fear your Lord; the earthquake of the Hour is a tremendous thing.'","5":"The stages of creation in the womb, and dead earth revived by rain, are proofs of resurrection.","11":"Some worship Allah 'on an edge': if good comes they are content, if trial comes they turn back.","27":"'Proclaim the pilgrimage to people; they will come on foot and on every lean camel.'"},"l":[["Worship is not conditional",11,"Do not worship Allah only when life is easy."],["Sincerity is what reaches Allah",37,"The inner state matters more than the outward act."],["Protect all places of worship",40,"Defending faith includes defending others' right to worship."],["Religion is ease",78,"Islam brings no undue hardship."]],"r":["Am I worshipping 'on an edge', faithful only when things go well?","Is my heart seeing, or only my eyes (22:46)?"],"k":[2,18,32,37,39,40,46,73,77,78]},"23":{"h":["Al-Mu'minun was revealed in Makkah, in the middle to late period. 'Umar reported that when it was revealed, the Prophet ﷺ said ten verses had been sent down; whoever lives by them will enter Paradise, and recited the first ten verses (Tirmidhi, Ahmad).","It opens with the qualities of successful believers, traces human creation stage by stage, and recalls Nuh, Musa, Harun, Maryam and 'Isa. It shows that all messengers brought one message, and that people later split into sects 'each rejoicing in what it has'.","Its final verses show the regret of those who wasted their lives, and a question: 'Did you think We created you without purpose, and that you would not be returned to Us?' (verse 115)."],"s":[[1,11,"The successful believers","Humble prayer, avoiding vain talk, zakah, chastity, keeping trusts, guarding prayer."],[12,22,"The stages of creation","From clay to drop to clot to flesh to bones, then another creation."],[23,56,"The messengers","Nuh, the messengers after him, Musa and Harun, Maryam and her son. One community, divided by people."],[57,77,"Those who hasten to good","They give while their hearts tremble; the heedless are absorbed in their wealth."],[78,118,"Signs and the final question","Hearing, sight and hearts given; a barrier after death; 'Did you think We created you in vain?'"]],"n":{"1":"'Certainly the believers have succeeded.' The first ten verses are a description of Paradise's people.","2":"They are humble (khashi'un) in their prayer.","3":"They turn away from vain, idle talk.","51":"'O messengers, eat of good things and do righteous work.' Lawful food is linked to accepted deeds (Muslim).","60":"'A'ishah asked if these were people who sin; the Prophet ﷺ said they are those who pray, fast and give charity yet fear it will not be accepted (Tirmidhi).","99":"At death, the wrongdoer says: 'My Lord, send me back, that I may do good.'","100":"Behind them is a barrier (barzakh) until the Day they are raised.","115":"'Did you think We created you without purpose?'"},"l":[["Success has ten qualities",1,"Measure yourself by the first ten verses."],["Lawful food, accepted deeds",51,"What you consume affects your worship."],["Do good, but stay humble",60,"Fear that your deeds may not be accepted keeps you sincere."],["Life has a purpose",115,"Every moment will be accounted for."]],"r":["Which of the qualities in verses 1–11 is strongest in me, and which is weakest?","If I asked to be 'sent back' to do good, what would I do? Can I do it now?"],"k":[8,14,53,61,96,118]},"24":{"h":["An-Nur was revealed in Madinah, around the fifth or sixth year after the Hijrah. Its central event is the 'incident of the slander' (hadith al-ifk). Returning from the expedition against Banu al-Mustaliq, 'A'ishah was accidentally left behind while searching for a lost necklace. Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal found her and brought her back on his camel.","The hypocrites, led by 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, spread rumours. For about a month there was no revelation, while 'A'ishah fell ill and wept day and night. Then verses 11–26 were revealed, declaring her innocence from above the seven heavens (Bukhari).","From this crisis came laws to protect honour and family: punishment for false accusation, the requirement of four witnesses, asking permission before entering homes, modesty for men and women, and encouragement of marriage. At the centre is the Verse of Light (35)."],"s":[[1,10,"Laws of chastity","Punishment for zina and for false accusation; the procedure of li'an between spouses."],[11,26,"The slander","'A'ishah's innocence declared; why did you not think well of each other?"],[27,34,"Privacy and modesty","Seek permission before entering homes; lower your gaze; marriage encouraged."],[35,46,"The Verse of Light","Allah is the Light of the heavens and earth; houses where His name is remembered."],[47,57,"Obedience and promise","Hypocrites' half-obedience; Allah promises the believers succession on earth."],[58,64,"Manners at home and with the Prophet ﷺ","Asking permission at times of privacy; eating together."]],"n":{"4":"Accusing chaste women without four witnesses is punished, and such accusers' testimony is never accepted.","11":"'Do not think it bad for you; rather it is good for you.' Even a painful trial brought lasting laws.","15":"'You received it with your tongues and said with your mouths what you had no knowledge of, thinking it trivial, while it was grave with Allah.'","22":"Abu Bakr vowed to stop supporting Mistah, a relative who had spread the slander. This verse asked: 'Do you not love that Allah forgive you?' He resumed his support (Bukhari).","30":"Tell believing men to lower their gaze and guard their chastity.","35":"'Allah is the Light of the heavens and the earth', compared to a lamp in a niche lit from a blessed olive tree.","61":"No blame in eating together or separately, in relatives' or friends' homes."},"l":[["Think well of others",12,"Assume the best of believers."],["Do not spread rumours",15,"What seems small on the tongue is grave with Allah."],["Forgive to be forgiven",22,"Do you not love that Allah forgive you?"],["Modesty protects society",30,"Lowering the gaze applies to men first."]],"r":["When I hear gossip, do I pass it on, or say 'this is a clear lie' as verse 12 teaches?","Is there someone I stopped helping because they hurt me? What would Abu Bakr do?"],"k":[1,12,19,27,31,37,39,55]},"25":{"h":["Al-Furqan was revealed in Makkah, in the middle period. The Quraysh raised objection after objection: why does this messenger eat food and walk in the markets? Why is no angel sent with him? Why was the Quran not revealed all at once? The surah answers each one.","It describes the Quran as al-Furqan, the Criterion that separates truth from falsehood. It recalls the fates of Musa's and Nuh's opponents, 'Ad, Thamud and the people of al-Rass, and draws signs from shade, night, wind, rain and the two seas.","It ends with one of the most beautiful passages in the Quran: the portrait of 'the servants of the Most Merciful' ('ibad al-Rahman), who walk humbly, pray at night, spend moderately, avoid great sins, and pray for righteous families (verses 63–77)."],"s":[[1,20,"The Criterion and the objections","Blessed is He who sent down the Criterion; the deniers' objections answered."],[21,34,"The Day of regret","The wrongdoer bites his hands; the Prophet ﷺ complains that his people abandoned the Quran."],[35,44,"Lessons of the nations","Musa, Nuh, 'Ad, Thamud, the people of al-Rass; those who take their desires as their god."],[45,62,"Signs in nature","The extending shade, night as covering, winds bearing good news, two seas with a barrier."],[63,77,"The servants of the Most Merciful","Humility, night prayer, moderation, purity, repentance and righteous families."]],"n":{"7":"'What is this messenger that eats food and walks in the markets?' Being human is not a flaw in a messenger.","27":"The wrongdoer will bite his hands: 'I wish I had taken a path with the Messenger.'","28":"'I wish I had not taken so-and-so as a friend.' Reported to concern 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, who turned from Islam under a friend's pressure.","30":"'My Lord, my people have treated this Quran as something abandoned.'","63":"The servants of the Most Merciful walk on the earth humbly, and when the ignorant address them, they say 'peace'.","67":"When they spend, they are neither extravagant nor stingy, but in between.","72":"They do not bear false witness, and pass by vain talk with dignity.","74":"'Our Lord, grant us from our spouses and children comfort to our eyes, and make us leaders of the God-fearing.'"},"l":[["Do not abandon the Quran",30,"Reading, understanding and living it are all part of honouring it."],["Choose friends carefully",28,"A friend can lead to regret on the Last Day."],["Moderation in spending",67,"Neither extravagant nor stingy."],["Repentance transforms",70,"Sins can be turned into good deeds."]],"r":["Am I one of those who has 'abandoned' the Quran in some way?","Read verses 63–77 slowly. Which quality of 'ibad al-Rahman do I most want to grow?"],"k":[1,32,43,53,64,70]},"27":{"h":["An-Naml was revealed in Makkah in the middle period, between Ash-Shu'ara and Al-Qasas, the three 'Tawasin' surahs. It continues the same themes: the Quran as clear guidance, the prophets and their peoples, and the signs of Allah.","Its heart is the story of Sulayman, who was given a vast kingdom and understood the speech of birds and ants. Hearing an ant warn her colony, he smiled and prayed in gratitude. Through a hoopoe he learned of the Queen of Sheba (Bilqis), whose people worshipped the sun; her journey ends with her submitting to Allah 'with Sulayman'.","Where Ash-Shu'ara shows prophets facing rejection, An-Naml shows a believing king using power with gratitude, and a ruler choosing wisdom over pride."],"s":[[1,6,"Guidance for believers","The Quran is guidance and good news for those who pray and are certain of the Hereafter."],[7,14,"Musa at the fire","Musa's signs; Pharaoh's people rejected them though their hearts were convinced."],[15,44,"Sulayman and the Queen of Sheba","The ant, the hoopoe, the letter, the throne, and the queen's submission."],[45,58,"Salih and Lut","Nine plotters in Thamud; Lut's people who sought to expel the pure."],[59,93,"Who is better?","A series of questions: who created, who answers the distressed, who guides in darkness? Signs of the Hour."]],"n":{"14":"They rejected the signs out of injustice and arrogance, though their souls were convinced.","16":"Sulayman: 'We have been taught the language of birds and given from everything.'","18":"The ant: 'Enter your dwellings, lest Sulayman and his soldiers crush you while they do not perceive.' She even excused them.","19":"Sulayman smiled and prayed: 'My Lord, enable me to be grateful for Your favour.'","22":"The hoopoe: 'I have encompassed what you have not.' A small bird brought news to a great king.","30":"Sulayman's letter: 'It is from Sulayman, and it is: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful.'","34":"The queen: 'Kings, when they enter a town, ruin it.' She chose diplomacy over war.","40":"The throne was brought in the blink of an eye; Sulayman said: 'This is from the favour of my Lord, to test me whether I am grateful.'","44":"The queen: 'I submit with Sulayman to Allah, Lord of the worlds.'","62":"'Who answers the distressed when he calls upon Him and removes the evil?'"},"l":[["Power with gratitude",19,"Success should lead to thanks, not pride."],["Wisdom in leadership",34,"The queen consulted and avoided bloodshed."],["Allah answers the distressed",62,"Turn to Him in your hardest moments."]],"r":["When I achieve something, is my first thought 'this is from my Lord's favour'?","Like the ant, do I protect others and assume the best of them?"],"k":[82,88]},"28":{"h":["Al-Qasas was revealed in Makkah, in the late period. Verse 85, 'He who ordained the Quran for you will bring you back to a place of return', is reported to have been revealed at al-Juhfah during the Hijrah, as the Prophet ﷺ left Makkah grieving. It was a promise that he would return, fulfilled eight years later.","The surah tells Musa's early life in more detail than anywhere else: Pharaoh's slaughter of Israelite boys, Musa's mother placing him in the river, his upbringing in the palace, the accidental killing, his flight to Madyan, his marriage, and his call at the bush.","It ends with Qarun, a man of Musa's people who became so rich that the keys of his treasures were heavy for a group of strong men. He said his wealth came from his own knowledge, and the earth swallowed him and his house. Verse 56, 'You cannot guide whom you love', was revealed about the death of the Prophet's ﷺ uncle Abu Talib (Bukhari)."],"s":[[1,13,"Pharaoh and the infant Musa","Pharaoh oppresses the weak; Allah inspires Musa's mother and returns him to her."],[14,28,"Musa's youth and flight","The accidental killing, flight to Madyan, helping two women at the well, and marriage."],[29,42,"The call and the mission","The fire on the mountain; the staff and hand; Pharaoh's tower and his drowning."],[43,75,"The Quran and the deniers","The Quraysh demand Musa's signs; you cannot guide whom you love; questions on the Day."],[76,88,"Qarun and the return","Qarun's wealth and arrogance; the home of the Hereafter is for the humble."]],"n":{"7":"'Put him in the river and do not fear or grieve; We will return him to you and make him a messenger.'","10":"The heart of Musa's mother became empty with worry; she nearly revealed him.","15":"Musa struck an Egyptian who was fighting an Israelite, and he died. Musa repented immediately.","24":"Exhausted in Madyan after helping the women, Musa prayed: 'My Lord, I am in need of whatever good You send down to me.'","25":"One of the women came walking with shyness. Modesty described in motion.","26":"'The best one to hire is the strong and trustworthy.' A principle for leadership and work.","56":"'You cannot guide whom you love, but Allah guides whom He wills.' Revealed about Abu Talib (Bukhari 4772).","77":"'Seek the home of the Hereafter, but do not forget your share of this world, and do good as Allah has done good to you.'","78":"Qarun: 'I was given it only because of knowledge I have.'","85":"A promise to the Prophet ﷺ, made as he left Makkah, that he would return."},"l":[["Allah protects the weak",7,"What seems most dangerous may be the path of rescue."],["A du'a of need",24,"Admit your need to Allah, and good will come."],["Strength with trustworthiness",26,"Both skill and character matter."],["Balance this world and the next",77,"Do not forget your share, but seek the Hereafter."]],"r":["Do I credit my success to 'my own knowledge', like Qarun?","What would it mean to pray Musa's du'a (28:24) sincerely today?"],"k":[4,5,83]},"29":{"h":["Al-'Ankabut was revealed in Makkah, in the late period, though some scholars consider its first eleven verses Madinan. It addresses the intense persecution the believers faced: families pressuring their children to leave Islam, and torture of the weak.","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas reported that his mother vowed not to eat or drink until he abandoned Islam. Verse 8 was revealed: be good to your parents, but do not obey them in associating partners with Allah (Muslim).","The surah's message is that faith will be tested, as it was for Nuh, Ibrahim, Lut, Shu'ayb and Musa. Those who rely on anything other than Allah are like the spider, whose house is the flimsiest of houses."],"s":[[1,13,"Faith will be tested","Do people think they will be left to say 'we believe' and not be tested?"],[14,40,"Prophets tested","Nuh's 950 years, Ibrahim in the fire, Lut, Shu'ayb, 'Ad, Thamud, Qarun, Pharaoh and Haman."],[41,55,"The spider's house","False protectors are like a spider's web. Recite the Book and establish prayer."],[56,69,"Allah's earth is vast","Migrate if you are oppressed; provision is with Allah; strive and He will guide you."]],"n":{"8":"Be good to your parents, but if they push you to associate partners with Allah, do not obey them. About Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (Muslim).","26":"Lut believed in Ibrahim, and Ibrahim said: 'I will emigrate to my Lord.'","41":"The weakest of houses is the spider's house.","45":"'Prayer restrains from indecency and wrongdoing, and the remembrance of Allah is greater.'","64":"This worldly life is only amusement and play; the home of the Hereafter is true life."},"l":[["Tests reveal truth",2,"Faith is proven in hardship."],["Rely on Allah alone",41,"Every other support is a spider's web."],["Prayer shapes conduct",45,"True prayer changes how you live."],["Strive and you will be guided",69,"The effort is yours; the guidance is Allah's."]],"r":["What 'spider's web' am I relying on instead of Allah?","Does my prayer actually keep me from wrongdoing? What does that tell me about my prayer?"],"k":[2,3,14,46,56,57,60,69]},"30":{"h":["Ar-Rum was revealed in Makkah around 615–616 CE. The Persian Empire had crushed the Byzantine (Roman) army and taken Jerusalem. The pagans of Makkah, who identified with the idol-worshipping Persians, rejoiced; the Muslims, who felt closer to the Christian Byzantines as People of the Book, were saddened.","The opening verses predicted that the Byzantines would be victorious 'within a few years' (bid' sinin: three to nine). Abu Bakr even made a wager with the Quraysh about it, before gambling was forbidden (Tirmidhi). Around 622–624 CE, Heraclius defeated the Persians decisively. Many reports place this near the same time as the Muslims' victory at Badr.","From this prophecy the surah turns to Allah's signs: the creation of humans from dust, spouses with love and mercy, the diversity of languages and colours, sleep, lightning and rain, and the natural disposition (fitrah) upon which Allah created people."],"s":[[1,10,"The prophecy","The Romans defeated, soon to be victorious. Travel and see the end of earlier nations."],[11,27,"His signs","Creation from dust, spouses with love and mercy, languages and colours, sleep, lightning."],[28,45,"The fitrah","Turn your face to the religion; corruption appears because of what people's hands earned."],[46,60,"Winds, rain and patience","Winds as good news; weakness, then strength, then weakness; be patient, Allah's promise is true."]],"n":{"2":"'The Romans have been defeated in the nearest land.'","3":"'They, after their defeat, will be victorious within a few years.'","4":"'On that day the believers will rejoice.' Many reports link this to Badr.","7":"They know the outer surface of worldly life but are heedless of the Hereafter.","21":"'He created for you spouses from yourselves to find tranquillity in them, and placed between you affection and mercy.'","30":"The fitrah: the natural disposition upon which Allah created people. No change should there be in the creation of Allah.","39":"Interest does not increase with Allah; zakah does.","54":"Allah created you from weakness, then gave strength, then after strength, weakness and grey hair.","60":"'Be patient; the promise of Allah is true.'"},"l":[["Allah's promises come true",3,"Trust His word, even when the present looks hopeless."],["Marriage is a sign",21,"Love and mercy between spouses are from Allah."],["Diversity is a sign",22,"Different languages and colours point to the Creator."],["Humans affect the earth",41,"Corruption of the environment follows human wrongdoing."]],"r":["How do I treat people of other languages and colours, knowing they are a sign of Allah?","Do I see love and mercy in my family as gifts from Allah?"],"k":[22,23,32,41]},"31":{"h":["Luqman was revealed in Makkah. Luqman was a wise man of ancient times; most scholars hold he was not a prophet but a righteous sage given wisdom. The Arabs knew of him, and his name stood for wisdom.","Reports say that al-Nadr ibn al-Harith, a Quraysh leader, bought entertaining stories and singing girls to distract people from the Quran. Verse 6 condemns those who 'buy idle talk to mislead from the way of Allah'.","At its centre is Luqman's advice to his son (verses 12–19): worship Allah alone, be grateful, honour your parents, remember that Allah sees even a mustard seed inside a rock, pray, enjoin good, be patient, do not turn your cheek in pride, walk modestly and lower your voice."],"s":[[1,11,"Guidance and distraction","A Book of guidance for the good-doers; those who buy idle talk to mislead."],[12,19,"Luqman's advice to his son","Tawhid, gratitude, parents, Allah's knowledge, prayer, patience and humility."],[20,34,"Signs and the unseen","Blessings outward and inward; if the trees were pens; the five keys of the unseen."]],"n":{"6":"Those who buy idle talk to mislead people from Allah's way.","12":"'We gave Luqman wisdom: Be grateful to Allah.'","13":"'O my son, do not associate anything with Allah; shirk is a great injustice.'","14":"His mother carried him in weakness upon weakness; weaning in two years. Be grateful to Me and to your parents.","18":"Do not turn your cheek away from people in pride, nor walk arrogantly.","19":"Be moderate in your walk and lower your voice; the harshest of sounds is the braying of donkeys.","20":"Allah has completed His favours upon you, outward and inward.","27":"If all the trees were pens and the sea ink, with seven seas more, Allah's words would not run out.","34":"With Allah alone is the knowledge of the Hour, the rain, the wombs, tomorrow's earnings and the place of death."},"l":[["Parenting with wisdom",13,"Luqman begins with tawhid and explains why."],["Kindness to parents despite disagreement",15,"Disobey only in shirk, yet remain kind."],["Humility in manner",18,"Pride shows in the face, the walk and the voice."],["Only Allah knows the unseen",34,"Accept what you cannot know."]],"r":["If I wrote advice to my child or a younger person, what would I say first?","How is my walk, my voice and my face toward people?"],"k":[15,16,17]},"32":{"h":["As-Sajdah was revealed in Makkah. Jabir reported that the Prophet ﷺ would not sleep until he had recited As-Sajdah and Al-Mulk (Tirmidhi), and he recited it in the Fajr prayer on Fridays (Bukhari).","It affirms that the Quran is from the Lord of the worlds, describes the stages of human creation, and pictures the regret of the guilty on the Day of Judgment, when they will hang their heads and ask to be returned.","It is named after its verse of prostration (15), describing believers who fall down in prostration when reminded of Allah's verses."],"s":[[1,11,"Creation and revelation","A Book without doubt; creation in six days; humans created from clay and given spirit."],[12,22,"Two kinds of people","The guilty hang their heads; the believers prostrate and leave their beds to pray."],[23,30,"Musa and the signs","Leaders who guide by Allah's command when they are patient; dead land revived."]],"n":{"9":"Then He proportioned him and breathed into him from His spirit, and gave you hearing, sight and hearts.","12":"The guilty will hang their heads: 'Our Lord, we have seen and heard, so return us.'","16":"Their sides leave their beds to call upon their Lord in fear and hope.","17":"No soul knows what joy is hidden for them. The Prophet ﷺ described it as what no eye has seen, nor ear heard (Bukhari)."},"l":[["Leave your bed for Allah",16,"The night prayer is the mark of the sincere."],["Hidden rewards",17,"Allah has prepared what no one can imagine."],["Small trials are warnings",21,"Hardships can be invitations to return."],["Patience and certainty make leaders",24,"Leadership in faith comes from these two."]],"r":["When did I last pray at night?","What 'nearer trial' might Allah be using to call me back?"],"k":[7,11,15,18,21,24]},"33":{"h":["Al-Ahzab was revealed in Madinah, mostly in the fifth year after the Hijrah. Its name, 'The Confederates', refers to the alliance of Quraysh, Ghatafan and other tribes, around 10,000 fighters, who besieged Madinah. On Salman al-Farisi's advice, the Muslims dug a trench (khandaq) around the open side of the city.","During the siege, the Jewish tribe of Banu Qurayzah inside Madinah broke their treaty, and the hypocrites spread fear. The believers were tested until 'hearts reached the throats' (verse 10). After about a month, a fierce cold wind and divisions among the enemy broke the siege.","The surah also addresses the Prophet's ﷺ household: the abolition of adoption as a legal relationship of blood (the case of Zayd ibn Harithah), the Prophet's ﷺ marriage to Zaynab bint Jahsh, guidance for the Prophet's ﷺ wives, the hijab, and the command to send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ."],"s":[[1,8,"Follow revelation; adoption","Rely on Allah; adopted sons are not your sons; the Prophet ﷺ is closer to believers than themselves."],[9,27,"The Battle of the Trench","The siege, the hypocrites' fear, the Prophet ﷺ as the excellent example, and Banu Qurayzah."],[28,35,"The Prophet's ﷺ wives","They chose Allah and His Messenger; the ten qualities of believing men and women."],[36,40,"Zayd and Zaynab","The decision of Allah and His Messenger; Muhammad ﷺ is the Seal of the Prophets."],[41,62,"Remembrance, blessings and modesty","Remember Allah often; send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ; manners in his home; the cloak."],[63,73,"The Hour and the Trust","Speak justly; the Trust offered to heavens, earth and mountains, which declined it."]],"n":{"4":"Allah has not placed two hearts in any man's chest.","6":"The Prophet ﷺ is closer to the believers than their own selves, and his wives are their mothers.","21":"'In the Messenger of Allah you have an excellent example.' Revealed in the context of the Trench, where he dug alongside them.","23":"Among the believers are men true to their covenant. Linked to Anas ibn al-Nadr, martyred at Uhud (Bukhari).","35":"Ten qualities for men and women equally, each promised forgiveness and great reward.","40":"Muhammad ﷺ is not the father of any of your men, but the Messenger of Allah and the Seal of the Prophets.","56":"Allah and His angels send blessings on the Prophet; O believers, send blessings on him.","59":"Believing women draw their outer garments over themselves, so they are recognised and not harmed.","72":"The Trust was offered to the heavens, earth and mountains; they declined, and man took it on."},"l":[["Follow the best example",21,"The Prophet ﷺ led by sharing the hardship."],["Remember Allah much",41,"Constant remembrance steadies the heart in crisis."],["Send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ",56,"Allah and the angels do so."],["Speak justly",70,"Upright speech leads to upright deeds."]],"r":["In my hardest moments, are my thoughts about Allah good or bad (33:10)?","How many times today did I send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ?"],"k":[5,10,33,36,41,70]},"34":{"h":["Saba' was revealed in Makkah. The Quraysh denied the Hour and said: 'The Hour will not come to us.' The surah answers with Allah's knowledge, from which not an atom's weight is hidden.","It contrasts two models. Dawud and Sulayman were given extraordinary power: mountains and birds glorified with Dawud, iron was softened for him, and jinn worked for Sulayman. They were grateful. The people of Saba' (Sheba) in Yemen had two gardens watered by a great dam; they turned away, and the dam burst (the flood of al-'Arim), scattering them.","Historically, the great dam of Ma'rib in Yemen did collapse, scattering its people across Arabia."],"s":[[1,9,"The Hour is coming","Nothing is hidden from Allah, not even an atom's weight."],[10,14,"Dawud and Sulayman","Gratitude in power; Sulayman's death unknown to the jinn until a termite ate his staff."],[15,21,"Saba' and the broken dam","Two gardens turned to bitter fruit; they became tales."],[22,54,"Arguments and warnings","Intercession only with permission; the Prophet ﷺ sent to all humanity; wealth does not bring nearness to Allah."]],"n":{"10":"'O mountains, glorify with him, and the birds.' Iron softened for Dawud.","14":"The jinn did not know of Sulayman's death until a creature ate his staff and he fell.","15":"Two gardens, right and left: 'Eat from your Lord's provision and be grateful.'","19":"They wronged themselves, so We made them tales and scattered them completely.","23":"Intercession only for whom He permits; the angels' awe at Allah's word (Bukhari 4800).","28":"'We have not sent you except to all humanity.'","37":"Neither wealth nor children bring you nearer to Us, but faith and good deeds.","46":"'Stand for Allah in pairs or alone, then reflect.'"},"l":[["Few are truly grateful",13,"Gratitude is shown in work, not only words."],["Blessings can be lost",16,"Turning away from Allah ends prosperity."],["Spending is never loss",39,"Allah replaces what you give."],["Reflect with sincerity",46,"Think honestly, alone or with one friend."]],"r":["Am I among the 'few who are grateful'?","What 'dam' of blessing in my life might I be taking for granted?"],"k":[3,13,16,39]},"35":{"h":["Fatir (also called Al-Mala'ikah, 'The Angels') was revealed in Makkah. It begins with praise of the Originator of the heavens and earth, who made angels as messengers with wings, two, three or four.","It invites reflection on the diversity of creation: mountains with streaks of white, red and black; people, animals and cattle of different colours. It concludes that 'only those of His servants who have knowledge truly fear Allah' (verse 28).","It describes three kinds of people who inherit the Book: those who wrong themselves, those in between, and those foremost in good deeds, all by Allah's permission entering Paradise (verse 32)."],"s":[[1,14,"The Originator","Allah's mercy cannot be withheld; Satan is an enemy; the two seas."],[15,26,"You are the poor","You are in need of Allah; He is free of need. No soul bears another's burden."],[27,38,"Knowledge and the Book","Colours of creation; the knowledgeable fear Allah; three kinds of inheritors."],[39,45,"Successors on earth","Arrogance and evil plotting rebound on their makers; Allah's patience."]],"n":{"1":"Angels with wings, two, three and four; He increases in creation what He wills.","15":"'O mankind, you are the poor in need of Allah, and Allah is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy.'","32":"Three kinds who inherit the Book: those who wrong themselves, the moderate, and those foremost in good.","37":"'Did We not give you life enough for one who reflects to reflect?'"},"l":[["Mercy cannot be stopped",2,"No one can block what Allah opens."],["We are the needy ones",15,"Humility before Allah is simply the truth."],["Knowledge brings awe",28,"Real knowledge leads to fear of Allah."],["Aim to be foremost",32,"Be among those who race to good."]],"r":["Which of the three groups in verse 32 am I in today?","What do I learn from creation that brings me closer to Allah?"],"k":[2,5,6,8,18,28,29,43,45]},"36":{"h":["Ya-Sin was revealed in Makkah, in the middle period. It is one of the most widely recited surahs. Some hadith call it 'the heart of the Quran', and many Muslims recite it for the dying and the dead, although scholars of hadith grade those specific narrations as weak. Its virtue lies in its powerful content.","It affirms the Prophet's ﷺ messengership, then tells of a town (traditionally Antioch) to which messengers were sent. The people rejected them, and a man came running from the far side of the city to defend them; he was killed, and was told 'Enter Paradise'. Even then he wished his people knew how Allah had forgiven him.","It closes with signs of resurrection, culminating in the man who brought a decayed bone to the Prophet ﷺ, crumbled it, and asked who could give it life. The answer: 'He who produced it the first time' (verse 79)."],"s":[[1,12,"You are a messenger","By the wise Quran, you are of the messengers, on a straight path."],[13,32,"The people of the town","Messengers rejected; the believing man who came running; martyrdom and Paradise."],[33,50,"Signs in the cosmos","Dead earth revived, night and day, the sun and moon in their orbits, ships at sea."],[51,68,"The Day of Resurrection","The trumpet; the people of Paradise; mouths sealed and hands speaking."],[69,83,"The proof of resurrection","Not a poet; cattle as blessings; the decayed bone; 'Be, and it is.'"]],"n":{"2":"An oath by the wise Quran.","12":"We record what they sent ahead and their traces left behind.","20":"A man came running from the farthest end of the city: 'O my people, follow the messengers.'","26":"'Enter Paradise.' He said: 'I wish my people knew how my Lord has forgiven me.'","40":"The sun cannot overtake the moon, nor the night the day; each floats in an orbit.","58":"'Peace': a word from a Merciful Lord.","65":"On that Day their mouths will be sealed; their hands will speak and their feet testify.","69":"We did not teach him poetry, nor is it fitting for him.","78":"He brings out a decayed bone: 'Who will give life to bones when they have rotted?'","82":"His command, when He wills a thing, is only to say 'Be', and it is."},"l":[["Stand up for the truth",20,"One man's courage is remembered forever."],["Care for people even when they harm you",26,"Even in Paradise he wished good for his people."],["Our traces remain",12,"What we leave behind is recorded."],["Resurrection is easy for the Creator",79,"Who made us once can make us again."]],"r":["What 'traces' (36:12) will I leave behind after I die?","Would I come 'running' to support the truth, like the man from the far side of the city?"],"k":[21,38,39,79]},"37":{"h":["As-Saffat was revealed in Makkah, in the middle period. Its opening oaths by the angels standing in rows answer the Quraysh's claim that the angels were Allah's daughters.","It describes the Hereafter vividly, including a believer in Paradise looking down and seeing his former companion in Hell, the one who used to mock his faith.","Its most moving passage is the story of Ibrahim and his son (traditionally understood to be Isma'il): the dream of sacrifice, the son's answer, 'O my father, do as you are commanded; you will find me, if Allah wills, among the patient', and the great ransom (verses 99–111). This is the origin of 'Eid al-Adha."],"s":[[1,10,"The angels in ranks","Your God is One; the protected heavens."],[11,74,"The Day of Judgment","The deniers' regret, the joys of Paradise, the tree of Zaqqum."],[75,148,"Peace upon the prophets","Nuh, Ibrahim and the sacrifice, Musa and Harun, Ilyas, Lut and Yunus, each ending with 'peace be upon'."],[149,182,"Glory to your Lord","The angels are servants, not daughters; peace upon the messengers."]],"n":{"1":"By those lined up in rows: the angels in their ranks.","24":"'Stop them; they are to be questioned.'","51":"A believer in Paradise recalls a companion who mocked his belief in resurrection.","96":"'Allah created you and what you make.'","99":"Ibrahim: 'I am going to my Lord; He will guide me.'","102":"'O my son, I see in a dream that I am sacrificing you. What do you think?' The son: 'Do as you are commanded.'","107":"'We ransomed him with a great sacrifice.' The root of 'Eid al-Adha.","130":"'Peace be upon Ilyas.'","143":"If he had not been among those who glorify Allah, he would have remained in the whale's belly until the Day of Resurrection."},"l":[["Submission is total trust",103,"Both father and son submitted to Allah."],["Ask Allah for righteous children",100,"Ibrahim's du'a before his test."],["Glorification rescues",143,"Yunus's tasbih saved him from the depths."],["Peace upon the messengers",181,"Honour every prophet."]],"r":["What am I being asked to give up for Allah, and can I answer like Isma'il?","How often do I glorify Allah in a normal day?"],"k":[100,103,139,180]},"38":{"h":["Sad was revealed in Makkah. Reports in Tirmidhi say that the Quraysh leaders went to Abu Talib to complain about his nephew. The Prophet ﷺ asked them for one word that would give them the Arabs and the non-Arabs: 'La ilaha illa Allah'. They replied: 'Has he made the gods into one God? This is a strange thing!' (verse 5).","It recounts prophets who were tested and turned back to Allah: Dawud, after the two disputants climbed into his prayer chamber; Sulayman, distracted by his fine horses; and Ayyub, patient in long illness. It ends with the story of Iblis refusing to bow to Adam.","Ibn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet ﷺ prostrated at verse 24 (Bukhari 1069)."],"s":[[1,16,"One God is 'strange'","The Quraysh leaders are astonished at tawhid."],[17,40,"Dawud and Sulayman","Dawud judges and repents; Sulayman's horses and kingdom."],[41,54,"Ayyub and the patient prophets","Ayyub strikes the ground and a healing spring flows; Ibrahim, Ishaq, Ya'qub."],[55,88,"Iblis and the final word","Iblis's refusal; the Quran is a reminder for the worlds."]],"n":{"17":"Be patient over what they say, and remember Our servant Dawud, the one who often turned back to Allah.","24":"Dawud realised he was being tested, sought forgiveness, and fell down bowing. A place of prostration.","35":"Sulayman: 'My Lord, forgive me and grant me a kingdom not fitting for anyone after me.'","41":"Ayyub called: 'Satan has touched me with hardship and torment.'","42":"'Strike the ground with your foot: here is cool water for washing and drinking.'","44":"'We found him patient. An excellent servant; he ever turned back to Allah.'","76":"Iblis: 'I am better than him.'"},"l":[["Turn back to Allah quickly",24,"Dawud's greatness was in returning."],["Judge with justice",26,"Power is a trust for justice."],["Reflect on the Quran",29,"It was sent for reflection, not only recitation."],["Patience is excellent",44,"Ayyub is praised for patience above all."]],"r":["When I realise my mistake, how quickly do I turn back to Allah?","Which verse have I truly reflected on this week?"],"k":[5,26,29,75,86]},"39":{"h":["Az-Zumar was revealed in Makkah, in the later period. Its central call is to sincere worship: 'Worship Allah, making religion sincerely for Him' (verse 2). The Quraysh claimed their idols only brought them nearer to Allah, and the surah firmly rejects this.","It contains what many scholars call the most hopeful verse in the Quran (53): 'O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy; Allah forgives all sins.' Reports link it to people who had committed great sins before Islam and feared they could never be forgiven.","It ends with a powerful scene: groups (zumar) of people driven to Hell and groups led to Paradise, whose gates open and whose keepers greet them with peace."],"s":[[1,9,"Sincere religion","Pure worship belongs to Allah; is one who stands in the night like one who does not?"],[10,31,"Two kinds of people","Patience rewarded without measure; the best speech; the Quran makes skins shiver."],[32,52,"Truth and denial","Who is more unjust than one who lies about Allah? Souls taken in sleep and death."],[53,63,"Do not despair","The most hopeful verse; turn back before it is too late."],[64,75,"The trumpet and the groups","The earth shines with its Lord's light; groups to Hell and groups to Paradise."]],"n":{"3":"'We only worship them to bring us nearer to Allah': the idolaters' excuse.","23":"The best speech: a Book whose verses resemble each other; skins shiver and then soften at Allah's remembrance.","67":"The whole earth in His grip on the Day of Resurrection (Bukhari 4811).","73":"The God-fearing are led to Paradise in groups: 'Peace be upon you; you have done well.'"},"l":[["Sincerity is everything",2,"Worship for Allah alone."],["Knowledge raises people",9,"Those who know are not like those who do not."],["Never despair",53,"No sin is too great for Allah's forgiveness."],["Follow the best",18,"Listen widely, follow wisely."]],"r":["Is there a sin I think is too big for Allah to forgive? Read 39:53 again.","When I hear the Quran, does my skin ever 'shiver'? Why or why not?"],"k":[9,10,18,22,30,42,53,54]},"40":{"h":["Ghafir (also called Al-Mu'min) was revealed in Makkah in the later period, when the Quraysh were openly plotting to kill the Prophet ﷺ. It is the first of seven surahs beginning with Ha-Mim.","Its heart is the story of a believing man from Pharaoh's own family who had hidden his faith. When Pharaoh resolved to kill Musa, this man spoke up: 'Will you kill a man because he says, my Lord is Allah?' (verse 28). 'Urwah reported that when 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt was strangling the Prophet ﷺ at the Ka'bah, Abu Bakr pushed him away, quoting these very words (Bukhari).","The surah opens with Allah's names, the Forgiver of sin and Accepter of repentance, and contains the promise 'Call upon Me; I will respond to you' (verse 60)."],"s":[[1,20,"The Forgiver and the angels' prayer","The angels carrying the Throne pray for the believers' forgiveness."],[21,54,"The believer of Pharaoh's family","A secret believer speaks out and warns his people; Pharaoh's tower."],[55,85,"Patience and du'a","Be patient; call upon Me; signs of creation; the end of the arrogant."]],"n":{"7":"The angels around the Throne pray: 'Our Lord, You encompass all things in mercy and knowledge, so forgive those who repent.'","8":"They pray for the believers' parents, spouses and children too.","16":"'To whom belongs the dominion today? To Allah, the One, the Prevailing.'","28":"'Will you kill a man because he says: My Lord is Allah?'","36":"Pharaoh orders Haman to build a tower to look for the God of Musa.","39":"'This worldly life is only enjoyment; the Hereafter is the home of permanence.'","55":"Be patient; Allah's promise is true; seek forgiveness and glorify Him evening and morning.","60":"'Call upon Me; I will respond to you.' The Prophet ﷺ said: du'a is worship (Tirmidhi).","78":"Some messengers We told you about, and some We did not."},"l":[["Speak up for the truth",28,"One brave voice can stand against tyranny."],["Entrust your affairs to Allah",44,"Then He protects you."],["Du'a is worship",60,"Allah invites us to ask Him."],["The angels pray for you",7,"Be among those who repent and follow His way."]],"r":["Have I stayed silent when I should have spoken, like the believer of Pharaoh's family?","What will I ask Allah for today, knowing He has promised to answer?"],"k":[3,44,45]},"41":{"h":["Fussilat (also called Ha-Mim al-Sajdah) was revealed in Makkah. The sirah of Ibn Ishaq reports that the Quraysh sent 'Utbah ibn Rabi'ah to offer the Prophet ﷺ wealth, leadership or medical help if he would stop. The Prophet ﷺ listened, then recited the opening of this surah. When he reached verse 13, 'Utbah put his hand over the Prophet's ﷺ mouth and begged him to stop.","'Utbah returned to the Quraysh changed, and told them: 'I have heard words unlike any I have ever heard; it is not poetry, nor magic, nor soothsaying.' He advised them to leave the Prophet ﷺ alone. They said he had been bewitched.","The surah also describes the Day when hearing, sight and skin will testify, and contains the famous command to repel evil with good, so that an enemy becomes a devoted friend."],"s":[[1,8,"A Book explained in detail","A Quran in Arabic for people who know; they say: our hearts are covered."],[9,18,"Creation and warning","The earth in two days, the heavens in two; 'Ad and Thamud."],[19,32,"Witnesses on the Day","Skin, hearing and sight will testify; angels descend on those who stand firm."],[33,44,"Repel evil with good","The best speech is calling to Allah; repel evil with what is better."],[45,54,"Signs in the horizons and within","We will show them Our signs in the horizons and in themselves."]],"n":{"11":"Allah said to the heavens and earth: 'Come willingly or unwillingly.' They said: 'We come willingly.'","13":"Warning of a thunderbolt like that of 'Ad and Thamud. Here 'Utbah begged the Prophet ﷺ to stop.","21":"They will ask their skins why they testified: 'Allah made us speak.'","22":"Revealed about men at the Ka'bah who doubted Allah hears secrets (Bukhari 4817).","30":"Those who say 'Our Lord is Allah' and then stand firm: angels descend upon them saying, do not fear.","37":"A place of prostration: do not prostrate to the sun or moon, but to Allah who created them.","53":"'We will show them Our signs in the horizons and within themselves until it becomes clear that it is the truth.'"},"l":[["Steadfastness brings angels' comfort",30,"Say 'my Lord is Allah' and stay firm."],["Good speech invites",33,"Call to Allah with your words and deeds."],["Answer harm with goodness",34,"It can turn an enemy into a friend."],["Look for signs",53,"In the universe and in yourself."]],"r":["Who has wronged me whom I could 'repel with what is better'?","What would my skin say about me on the Day of Judgment?"],"k":[5,20,33,34,35]},"42":{"h":["Ash-Shura was revealed in Makkah, in the later period. It stresses that the religion given to Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa, 'Isa and Muhammad ﷺ is one, and warns against dividing into sects.","It is named after verse 38, which describes believers whose 'affairs are decided by consultation among themselves' (shura). It was revealed before an Islamic state existed, showing consultation as a core quality of faith, alongside prayer and charity.","The surah also addresses justice and forgiveness: the right to answer wrong with its like, the greater merit of forgiving, and patience as a mark of resolve."],"s":[[1,12,"The One who reveals","Allah reveals to you as He revealed to those before; nothing is like Him."],[13,26,"One religion","The same religion ordained for Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa and 'Isa; do not divide."],[27,43,"Qualities of believers","Rely on their Lord, avoid great sins, forgive when angry, consult and spend."],[44,53,"How Allah speaks to humans","By inspiration, from behind a veil, or through a messenger; the Quran as a light."]],"n":{"13":"He ordained for you the religion He enjoined upon Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa and 'Isa: establish the religion and do not divide.","19":"Allah is Subtle (Latif) with His servants; He provides for whom He wills.","23":"'I ask no reward except love for kinship' (Bukhari 4818).","27":"If Allah extended provision fully, people would transgress; He sends it in measure.","40":"The recompense of evil is an evil like it, but whoever pardons and makes reconciliation, his reward is with Allah.","52":"We revealed to you a spirit (the Quran) by Our command, a light by which We guide."},"l":[["Do not divide religion",13,"Unity is a command."],["Consultation is faith",38,"Decide together."],["Forgive when angry",37,"Control in anger is a mark of faith."],["Forgiveness is better",40,"Justice is allowed; forgiveness is rewarded by Allah."]],"r":["Do I consult my family or colleagues before decisions?","When did I last forgive someone in the moment of anger?"],"k":[11,30,37,38,43,49]},"43":{"h":["Az-Zukhruf was revealed in Makkah. The Quraysh asked why the Quran was not revealed to a great man of one of the two cities, Makkah or Ta'if, such as al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah or 'Urwah ibn Mas'ud. The surah answers that Allah distributes His mercy, not the people (verse 32).","It shows how little worldly luxury means to Allah: if it were not that all people would become one community of disbelief, He would give the disbelievers roofs, stairs and doors of silver, and ornaments of gold (verses 33–35).","It also confronts blind imitation: 'We found our fathers on a religion, and we are following in their footsteps' (verse 22), recalls Ibrahim, Musa and Pharaoh, and describes 'Isa as a servant whom Allah favoured."],"s":[[1,25,"Following forefathers","A clear Book in Arabic; they claimed angels were daughters; 'we found our fathers upon this'."],[26,45,"Ibrahim and worldly glitter","Ibrahim disowns idols; Allah distributes mercy; luxury is nothing to Allah."],[46,65,"Pharaoh and 'Isa","Pharaoh boasts of his rivers; 'Isa is only a servant favoured by Allah."],[66,89,"The Hour and friendship","Friends will be enemies on that Day, except the God-fearing."]],"n":{"13":"The du'a for riding: 'Glory to Him who subjected this to us; we could not have done it ourselves.'","26":"Ibrahim: 'I am free of what you worship, except He who created me.'","33":"If not for people becoming one community of disbelief, We would give the disbelievers roofs of silver.","36":"Whoever turns away from the remembrance of the Most Merciful, We appoint for him a devil as companion.","51":"Pharaoh: 'Is not the kingdom of Egypt mine, and these rivers flowing beneath me?'","54":"He made fools of his people, and they obeyed him.","59":"'Isa was only a servant upon whom We bestowed favour.","71":"In Paradise: whatever souls desire and eyes delight in."},"l":[["Think for yourself",22,"Tradition is not proof."],["Luxury is not honour",33,"Wealth is not a sign of Allah's love."],["Choose God-fearing friends",67,"Only friendship for Allah lasts."],["Remember Allah when travelling",13,"Every journey begins with gratitude."]],"r":["What do I believe or do only because 'our fathers' did?","Which of my friendships will last into the Hereafter?"],"k":[22,32,67]},"44":{"h":["Ad-Dukhan was revealed in Makkah. When the Quraysh persisted in rejection, the Prophet ﷺ prayed for years of famine like those of Yusuf. Hunger became so severe they ate bones and carcasses, and a man would look at the sky and see something like smoke from exhaustion. Abu Sufyan came asking the Prophet ﷺ to pray for rain, and he did; then they returned to their ways (Bukhari, from Ibn Mas'ud).","Ibn Mas'ud explained that the 'smoke' (dukhan) of verse 10 refers to this, while other Companions understood it as a sign before the Hour. Both views are recorded.","The surah opens by saying the Quran was revealed on a blessed night, which most scholars identify as Laylat al-Qadr, and ends by contrasting the tree of Zaqqum with the gardens of the righteous."],"s":[[1,16,"The blessed night and the smoke","Revelation on a blessed night; the warning of the smoke."],[17,33,"Pharaoh","Pharaoh's people are drowned; gardens and springs left behind; the earth and sky did not weep for them."],[34,59,"The Day of Sorting","The tree of Zaqqum and the gardens of the God-fearing."]],"n":{"3":"'We sent it down on a blessed night.' Identified with Laylat al-Qadr.","4":"In it every wise matter is decreed.","10":"Watch for the day the sky brings a visible smoke (Bukhari 4821).","43":"The tree of Zaqqum, food of the sinner.","51":"The God-fearing will be in a secure place, among gardens and springs.","58":"We have made it easy in your tongue, that they may remember."},"l":[["Hardship can be a reminder",12,"Do not return to heedlessness when relief comes."],["Worldly things are left behind",25,"Only deeds go with you."],["Creation has purpose",38,"Nothing was made in play."]],"r":["When Allah relieved a hardship for me, did I change, or return to my old ways?","Would the earth and sky weep for me (44:29)? What would make them?"],"k":[15,25,29,38]},"45":{"h":["Al-Jathiyah was revealed in Makkah. It is named after verse 28: on the Day of Judgment every nation will be seen kneeling (jathiyah), each called to its record.","It lists signs in the heavens, the earth, the creation of creatures, the alternation of night and day, rain and the winds, for 'people who reflect'. It warns against those who take their own desires as their god (verse 23).","It also records the materialist claim, 'Nothing destroys us but time' (verse 24). The Prophet ﷺ related in a hadith qudsi: 'The son of Adam curses time, and I am time; in My hand is the night and day' (Bukhari 4826)."],"s":[[1,15,"Signs for those who reflect","In the heavens, earth, creatures, night and day, rain and wind."],[16,26,"Desire as a god","The Children of Israel divided; the one who takes his desire as his god."],[27,37,"Every nation kneeling","Each nation called to its book; this is Our record which speaks the truth."]],"n":{"24":"'Nothing destroys us but time': the denial of resurrection."},"l":[["The universe serves a purpose",13,"Everything is subjected to us as a trust."],["Do not worship desire",23,"Desire can become an idol."],["Every deed is recorded",29,"Live as if your book is being written, because it is."]],"r":["Where does my desire overrule what I know is right?","If my record were read today, what would I want to change?"],"k":[3,13,14,18,21,23,28,29]},"46":{"h":["Al-Ahqaf was revealed in Makkah, around the tenth year of the mission, after the Prophet ﷺ returned from Ta'if, where he had been stoned and rejected. On the way back, at Nakhlah, a group of jinn heard him reciting the Quran in prayer and believed (verses 29–32).","It recalls the people of 'Ad, who lived among the sand dunes (al-ahqaf) of southern Arabia. When they saw a cloud approaching, they rejoiced, thinking it would bring rain; it was a wind of punishment. 'A'ishah reported that whenever the Prophet ﷺ saw clouds or wind, the worry showed on his face, because of this story (Bukhari 3206).","It contains a moving passage on parents: the hardships of the mother in pregnancy and birth, and the prayer of one who reaches forty years (verse 15)."],"s":[[1,14,"Truth and its deniers","Show me what your false gods have created. Say: I am not new among the messengers."],[15,20,"Parents and the age of forty","Kindness to parents; the du'a at forty; the ungrateful child."],[21,28,"'Ad and the cloud","Hud warns 'Ad among the dunes; a cloud of punishment."],[29,35,"The jinn and patience","The jinn listen and believe; be patient as the messengers of firm resolve were patient."]],"n":{"9":"'I am not something new among the messengers, and I do not know what will be done with me or you.'","13":"Those who say 'Our Lord is Allah' and remain steadfast: no fear will be upon them.","15":"His mother carried him with hardship and delivered him with hardship; carrying and weaning thirty months. The du'a at forty: 'My Lord, enable me to be grateful.'","17":"The one who says to his parents 'uff to you!' and mocks resurrection.","20":"'You exhausted your good things in your worldly life.'","24":"They saw a cloud approaching their valleys and said: 'This will bring us rain.' No, it is what you sought to hasten.","29":"We directed to you a group of jinn listening to the Quran (Bukhari 4921).","30":"The jinn: 'We have heard a Book revealed after Musa, confirming what came before.'"},"l":[["Honour your mother",15,"Her hardship deserves lifelong gratitude."],["Renew yourself at forty",15,"A milestone for gratitude and repentance."],["Not every cloud is rain",24,"Do not be deceived by appearances."],["Patience like the great messengers",35,"Hold firm through rejection."]],"r":["When did I last thank my mother for what she endured for me?","Whatever my age, can I make the 'forty' du'a today?"],"k":[35]},"47":{"h":["Muhammad (also called Al-Qital, 'Fighting') was revealed in Madinah, in the early years, before the conquest of Makkah. It is one of four places where the Prophet ﷺ is named in the Quran.","The Muslims now faced armed hostility from the Quraysh. The surah contrasts those who believe and do good, whose deeds Allah perfects, with those who disbelieve, whose deeds are lost. It gives rulings on prisoners of war and calls the believers to steadfastness.","It exposes hypocrites who, when a surah about fighting was revealed, looked at the Prophet ﷺ like someone fainting at death. It asks pointedly: 'Do they not reflect on the Quran, or are there locks upon their hearts?' (verse 24)."],"s":[[1,11,"Two outcomes","Allah improves the condition of believers; prisoners of war; help Allah and He will help you."],[12,19,"Rivers of Paradise","Rivers of water, milk, wine and honey; know that there is no god but Allah, and seek forgiveness."],[20,32,"The hypocrites","Locks on hearts; cutting ties of kinship; Allah will expose their malice."],[33,38,"Do not weaken","Do not call for peace from weakness; Allah does not need your wealth, but tests your generosity."]],"n":{"19":"'Know that there is no god but Allah, and ask forgiveness for your sin.' Imam al-Bukhari titled a chapter: knowledge comes before speech and action.","22":"Would you, if you turned away, cause corruption and cut your ties of kinship? (Bukhari 4830)","30":"You will recognise the hypocrites by the tone of their speech."},"l":[["Support Allah's cause",7,"He supports those who support His way."],["Knowledge before action",19,"Know Allah, then act and seek forgiveness."],["Unlock your heart",24,"Reflection on the Quran opens what is locked."],["We are replaceable",38,"Serve while you have the chance."]],"r":["Is there a 'lock' on my heart when I read the Quran? What might it be?","Who are the relatives I have drifted from? Could I reconnect this week?"],"k":[2,7,15,24,31,38]},"48":{"h":["Al-Fath was revealed in Madinah, in the sixth year after the Hijrah, on the road back from al-Hudaybiyah. The Prophet ﷺ had set out with about 1,400 Companions to perform 'Umrah, but the Quraysh blocked them at Hudaybiyah, near Makkah.","When a rumour spread that 'Uthman, their envoy, had been killed, the Companions pledged under a tree to stand by the Prophet ﷺ to the end: the Pledge of Ridwan (verse 18). A treaty followed whose terms seemed humiliating: no 'Umrah that year, and Muslims fleeing Makkah to be returned. 'Umar was deeply troubled.","Then this surah came: 'Indeed, We have given you a clear victory.' The Prophet ﷺ said a surah had been revealed that was dearer to him than everything the sun rises over (Bukhari 4833). Within two years, so many people entered Islam that Makkah was opened almost without fighting."],"s":[[1,10,"A clear victory","The treaty is a victory; tranquillity sent down into believers' hearts."],[11,17,"Those who stayed behind","The Bedouins' excuses; the sick and blind are excused."],[18,28,"The pledge under the tree","Allah is pleased with those who pledged; the dream of entering the Sacred Mosque will come true."],[29,29,"The portrait of the Companions","Firm against disbelief, merciful among themselves, like a seed that grows strong."]],"n":{"1":"'Indeed, We have given you a clear victory.'","4":"He sent down tranquillity (sakinah) into the believers' hearts to increase them in faith.","10":"Those who pledge to you pledge to Allah; the hand of Allah is over their hands.","24":"He restrained their hands from you and yours from them in the valley of Makkah.","26":"The disbelievers had zealotry of ignorance in their hearts; Allah sent tranquillity on the believers.","27":"The dream is true: 'You will enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allah wills, in safety.'","29":"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; those with him are firm against disbelievers, merciful among themselves; their sign is on their faces from prostration."},"l":[["Victory may look like loss",1,"Trust Allah's wisdom over appearances."],["Tranquillity is from Allah",4,"Ask Him for sakinah in hard decisions."],["Mercy among believers",29,"Gentleness with each other is the mark of the Companions."]],"r":["What 'defeat' in my life might later prove to be a victory?","Am I gentle with fellow believers, as the Companions were?"],"k":[2,18]},"49":{"h":["Al-Hujurat was revealed in Madinah, in the ninth year after the Hijrah, the 'Year of Delegations', when tribes came from across Arabia to meet the Prophet ﷺ. Some Bedouins of Banu Tamim stood outside his private apartments (hujurat) calling loudly, 'O Muhammad, come out to us!' (verse 4).","Abu Bakr and 'Umar raised their voices disputing in his presence over who should lead a tribe, and verse 2 was revealed. Thabit ibn Qays, who had a naturally loud voice, feared his deeds were void and stayed home weeping, until the Prophet ﷺ told him he was among the people of Paradise (Bukhari 4846).","Though only 18 verses, it is a complete code for a healthy community: verify news, reconcile those who quarrel, do not mock, insult, assume the worst, spy or backbite. It ends with the principle that the most honoured in Allah's sight is the most God-conscious."],"s":[[1,5,"Respect for the Prophet ﷺ","Do not put yourselves before Allah and His Messenger; do not raise your voices."],[6,10,"Verify and reconcile","Verify news from the untrustworthy; make peace between believers."],[11,13,"Protecting brotherhood","No mockery, insults, suspicion, spying or backbiting. Created into nations to know one another."],[14,18,"True faith","Faith has not yet entered your hearts; the true believers."]],"n":{"6":"If a corrupt person brings you news, verify it, lest you harm a people in ignorance.","11":"Let not a people mock another people; perhaps they are better than them.","12":"Avoid much suspicion; do not spy; do not backbite. Would one of you like to eat the flesh of his dead brother?","13":"'We created you from a male and female and made you nations and tribes that you may know one another. The most honoured of you with Allah is the most righteous.'","14":"The Bedouins say 'We believe'. Say: you have not yet believed; say 'we have submitted'."},"l":[["Verify before you share",6,"Rumours can harm innocent people."],["Make peace",10,"Reconciliation is a duty."],["Guard the tongue and mind",12,"Suspicion, spying and backbiting destroy brotherhood."],["Honour is in taqwa",13,"No race or tribe is superior."]],"r":["Did I pass on any news this week without checking it?","Is there anyone I have backbitten that I need to make amends with?"],"k":[1,2,9,10,15]},"50":{"h":["Qaf was revealed in Makkah, in the early-middle period. Umm Hisham bint Harithah said she learned Surah Qaf from the Prophet's ﷺ own mouth, because he recited it from the pulpit every Friday (Muslim 873). He also recited it in the Eid prayers.","The Quraysh found the idea of resurrection absurd: 'When we have died and become dust, that is a far return' (verse 3). The surah answers by pointing to the sky, the earth, the rain and the date palms: the One who did this can easily bring you back.","It describes the moment of death, the two recording angels, the companion who testifies, and Allah's nearness to every person, closer than the jugular vein."],"s":[[1,15,"A far return?","Dead land revived; were We tired by the first creation?"],[16,29,"Nearer than the jugular vein","Two angels record every word; the stupor of death; the companion's testimony."],[30,45,"Hell asks for more","Paradise brought near; glorify before sunrise and sunset; remind with the Quran."]],"n":{"6":"Have they not looked at the sky above them, how We built it without any rifts?","15":"Were We tired by the first creation?","18":"He utters no word except that a watcher is ready beside him.","19":"The stupor of death comes in truth: that is what you were trying to avoid.","22":"'Your sight today is sharp.' The veil is lifted.","30":"Hell is asked: 'Are you filled?' It says: 'Are there any more?'"},"l":[["Allah is near",16,"He knows even the whispers of your soul."],["Every word is recorded",18,"Speak as if it is being written, because it is."],["Death will come",19,"Prepare for what you are avoiding."],["A present heart",37,"The Quran benefits whoever listens attentively."]],"r":["If every word I said today was written down, what would I erase?","How does knowing Allah is closer than my jugular vein change how I feel when alone?"],"k":[3,9,16,37,45]},"51":{"h":["Adh-Dhariyat was revealed in Makkah, in the early period. It opens with oaths by the scattering winds, the clouds heavy with rain, the ships gliding easily and the angels distributing affairs, all leading to one truth: what you are promised is true, and the Judgment will happen.","It describes the God-fearing, who slept little at night and sought forgiveness before dawn, and who recognised a right in their wealth for the beggar and the deprived. It then tells of Ibrahim's honoured guests, the angels, whom he hurried to serve with a roasted calf before knowing who they were.","It contains one of the clearest statements of human purpose: 'I did not create jinn and humans except to worship Me' (verse 56)."],"s":[[1,23,"What you are promised is true","Oaths by the winds; the God-fearing; signs in the earth and in yourselves."],[24,46,"Ibrahim's guests and the nations","Generous hospitality; destruction of Lut's people, Pharaoh, 'Ad, Thamud and Nuh's people."],[47,60,"The purpose of creation","The expanding heaven; flee to Allah; created only to worship Him."]],"n":{"19":"In their wealth was a right for the one who asks and the deprived.","21":"And in yourselves: do you not see?","49":"Of everything We created pairs."},"l":[["Night worship and morning forgiveness",18,"The habits of the God-fearing."],["Generosity is a right, not a favour",19,"The poor have a right in your wealth."],["Honour your guests",26,"Ibrahim served before asking."],["Know your purpose",56,"Everything else serves this."]],"r":["Do I see giving as the poor's right, or as my favour to them?","If my purpose is worship, how much of my day is aligned with it?"],"k":[17,18,22,24,26,47,50,55,56,58]},"52":{"h":["At-Tur was revealed in Makkah. It opens with oaths by Mount Sinai (al-Tur), a written Book, the frequented House, the raised canopy of the sky and the swelling sea.","Jubayr ibn Mut'im came to Madinah as a non-Muslim to negotiate the release of captives after Badr. He heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting At-Tur in the Maghrib prayer, and when he reached verses 35–37, 'Were they created from nothing, or are they the creators?', Jubayr said his heart almost flew. It was the first time faith settled in his heart (Bukhari 4854).","The surah describes the joy of the righteous in Paradise, including the reunion of believers with their faithful children, and ends with a command to the Prophet ﷺ to be patient, 'for you are in Our eyes' (verse 48)."],"s":[[1,16,"The punishment will come","Oaths by Sinai and the Book; the day the sky sways."],[17,28,"The joy of the righteous","Gardens, reunion with faithful children, conversation and gratitude."],[29,49,"Questions to the deniers","A poet? Mad? Created from nothing? Be patient; you are in Our eyes."]],"n":{"1":"By the Mount, Sinai, where Musa received revelation.","4":"Al-Bayt al-Ma'mur, the frequented House in the seventh heaven.","21":"Believers whose descendants followed them in faith will be joined with them, without any loss to their deeds.","26":"'We used to be fearful among our families.'","28":"'We used to call upon Him before. He is the Righteous, the Merciful.'"},"l":[["Families reunited in faith",21,"Raise your children in faith; you may be together forever."],["Fear among family",26,"Gentle concern for the Hereafter at home."],["The question of creation",35,"Every heart can ask it honestly."],["You are in Allah's sight",48,"Patience is easier knowing He watches over you."]],"r":["Reflect on verse 35 as if hearing it for the first time, like Jubayr.","What can I do now so my family is reunited in Paradise?"],"k":[35,36,48,49]},"53":{"h":["An-Najm was revealed in Makkah, in about the fifth year of the mission. It was the first surah containing a prostration that the Prophet ﷺ recited publicly. Ibn Mas'ud reported that when he recited it at the Ka'bah and prostrated at the end, everyone present prostrated, Muslims and idolaters, except one old man who lifted dust to his forehead (Bukhari 1067).","It describes the Prophet's ﷺ two visions of Jibril in his true form: once on the horizon, and once at the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary (Sidrat al-Muntaha) during the Mi'raj, the ascension through the heavens on the night of the Night Journey.","It then confronts the three goddesses of the Arabs, al-Lat, al-'Uzza and Manat, calling them 'mere names you have named', and lays down principles of personal responsibility: each soul has only what it strives for."],"s":[[1,18,"The vision","He does not speak from desire; he saw Jibril at the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary."],[19,32,"Mere names","Al-Lat, al-'Uzza and Manat; assumption is no substitute for truth; avoid the great sins."],[33,62,"Each soul and its effort","No soul bears another's burden; man has only what he strives for; prostrate to Allah."]],"n":{"23":"They are only names you have named, you and your fathers.","32":"Those who avoid the major sins, except small slips: your Lord is vast in forgiveness. Do not claim yourselves pure."},"l":[["Trust the Prophet's ﷺ words",3,"His teaching is revelation, not desire."],["Do not claim purity",32,"Humility about your own righteousness."],["You reap what you strive for",39,"Effort is yours to give."],["Laughter and tears are from Allah",43,"Both are part of His plan."]],"r":["Do I think of myself as better than others in religion (53:32)?","What am I striving for, given that it is all I will have?"],"k":[1,3,4,9,14,18,19,28,38,39,43,62]},"54":{"h":["Al-Qamar was revealed in Makkah, after the splitting of the moon. Anas reported that the people of Makkah asked the Prophet ﷺ for a sign, and he showed them the moon split into two parts, with Mount Hira between them (Bukhari 3636, 3868). The Quraysh dismissed it as 'passing magic'.","The surah then retells the fates of the people of Nuh, 'Ad, Thamud, Lut and Pharaoh. After each comes the refrain: 'We have made the Quran easy to remember; is there anyone who will be mindful?' (verses 17, 22, 32, 40).","The Prophet ﷺ would recite Qaf and Al-Qamar in the 'Eid prayers (Muslim)."],"s":[[1,8,"The moon split","The Hour has come near; they see a sign and turn away."],[9,42,"Five nations, one refrain","Nuh, 'Ad, Thamud, Lut and Pharaoh's people, each followed by: the Quran is made easy."],[43,55,"Everything by measure","Are your disbelievers better? Everything is created by decree; the righteous near a powerful King."]],"n":{"2":"If they see a sign, they turn away and say: 'Passing magic.'","14":"The ark sailed under Our eyes.","17":"'We have made the Quran easy to remember. Is there anyone who will be mindful?'","49":"Indeed, We created everything by a measure (qadar)."},"l":[["The Quran is made easy",17,"Anyone can begin to learn and remember it."],["Signs do not force faith",2,"A heart must be willing to see."],["Everything is measured",49,"Nothing in your life is random."]],"r":["The surah asks four times: 'Is there anyone who will be mindful?' How do I answer?","Which part of the Quran could I start memorising this week?"],"k":[1,11,19,27,50,55]},"55":{"h":["Ar-Rahman is classified as Makkan by most scholars, with some considering it Madinan. It is addressed to both humans and jinn, using the dual form throughout.","Its refrain, 'Which of the favours of your Lord will you deny?', appears 31 times. Jabir reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited it to his Companions, who stayed silent, and said: 'I recited it to the jinn on the night of the jinn, and they responded better than you. Every time I came to that verse, they said: none of Your favours, our Lord, do we deny, and to You is all praise' (Tirmidhi 3291).","It moves from the teaching of the Quran and the creation of humans, through the balance of the cosmos, the two seas and the passing away of all things, to the two pairs of gardens prepared for the righteous."],"s":[[1,25,"Favours of creation","The Quran taught, speech taught, sun and moon in their courses, the balance, the two seas, ships."],[26,45,"Everything perishes","All on earth will pass, but the face of your Lord remains; the Day of Judgment."],[46,78,"The gardens","Two gardens for whoever fears standing before his Lord, and two more beneath them."]],"n":{"1":"The Most Merciful: the surah begins with His name, not with an oath.","2":"He taught the Quran before mentioning the creation of humans.","4":"He taught him eloquent speech (al-bayan).","7":"He raised the heaven and set up the balance (al-mizan).","13":"'Which of the favours of your Lord will you deny?' The first of 31 times.","46":"For whoever fears standing before his Lord are two gardens.","60":"'Is the reward for excellence anything but excellence?'"},"l":[["Mercy before all",1,"The surah of favours begins with al-Rahman."],["Keep the balance",9,"Justice reflects the cosmic order."],["Only Allah remains",27,"Attach your heart to what lasts."],["Excellence is rewarded with excellence",60,"Do your best; Allah gives His best."]],"r":["Every time you hear 'Which favours will you deny?', name one favour silently.","Where in my life am I falling short in the 'balance' of justice?"],"k":[9,19,26,27,29,78]},"56":{"h":["Al-Waqi'ah was revealed in Makkah, in the early period. It is one of the surahs the Prophet ﷺ said had turned his hair grey (Tirmidhi 3297), because of its vivid description of the Last Day.","It divides humanity into three groups: the foremost (al-sabiqun), who are brought nearest to Allah; the companions of the right; and the companions of the left. Each group's fate is described in detail.","It then offers proofs of resurrection from everyday life: human creation from a drop, seeds you sow but Allah grows, water you drink but Allah sends down, and fire you kindle from wood Allah created. It closes with the moment of death, when the soul reaches the throat."],"s":[[1,12,"The Inevitable Event","When it occurs, none can deny it; people sorted into three kinds."],[13,56,"Three groups","The foremost, the companions of the right, the companions of the left."],[57,74,"Proofs from daily life","Sperm, seeds, water and fire: who creates them?"],[75,96,"The noble Quran and the moment of death","A noble Quran in a protected Book, touched only by the purified; the soul at the throat."]],"n":{"1":"When the Inevitable Event occurs.","10":"'The foremost, the foremost': those who were first to good deeds.","13":"Many from the earlier generations and a few from the later ones.","58":"Have you considered what you emit? Is it you who create it?","63":"Have you considered what you sow? Is it you who make it grow?","71":"Have you considered the fire you kindle?","77":"It is a noble Quran."},"l":[["Race to be foremost",10,"Be first in good, not last."],["Ordinary things are miracles",63,"Seeds, water and fire point to the Creator."],["Honour the Quran",77,"Treat it with purity and respect."],["Remember the final moment",83,"Live for the moment the soul reaches the throat."]],"r":["In which good deed could I be 'foremost' this week?","Look at your next glass of water as verse 68 asks. What do you feel?"],"k":[7,11,68,75,79,83]},"57":{"h":["Al-Hadid was revealed in Madinah. It begins the group of 'musabbihat', surahs opening with Allah's glorification, and its first six verses are among the most profound descriptions of Allah: the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden.","It calls on believers to spend in Allah's way and notes that those who spent and fought before the conquest are greater in rank than those who did so later (verse 10).","Verse 16 asks: 'Has the time not come for the hearts of the believers to be humbled at the remembrance of Allah?' Ibn Mas'ud said only four years passed between their Islam and this gentle rebuke (Muslim). Many later Muslims, including the scholar al-Fudayl ibn 'Iyad, reportedly changed their lives on hearing it. The surah takes its name from verse 25: 'We sent down iron, in which is great strength and benefits for people.'"],"s":[[1,6,"The First and the Last","Everything glorifies Allah; He is the First, the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden."],[7,19,"Spend and believe","Spend from what He made you trustees of; a goodly loan; light for the believers on the Day."],[20,29,"This world and the balance","Life is play and adornment; messengers sent with the Book, the balance and iron."]],"n":{"3":"He is the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden.","16":"'Has the time not come for the hearts of the believers to be humbled at the remembrance of Allah?'","23":"So that you do not despair over what you missed, nor exult over what you received.","27":"Monasticism was invented by them; We did not prescribe it."},"l":[["Allah is with you",4,"Wherever you are, He knows."],["You are a trustee of wealth",7,"It is His, held in your hands."],["Soften your heart",16,"It is time."],["Balance in loss and gain",23,"Neither despair nor arrogance."]],"r":["Read verse 16 as if it were addressed to you today. What would change?","How do I react to loss and to success? Compare with verse 23."],"k":[4,7,10,11,12,20,21,22,25]},"58":{"h":["Al-Mujadilah was revealed in Madinah. Its opening concerns Khawlah bint Tha'labah, whose husband Aws ibn al-Samit had declared zihar, saying 'You are to me like my mother's back', a pre-Islamic practice that left a wife neither divorced nor married.","Khawlah came to the Prophet ﷺ and argued her case, pleading to Allah about her situation. 'A'ishah said: 'Glory to Him whose hearing encompasses all voices. The woman who disputed came to the Prophet ﷺ and spoke to him while I was in a corner of the house and could not hear what she said, yet Allah revealed: Allah has heard the speech of the one who argues with you' (Bukhari, Nasa'i).","The surah abolishes zihar and sets an expiation for it. It then teaches manners for gatherings, private conversations and making room for others, and ends by describing the 'party of Allah'."],"s":[[1,6,"Allah heard her","Khawlah's plea; zihar abolished and its expiation set."],[7,13,"Secret talk and gatherings","Allah is the fourth of any three; make room in gatherings; Allah raises those with knowledge."],[14,22,"The party of Allah","Those who befriend the enemies of Allah; the party of Allah succeeds."]],"n":{"1":"'Allah has heard the speech of the one who argues with you about her husband and complains to Allah.'","2":"Their wives are not their mothers; their mothers are only those who gave birth to them.","3":"The expiation for zihar: freeing a slave, two months of fasting, or feeding sixty poor people.","11":"Make room in gatherings and Allah will make room for you. Allah raises those who believe and those given knowledge by degrees.","22":"You will not find a people who believe in Allah and the Last Day loving those who oppose Allah, even if they were their fathers or sons."},"l":[["Allah hears every plea",1,"No complaint to Him is too small."],["Justice for wives",2,"Harmful customs were abolished to protect women."],["Make room for others",11,"Generosity in a gathering is rewarded."],["Knowledge raises",11,"Seek knowledge and Allah raises you."]],"r":["What would I say if I pleaded to Allah as honestly as Khawlah did?","Do I make room for others, literally and figuratively?"],"k":[7,9]},"59":{"h":["Al-Hashr was revealed in Madinah in the fourth year after the Hijrah. Ibn 'Abbas called it 'the surah of Banu al-Nadir' (Bukhari). This Jewish tribe of Madinah had a treaty with the Muslims, but plotted to drop a stone on the Prophet ﷺ while he sat by a wall in their quarter.","After a siege of their fortresses, they agreed to leave, taking what their camels could carry; many went to Khaybar. The surah describes this exile (al-hashr) and gives rulings on the property left behind (fay'), distributed mainly to the poor emigrants.","It contains a famous portrait of the Ansar, who loved those who migrated to them and preferred them over themselves even in their own need (verse 9), and ends with a sequence of Allah's beautiful names (verses 22–24)."],"s":[[1,5,"The exile of Banu al-Nadir","They thought their fortresses would protect them."],[6,10,"Distributing the fay'","For the poor emigrants, the Ansar who preferred others, and those who came after them."],[11,17,"The hypocrites' false promises","They promised to support the Nadir and did not; like Satan who says 'disbelieve' and then disowns."],[18,24,"Prepare for tomorrow; the beautiful names","Let every soul look to what it has sent ahead; if this Quran came down on a mountain; Allah's names."]],"n":{"2":"They thought their fortresses would protect them, but Allah came from where they did not expect.","7":"'Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it; whatever he forbids you, refrain.' So wealth does not circulate only among the rich.","9":"The Ansar preferred others over themselves, even though they were in need (Bukhari 4889).","10":"'Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith, and put no rancour in our hearts.'","21":"If We had sent this Quran down on a mountain, you would have seen it humbled and splitting from fear of Allah.","22":"He is Allah; there is no god but Him, Knower of the unseen and the seen.","23":"The King, the Holy, Peace, the Granter of Security, the Guardian, the Mighty.","24":"The Creator, the Inventor, the Fashioner; to Him belong the most beautiful names."},"l":[["Prefer others",9,"Generosity even in your own need."],["Pray for earlier believers",10,"Remove rancour from your heart."],["Prepare for tomorrow",18,"Every day, ask what you are sending ahead."],["Forgetting Allah is forgetting yourself",19,"Your true self is found with Him."]],"r":["When did I last put someone else's need before my own?","What have I 'sent ahead' today for my tomorrow?"],"k":[8,18,19]},"60":{"h":["Al-Mumtahanah was revealed in Madinah, around the Treaty of Hudaybiyah (6 AH) and the conquest of Makkah (8 AH). Its opening relates to Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah, a veteran of Badr who secretly wrote to the Quraysh warning them of the Prophet's ﷺ plans, hoping to protect his family in Makkah.","The letter was intercepted by 'Ali, al-Zubayr and al-Miqdad. 'Umar wanted to execute Hatib, but the Prophet ﷺ accepted his explanation and forgave him, saying Allah had looked upon the people of Badr and forgiven them (Bukhari).","The surah also sets principles for relations with non-Muslims: kindness and justice toward those who do not fight you or expel you (verse 8). Its name, 'The Woman Tested', comes from verse 10, about believing women who migrated to Madinah and were to be examined about their faith."],"s":[[1,6,"Loyalty and Ibrahim's example","Do not take Allah's enemies as allies; an excellent example in Ibrahim."],[7,9,"Kindness to the peaceful","Allah does not forbid kindness and justice to those who do not fight you."],[10,13,"The believing women","Examining the women who migrated; their pledge to the Prophet ﷺ."]],"n":{"1":"Hatib's letter to the Quraysh, intercepted on the road (Bukhari).","4":"There is an excellent example for you in Ibrahim and those with him.","8":"'Allah does not forbid you from being kind and just to those who have not fought you in religion or expelled you from your homes.'","10":"When believing women come as emigrants, examine them; Allah knows best their faith.","12":"The women's pledge: no shirk, no theft, no adultery, no killing children, no slander."},"l":[["Justice and kindness to all peaceful people",8,"Islam commands good relations with non-Muslim neighbours."],["Hearts can change",7,"Today's enemy may be tomorrow's friend."],["Mercy for a past mistake",1,"The Prophet ﷺ weighed Hatib's whole record."]],"r":["Am I kind and just to my non-Muslim neighbours and colleagues?","How do I judge someone's mistake: by one act or by their whole life?"],"k":[7,9]},"61":{"h":["As-Saff was revealed in Madinah. 'Abd Allah ibn Salam reported that some Companions were sitting together and said, 'If we knew which deed is most beloved to Allah, we would do it.' Then this surah was revealed, and the Prophet ﷺ recited it to them from beginning to end (Tirmidhi).","Its opening rebukes saying what one does not do: 'It is most hateful to Allah that you say what you do not do' (verse 3). It praises those who stand in ranks (saff) like a solid structure.","It recalls Musa, whose people hurt him, and 'Isa, who gave glad tidings of a messenger after him named Ahmad (verse 6). It ends with a call to be 'helpers of Allah', as the disciples of 'Isa were."],"s":[[1,4,"Say only what you do","Allah loves those who stand in ranks like a solid structure."],[5,9,"Musa, 'Isa and the light of Allah","The good news of Ahmad; they want to extinguish Allah's light with their mouths."],[10,14,"The best trade","Faith and striving: a trade that saves from painful punishment; be helpers of Allah."]],"n":{"3":"It is most hateful to Allah that you say what you do not do.","5":"Musa: 'Why do you harm me while you know I am Allah's messenger to you?'","6":"'Isa: 'Giving good tidings of a messenger to come after me, whose name is Ahmad.'","14":"Be helpers of Allah, as 'Isa said to the disciples: 'Who are my helpers for Allah?'"},"l":[["Match words with deeds",3,"Saying without doing is hateful to Allah."],["Unity like a building",4,"Each person supports the others."],["The best trade",10,"Faith and effort earn the greatest profit."],["Be a helper of Allah",14,"Answer the call like the disciples."]],"r":["What have I said I would do, but have not done?","How am I a 'brick' in my community's structure?"],"k":[2,4,8,10]},"62":{"h":["Al-Jumu'ah was revealed in Madinah. Its final verses relate to an incident when the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the Friday sermon and a caravan arrived with goods. Many people left to see it, leaving only twelve men with him (Bukhari, Muslim).","The surah reminds the believers of the blessing of a Messenger sent among the unlettered to recite, purify and teach, and that this blessing extends to 'others who have not yet joined them'. When asked who they were, the Prophet ﷺ placed his hand on Salman al-Farisi and said: 'If faith were at the Pleiades, men from these would reach it' (Bukhari 4897).","The Prophet ﷺ recited Al-Jumu'ah and Al-Munafiqun in the Friday prayer (Muslim)."],"s":[[1,4,"The Messenger who purifies","Recites, purifies, teaches the Book and wisdom, to the unlettered and those who will come."],[5,8,"Knowledge without action","Like a donkey carrying books; death you flee from will meet you."],[9,11,"The Friday prayer","Leave trade at the call; afterward spread out and seek Allah's bounty."]],"n":{"2":"He sent among the unlettered a Messenger to recite, purify, and teach them the Book and wisdom.","3":"'And others of them who have not yet joined them' (Bukhari 4897).","5":"Those given the Torah who did not carry it out are like a donkey carrying volumes of books.","9":"When the call is made for prayer on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade.","11":"When they saw trade or amusement, they rushed to it and left you standing. What is with Allah is better."},"l":[["Knowledge must be lived",5,"Carrying knowledge without acting on it is like a donkey with books."],["Friday is sacred time",9,"Put worship before business."],["Work is also worship",10,"After prayer, seek Allah's provision."]],"r":["Is there knowledge I 'carry' without living by it?","How do I spend Friday? Does it feel different from other days?"],"k":[8,10]},"63":{"h":["Al-Munafiqun was revealed in Madinah, during or after the expedition against Banu al-Mustaliq (around 5–6 AH). During a quarrel over water between an emigrant and a helper, 'Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, leader of the hypocrites, said: 'When we return to Madinah, the more honourable will expel the more humble' (verse 8).","Zayd ibn Arqam, then a boy, heard this and reported it. Ibn Ubayy swore he had not said it, and some doubted Zayd. Zayd was deeply distressed until this surah was revealed confirming him, and the Prophet ﷺ told him: 'Allah has confirmed you' (Bukhari 4900).","Ibn Ubayy's own son, a sincere believer also named 'Abd Allah, stood at the gate of Madinah and refused to let his father enter until he admitted that the Prophet ﷺ was the honourable one."],"s":[[1,4,"Their appearance","They testify falsely; their bodies impress you, but they are like propped-up timber."],[5,8,"Their arrogance","They turn away from forgiveness; 'the more honourable will expel the humble'."],[9,11,"A warning to believers","Do not let wealth and children distract you; give before death comes."]],"n":{"1":"They say 'We testify you are the Messenger'; Allah testifies they are liars.","4":"When you see them, their bodies please you; they are like propped-up pieces of wood.","7":"'Do not spend on those with the Messenger until they disperse.' But to Allah belong the treasures of the heavens and earth.","8":"'The more honourable will expel the more humble.' Honour belongs to Allah, His Messenger and the believers.","10":"Spend before death comes, when one says: 'My Lord, if only You delayed me a little, I would give charity.'"},"l":[["Appearance is not faith",4,"Do not be deceived by eloquence."],["True honour belongs to Allah",8,"And to those who are faithful to Him."],["Give now",10,"Do not wait for a later that may not come."]],"r":["What is distracting me from the remembrance of Allah (63:9)?","What charity have I been putting off?"],"k":[9,11]},"64":{"h":["At-Taghabun was revealed in Madinah, though some scholars consider its early part Makkan. Its name means 'mutual loss and gain', referring to the Day of Judgment, when the true winners and losers become clear.","Ibn 'Abbas reported that verse 14 concerns men from Makkah who wanted to migrate to Madinah but were held back by their wives and children. When they finally came and saw that others had gained knowledge of religion before them, they were tempted to punish their families. Allah told them to pardon and forgive (Tirmidhi).","It ends with one of the most practical principles of the Quran: 'Fear Allah as much as you are able' (verse 16)."],"s":[[1,10,"The Day of mutual loss","Everything glorifies Allah; He knows what is in the hearts; the Day of Gathering."],[11,18,"Families and wealth as tests","No calamity without Allah's permission; some spouses and children may be a trial; fear Allah as much as you can."]],"n":{"9":"That is the Day of mutual loss and gain.","11":"No calamity strikes except by Allah's permission; whoever believes, He guides his heart.","14":"Among your spouses and children are enemies to you, so beware; but if you pardon and forgive, Allah is Forgiving."},"l":[["Calamity guides the believing heart",11,"Faith turns hardship into guidance."],["Forgive your family",14,"Even when they hold you back."],["Do what you can",16,"Allah asks for your best, not the impossible."]],"r":["Is my family helping me toward Allah, or holding me back? How can I respond with forgiveness?","Where am I failing to do 'as much as I am able'?"],"k":[2,4,15,16,17]},"65":{"h":["At-Talaq was revealed in Madinah. It was prompted in part by 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar divorcing his wife during her menstruation. The Prophet ﷺ was angry and told him to take her back and, if he wished to divorce, to do so at the proper time (Bukhari, Muslim).","The surah sets the procedure for divorce: at the start of a waiting period ('iddah), not expelling the wife from her home during it, taking her back or separating with kindness, and providing for her, especially if she is pregnant or nursing.","Woven through these laws are promises of hope: 'Whoever fears Allah, He will make for him a way out and provide for him from where he does not expect' (verses 2–3). Divorce is treated as a matter of God-consciousness, not only law."],"s":[[1,7,"The law of divorce","Divorce at the proper time; do not expel them; a way out for the God-fearing; maintenance."],[8,12,"Lessons and light","Towns that rebelled; a Messenger reciting clear verses; seven heavens and earths."]],"n":{"1":"Divorce them at their waiting period; do not turn them out of their houses. You do not know; perhaps Allah will bring about a new situation.","3":"'Whoever fears Allah, He will make a way out for him, and provide from where he does not expect. Whoever relies on Allah, He is sufficient for him.'","7":"Allah does not burden a soul beyond what He has given it. After hardship, Allah will bring ease."},"l":[["A way out for the God-conscious",2,"Taqwa opens doors you cannot see."],["Kindness even in separation",2,"End relationships with dignity."],["Ease follows hardship",7,"Allah promises relief."]],"r":["What situation am I in where I need 'a way out'? What does taqwa look like in it?","If a relationship ends, can I part 'with kindness'?"],"k":[2,4,6,12]},"66":{"h":["At-Tahrim was revealed in Madinah. Its opening concerns the Prophet ﷺ declaring honey forbidden to himself after some of his wives, out of jealousy, suggested it gave him an unpleasant smell (Bukhari 4912). Another report links it to a secret he shared with one wife, which she disclosed.","Allah gently corrected the Prophet ﷺ for forbidding himself something lawful, and admonished the wives involved. This shows the honesty of the Quran: it records even the private struggles of the Prophet's ﷺ household.","The surah then turns to every believer: protect yourselves and your families from the Fire, repent sincerely (tawbah nasuh), and learn from women of opposite examples: the wives of Nuh and Lut, who betrayed their husbands' mission, and the wife of Pharaoh and Maryam, models of faith."],"s":[[1,5,"In the Prophet's ﷺ household","Why forbid what Allah made lawful? A secret disclosed."],[6,9,"Protect your families","A Fire whose fuel is people and stones; sincere repentance; the light of the believers."],[10,12,"Four women","The wives of Nuh and Lut; the wife of Pharaoh and Maryam."]],"n":{"1":"'O Prophet, why do you forbid what Allah has made lawful for you, seeking your wives' approval?'","8":"'Turn to Allah in sincere repentance (tawbah nasuh).'","10":"The wives of Nuh and Lut: being married to a prophet did not save them.","11":"The wife of Pharaoh: 'My Lord, build for me near You a house in Paradise, and save me from Pharaoh and his deeds.'"},"l":[["Do not forbid what is lawful",1,"Religion is not about making life harder than Allah made it."],["Responsibility for family",6,"Guide your family with care."],["Sincere repentance",8,"Repent fully, resolve not to return."],["Faith is personal",10,"No relationship can replace your own faith."]],"r":["Am I helping protect my family's faith, or neglecting it?","The wife of Pharaoh lived with a tyrant and kept her faith. What does that teach me about my circumstances?"],"k":[3,6,12]},"67":{"h":["Al-Mulk was revealed in Makkah. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'There is a surah of the Quran of thirty verses which interceded for a man until he was forgiven: Tabarak alladhi bi-yadihi al-mulk' (Tirmidhi 2891, Abu Dawud 1400, graded hasan). Another report calls it al-Mani'ah, the Protector, from the punishment of the grave.","Jabir reported that the Prophet ﷺ would not sleep until he had recited As-Sajdah and Al-Mulk (Tirmidhi), and many Muslims recite it every night.","It opens Juz 29 with a sweeping vision: Allah created death and life to test who is best in deeds; look at the sky again and again and you will find no flaw; the birds above, spreading and folding their wings, are held up by none but the Most Merciful."],"s":[[1,5,"The test of life","Death and life created to test who is best in deeds; a flawless sky."],[6,15,"Hell and the hidden fear","The people of the Fire admit: if we had listened or reasoned. Walk the earth and eat His provision."],[16,30,"Who else?","Who holds up the birds? Who provides if He withholds? If your water sank away, who would bring it back?"]],"n":{"3":"Look again: do you see any flaw?","4":"Look again and again; your sight will return to you humbled and tired.","19":"Do they not see the birds above them, spreading and folding their wings? None holds them up but the Most Merciful."},"l":[["Best deeds, not most",2,"Quality matters more than quantity."],["Reflection saves",10,"The people of the Fire regret not listening or thinking."],["Allah knows His creation",14,"Nothing about you is hidden."],["Small wonders",19,"A bird in flight is a sign of mercy."]],"r":["Am I trying to do the best deeds, or just many deeds?","Recite Al-Mulk tonight before sleeping, and pause at verse 19."],"k":[1,2,10,12,14,15,30]},"68":{"h":["Al-Qalam is one of the earliest surahs, revealed in Makkah soon after Al-'Alaq. It begins with the letter Nun and an oath by the pen, a striking start for a message brought to an unlettered people.","The Quraysh were calling the Prophet ﷺ mad. Allah answers: 'You are not, by the favour of your Lord, a madman... and indeed, you are of a great moral character' (verses 2–4). When 'A'ishah was later asked about the Prophet's ﷺ character, she said: 'His character was the Quran' (Muslim).","It tells the parable of the owners of a garden who planned to harvest early in the morning so the poor would get nothing, and found the garden destroyed overnight. It ends by urging the Prophet ﷺ to be patient and not be like Yunus, who left his people in anger."],"s":[[1,16,"A tremendous character","Not mad, but of great character; do not obey every contemptible swearer."],[17,33,"The owners of the garden","They swore to harvest at dawn without the poor; the garden was destroyed."],[34,52,"Patience like Yunus, not haste","Be patient for your Lord's decision; do not be like the companion of the fish."]],"n":{"17":"We tested them as We tested the owners of the garden.","28":"The most moderate of them said: 'Did I not tell you to glorify Allah?'","51":"The disbelievers almost strike you down with their eyes when they hear the Reminder."},"l":[["Character is the message",4,"The Prophet ﷺ is the living Quran."],["Share with the poor",24,"Stinginess destroys blessings."],["Listen to the moderate voice",28,"Wisdom often comes from the quiet one."],["Patience over anger",48,"Do not abandon your duty in frustration."]],"r":["What do people see in my character that reflects the Quran?","Have I ever 'harvested early' to avoid giving to those in need?"],"k":[1,2,4,10,24,42,48]},"69":{"h":["Al-Haqqah was revealed in Makkah, in the early period. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab related that before his Islam, he went out to confront the Prophet ﷺ and found him praying at the Ka'bah, reciting Al-Haqqah. 'Umar thought: 'He is a poet.' The Prophet ﷺ recited: 'It is not the word of a poet.' 'Umar thought: 'A soothsayer.' He recited: 'Nor the word of a soothsayer.' 'Umar said Islam then entered his heart (Ahmad).","Its name means 'the Inevitable Reality', the Day of Judgment. It describes the fates of Thamud, 'Ad, Pharaoh and the people of Nuh, then the trumpet, the splitting of the sky, and the records given in the right or left hand.","It ends with a powerful affirmation that the Quran is the word of a noble Messenger, not invented."],"s":[[1,12,"The Reality and the nations","Thamud, 'Ad, Pharaoh, the overturned cities and the flood."],[13,37,"The two records","Read my record! I wish I had not been given mine."],[38,52,"Not a poet","The word of a noble Messenger; if he had invented anything, We would have seized him."]],"n":{"19":"'Here, read my record! I was certain I would meet my account.'","28":"'My wealth has not availed me; my authority has gone from me.'","41":"It is not the word of a poet; little do you believe.","42":"Nor the word of a soothsayer; little do you remember.","44":"If he had invented any sayings in Our name, We would have seized him."},"l":[["Certainty of the meeting",20,"The successful lived knowing they would be questioned."],["Wealth and power disappear",28,"Only deeds remain."],["Feeding the poor matters",34,"Neglecting it is listed among the causes of ruin."]],"r":["If I were handed my record today, in which hand would I expect it?","How do I respond to verse 34? Am I encouraging others to feed the poor?"],"k":[1,7,24,25,34]},"70":{"h":["Al-Ma'arij was revealed in Makkah. Some commentators report that al-Nadr ibn al-Harith mockingly asked Allah to send down the punishment if the message were true. The surah opens: 'A questioner asked about a punishment bound to happen.'","It describes a Day whose length is fifty thousand years, when the sky will be like molten metal and mountains like wool, and no friend will ask about a friend.","It then describes human nature, anxious, impatient when harm touches it and stingy when good comes, except for those who pray and are constant in prayer, who recognise a right for the poor in their wealth, fear their Lord, guard their chastity, keep their trusts and stand by their testimony."],"s":[[1,18,"The Day of the ascending stairways","A day of fifty thousand years; no friend asks about a friend."],[19,35,"Human nature and the prayerful","Anxious by nature, except the people of prayer."],[36,44,"The deniers hurry","Do they hope to enter Paradise? They will come out of graves rushing."]],"n":{"22":"Except those who pray."},"l":[["Beautiful patience",5,"Patience without complaint."],["Prayer heals anxiety",22,"The cure for human restlessness."],["Consistency matters",23,"Constant prayer, not occasional."],["A known right for the poor",24,"Plan your giving."]],"r":["When good comes to me, am I generous or do I hold back (70:21)?","Is my prayer constant, or does it come and go with my mood?"],"k":[1,4,5,10,11,19,20,21,23,24,32]},"71":{"h":["Nuh was revealed in Makkah. It is devoted entirely to Nuh's call to his people, told largely in his own words as a report to his Lord. He called them by night and by day, in public and in private, for 950 years (29:14), and they put their fingers in their ears and covered themselves with their garments.","He promised them that seeking forgiveness would bring rain, wealth, children, gardens and rivers. He pointed to their own creation in stages and to the sun and moon. They clung to their idols: Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq and Nasr.","Ibn 'Abbas explained that these were the names of righteous men from Nuh's people. After they died, Satan inspired people to make statues of them as reminders; later generations forgot why and worshipped them. These idols later appeared among Arab tribes (Bukhari 4920)."],"s":[[1,20,"Nuh's call","Day and night, public and private: seek forgiveness and Allah will send rain."],[21,28,"The idols and Nuh's prayer","Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq and Nasr; drowned; Nuh's du'a for himself and the believers."]],"n":{"8":"I called them publicly.","10":"'Seek forgiveness of your Lord; He is ever Forgiving.'","13":"What is the matter with you that you do not hope for Allah's dignity?","23":"Wadd, Suwa', Yaghuth, Ya'uq and Nasr: righteous men who became idols (Bukhari 4920).","28":"'My Lord, forgive me, my parents, whoever enters my house a believer, and the believing men and women.'"},"l":[["Never stop calling",5,"Patience in da'wah, by every means."],["Istighfar brings blessings",10,"Forgiveness opens rain, wealth and children."],["Beware of how idols begin",23,"Excessive honour can become worship."],["Pray for all believers",28,"Make your du'a wide."]],"r":["How often do I seek forgiveness, and do I expect its blessings?","Is there anything I honour so much that it competes with Allah?"],"k":[5,7,9,11,12,14]},"72":{"h":["Al-Jinn was revealed in Makkah. Ibn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet ﷺ set out with some Companions for the market of 'Ukaz. Meanwhile the jinn found the heavens guarded and meteors cast at them, and went searching for the cause. They found the Prophet ﷺ leading the Fajr prayer at Nakhlah and listened to the Quran. They said: 'We have heard a wondrous Quran' (Bukhari 4921).","Most of the surah is the jinn's own speech: their wonder at the Quran, their admission that some of them had misled humans, and their belief that Allah has no wife or son.","The surah teaches that the mosques belong to Allah alone, that the Prophet ﷺ has no power to harm or benefit anyone, and that Allah alone knows the unseen, revealing it only to messengers He chooses."],"s":[[1,15,"The jinn's speech","We heard a wondrous Quran; the heavens are guarded; among us are Muslims and the unjust."],[16,28,"The mosques are for Allah","Call on no one with Allah; I have no power to harm or benefit you; the Knower of the unseen."]],"n":{"1":"A group of jinn listened and said: 'We have heard a wondrous Quran.'","14":"Among us are Muslims and among us are the unjust.","18":"'The mosques are for Allah, so do not call upon anyone along with Allah.'","21":"Say: 'I do not possess for you harm or right direction.'"},"l":[["The Quran moves every listener",1,"Even the jinn were amazed."],["Seek refuge in Allah alone",6,"Not in jinn, charms or fortune-tellers."],["Worship is for Allah alone",18,"No intermediary in the mosque."],["The unseen belongs to Allah",26,"Do not trust those who claim to know the future."]],"r":["When did the Quran last truly amaze me?","Do I ever seek protection from anything other than Allah, such as charms or superstitions?"],"k":[2,3,6,8,26,27]},"73":{"h":["Al-Muzzammil is among the earliest revelations, from the first months of the mission in Makkah. 'Al-Muzzammil' means 'the one wrapped in garments'. After the first revelation, the Prophet ﷺ returned to Khadijah trembling and asked to be wrapped up.","It commands him to stand in prayer for much of the night, reciting the Quran slowly and beautifully (tartil), to prepare for 'a weighty word'. 'A'ishah reported that the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions prayed at night for a year until their feet swelled, until the last verse came about a year later, lightening the obligation (Muslim 746).","The final verse (20) is long and is considered by many to be Madinan, as it mentions fighting in Allah's way and zakah. It makes night prayer voluntary and shows Allah's knowledge of the believers' circumstances: the sick, travellers and those fighting."],"s":[[1,9,"Stand in the night","Recite the Quran with tartil; a weighty word; night prayer is firmer."],[10,19,"Patience with the deniers","Be patient with what they say; a warning like Pharaoh's."],[20,20,"The lightening","Recite what is easy; Allah knows some of you are sick, travelling or fighting."]],"n":{"4":"Recite the Quran with measured recitation (tartil).","6":"The rising by night is firmer in impression and more suitable for words.","10":"Be patient with what they say, and leave them gracefully."},"l":[["The night prepares you",6,"Strength for the day is built in the night."],["Recite slowly",4,"Tartil allows the heart to follow the tongue."],["Leave gracefully",10,"Ignore insults with dignity."],["Do what is easy",20,"Consistency in what you can manage."]],"r":["Could I wake ten minutes before Fajr to pray?","Do I recite the Quran slowly enough to understand it?"],"k":[1,2,5,8,20]},"74":{"h":["Al-Muddaththir contains some of the very first verses revealed. Jabir reported the Prophet ﷺ describing how, after a pause in revelation, he heard a voice, looked up and saw the angel who had come to him in Hira, seated on a throne between heaven and earth. He hurried home in fear and said, 'Wrap me up!' Then Allah revealed: 'O you covered in your cloak, arise and warn' (Bukhari 4922).","These first five verses launched the public mission: arise and warn, magnify your Lord, purify your garments, avoid idols.","The later part describes al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, a powerful Quraysh leader who heard the Quran, was moved, then deliberately decided to call it 'magic handed down' to protect his status. The surah mentions nineteen angels guarding Saqar."],"s":[[1,10,"Arise and warn","The command to begin the mission; a hard Day for the disbelievers."],[11,31,"The one who thought and plotted","Al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah reflects, frowns, then calls the Quran magic; nineteen guardians."],[32,56,"Every soul is held in pledge","What led you into Saqar? We were not of those who prayed."]],"n":{"3":"And your Lord, magnify.","6":"Do not give to gain more in return.","24":"He said: 'This is nothing but magic imitated.'"},"l":[["Begin with purity",4,"Inner and outer cleanliness prepare for service."],["Give without expecting return",6,"Pure generosity."],["Truth rejected for status",24,"Pride can make people deny what they know."],["Prayer and feeding the poor",43,"Neglecting them leads to ruin."]],"r":["What does 'arise' mean for me today?","Have I ever rejected something true because accepting it would cost me status?"],"k":[1,2,4,5,18,31,38,43,44]},"75":{"h":["Al-Qiyamah was revealed in Makkah. It begins with two oaths: by the Day of Resurrection and by the self-reproaching soul (al-nafs al-lawwamah), the conscience that blames itself for wrongdoing.","Ibn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when receiving revelation, would move his tongue quickly to memorise it, fearing he would forget. Allah revealed verses 16–19: do not hasten with it; it is on Us to collect it and recite it (Bukhari 5).","It answers those who doubt Allah can reassemble bones: He is able to restore even the fingertips. It describes the moment of death, when the soul reaches the collarbone, and the believers' faces radiant, looking at their Lord."],"s":[[1,15,"The self-reproaching soul","Does man think We will not assemble his bones? Man will be a witness against himself."],[16,19,"How revelation was received","Do not hasten your tongue; it is on Us to collect and explain it."],[20,40,"Faces radiant and faces gloomy","Some faces looking at their Lord; the soul reaches the collarbone."]],"n":{"16":"Do not move your tongue with it to hasten it (Bukhari 5)."},"l":[["Listen to your conscience",2,"The soul that blames itself is alive."],["You know yourself best",14,"Excuses do not hide the truth from you."],["Allah preserves the Quran",17,"Trust Him with what you learn."],["Hope to see Allah",23,"The greatest reward of Paradise."]],"r":["What has my conscience been telling me lately?","What excuses do I make that I know are not true?"],"k":[2,4,14,15,17,19,20,22,23,26,36]},"76":{"h":["Al-Insan (also called Ad-Dahr) is Makkan according to many scholars and Madinan according to others. The Prophet ﷺ recited it with As-Sajdah in the Fajr prayer on Fridays (Bukhari 891).","It opens by reminding humans that there was a time when they were nothing worth mentioning, then created from a mixed drop and given hearing and sight, and shown the way, whether grateful or ungrateful.","Its centre is a portrait of the righteous, who 'give food, despite their love for it, to the poor, the orphan and the captive, saying: we feed you only for the face of Allah; we want neither reward nor thanks from you' (verses 8–9). Some commentators link this to 'Ali and Fatimah, though the report is weak. A rich description of Paradise follows."],"s":[[1,4,"From nothing to the choice","Created from a mixed drop, shown the way: grateful or ungrateful."],[5,22,"The righteous and their reward","They fulfil vows, fear the Day, feed the poor for Allah's face; Paradise described."],[23,31,"Patience and remembrance","Be patient; remember your Lord morning and evening; prostrate at night."]],"n":{"8":"They give food, despite loving it, to the poor, the orphan and the captive.","9":"'We feed you only for the face of Allah; we want no reward or thanks.'"},"l":[["Remember where you came from",1,"Humility about our origin."],["Free will is real",3,"You choose gratitude or ingratitude."],["Give for Allah alone",9,"Expect no thanks from people."]],"r":["When I help someone, do I secretly want thanks?","What vow or promise have I made that I need to fulfil?"],"k":[1,2,3,7,12,20,24,26,30]},"77":{"h":["Al-Mursalat was revealed in Makkah. Ibn Mas'ud reported that it was revealed while the Prophet ﷺ and some Companions were in a cave at Mina. As they were learning it fresh from his mouth, a snake came out; they rushed to kill it, but it escaped. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'It was saved from your harm as you were saved from its harm' (Bukhari 4930).","It is one of the surahs the Prophet ﷺ said had turned his hair grey (Tirmidhi). Ten times it repeats: 'Woe that Day to the deniers!'","It opens with oaths by winds sent forth in succession, and uses the signs of creation (a humble drop, the earth that holds the living and the dead, firm mountains, sweet water) to argue that the Day of Sorting is certain."],"s":[[1,15,"The winds and the Day","Oaths by the winds; the stars extinguished; the messengers' appointed time."],[16,40,"Signs of creation","Did We not create you from a humble fluid? Did We not make the earth a container?"],[41,50,"Shade and springs","The God-fearing among shades and springs; 'In what statement after this will they believe?'"]],"n":{"15":"Woe that Day to the deniers. The first of ten times.","25":"Did We not make the earth a container for the living and the dead?","38":"This is the Day of Sorting.","48":"When told 'Bow down', they do not bow."},"l":[["The repeated warning",15,"Repetition is mercy, giving chance after chance."],["The earth holds us all",25,"Living and dead, all are held by Allah's design."],["Bow when called",48,"Respond to the call to prayer."]],"r":["Each 'woe' is a chance to reflect: which of my denials, small or large, could it address?","When I hear the call to prayer, how quickly do I respond?"],"k":[1,20,27,35,41,50]},"78":{"h":["An-Naba' was revealed in Makkah, in the early period, and opens the 30th part of the Quran (Juz 'Amma), which many children memorise first. Commentators report that when the Prophet ﷺ began preaching resurrection, the Quraysh debated it endlessly among themselves, some doubting and some denying.","The surah answers their questions with a list of everyday signs: the earth spread like a bed, mountains as pegs, people created in pairs, sleep for rest, night as a covering, day for livelihood, a blazing lamp in the sky, and rain bringing grain and gardens.","It then describes the Day of Sorting, the punishment of those who did not expect a reckoning, and the reward of the God-fearing, ending with the disbeliever's cry: 'I wish I were dust!'"],"s":[[1,5,"The Great News","What are they asking about? The Great News over which they disagree."],[6,16,"Signs of design","Earth, mountains, pairs, sleep, night, day, the sun and rain."],[17,30,"The Day of Sorting","The trumpet, the opened sky; Hell lies in wait for the transgressors."],[31,40,"The reward of the righteous","Gardens and grapes; no idle talk; 'I wish I were dust'."]],"n":{"7":"And the mountains as pegs?","10":"And the night as a covering?","13":"And placed a burning lamp (the sun)?","35":"They will hear no vain talk there, nor lies.","40":"The disbeliever will say: 'I wish I were dust!'"},"l":[["Sleep is a sign",9,"Even rest is a gift and a reminder of death and waking."],["Expect the account",27,"Living as if unaccountable is the root of ruin."],["Paradise is free of falsehood",35,"Prepare your tongue for it now."]],"r":["Which of the signs in verses 6–16 did I benefit from today?","How would I live differently if I truly expected an account?"],"k":[1,2,6,8,9,14,17,27]},"79":{"h":["An-Nazi'at was revealed in Makkah. It opens with oaths by angels who extract souls, some forcefully (from disbelievers) and some gently (from believers), and by those who swim, race and arrange affairs.","The Quraysh mocked: 'Will we be returned to our former state, even when we are decayed bones?' The surah answers by telling the story of Musa and Pharaoh, who proclaimed 'I am your lord, most high', and by pointing to the creation of the heavens and earth.","It closes with the question of the Hour. When asked about its time, the Prophet ﷺ held up two fingers and said: 'I have been sent, and the Hour, like these two' (Bukhari 4936)."],"s":[[1,14,"Souls extracted, hearts trembling","Oaths by the angels; 'Will we be returned when we are bones?'"],[15,26,"Musa and Pharaoh","Go to Pharaoh; he has transgressed. 'I am your lord, most high.'"],[27,33,"Are you harder to create?","Or the heaven He built? The earth spread out, water, pasture and mountains."],[34,46,"The great catastrophe","Whoever restrains the soul from desire, Paradise is the refuge; the Hour."]],"n":{"2":"And those who draw out gently.","10":"They say: 'Will we be returned to our former state?'","17":"Go to Pharaoh; he has transgressed.","18":"'Would you purify yourself?' The gentlest invitation to the greatest tyrant.","24":"Pharaoh: 'I am your lord, most high.'","37":"As for one who transgressed and preferred the life of this world.","40":"As for one who feared standing before his Lord and restrained the soul from desire.","42":"They ask you about the Hour: when is its arrival?","46":"On the Day they see it, it will be as if they had stayed only an evening or a morning."},"l":[["Invite gently",18,"Even to Pharaoh, the call was 'would you purify yourself?'"],["Restrain desire",40,"The key to Paradise."],["Life is short",46,"It will feel like an evening or a morning."]],"r":["Which desire do I most need to restrain?","If my whole life will feel like 'an evening', how should I spend today?"],"k":[1,27,41]},"80":{"h":["'Abasa was revealed in Makkah. The Prophet ﷺ was speaking with leaders of Quraysh, hoping they would accept Islam, when 'Abd Allah ibn Umm Maktum, a blind Companion, came asking to be taught. The Prophet ﷺ frowned and turned away, focused on the leaders (Tirmidhi 3331).","Allah revealed these verses gently correcting him: the one who comes seeking guidance deserves attention, however lowly in society. Afterward the Prophet ﷺ honoured Ibn Umm Maktum, greeting him with 'Welcome to the one for whose sake my Lord admonished me', and later appointed him to lead prayer in Madinah when he travelled.","That the Quran preserves a correction of the Prophet ﷺ himself is a sign of its honesty. The surah then turns to human ingratitude, the food Allah provides, and the Day when a person flees from brother, mother, father, spouse and children."],"s":[[1,16,"He frowned","The blind seeker and the wealthy man; the Quran in honoured pages."],[17,32,"Man's ingratitude","From what was he created? From a drop; look at your food."],[33,42,"The Deafening Blast","A man flees from his brother, mother, father, wife and children."]],"n":{"3":"How could you know? Perhaps he might be purified.","13":"The Quran is in honoured pages.","37":"Every person that Day will have enough concern of his own."},"l":[["Every seeker matters",3,"Never judge who deserves your attention by status."],["Look at your food",24,"Every meal is a chain of miracles."],["Each soul alone",37,"Prepare your own account."]],"r":["Whom do I give less attention because of their status?","At my next meal, think of verses 24–32."],"k":[1,2,5,8,9,24,25,34,35,38]},"81":{"h":["At-Takwir was revealed in Makkah, in the early period. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Whoever would like to look at the Day of Resurrection as if seeing it with his own eyes, let him read At-Takwir, Al-Infitar and Al-Inshiqaq' (Tirmidhi 3333).","Its opening twelve verses describe the collapse of the universe: the sun folded up, the stars falling, the mountains moved, pregnant camels abandoned, wild beasts gathered, seas set ablaze, and souls paired.","Among these cosmic scenes is a moment of justice: 'when the girl buried alive is asked for what sin she was killed' (verses 8–9). Some Arab tribes buried infant daughters alive out of fear of poverty or shame. Islam abolished this, and the Quran gives the victim the voice."],"s":[[1,14,"When the sun is folded up","Twelve scenes of the end, then every soul will know what it has brought."],[15,29,"The noble Messenger","Oaths by the stars and dawn; the Quran is the word of a noble messenger, a reminder for the worlds."]],"n":{"4":"When full-term she-camels are neglected: the most precious possession, forgotten.","10":"When the pages are spread open.","19":"It is the word of a noble messenger (Jibril)."},"l":[["Justice for the voiceless",8,"Allah will give voice to every victim."],["You will see your record",10,"Live knowing your pages will be opened."],["Where are you going?",26,"Ask yourself honestly."]],"r":["Answer verse 26 honestly: where am I going?","Who are the 'voiceless' around me that I can help today?"],"k":[1,2,8,9,14,18,22,26,27,29]},"82":{"h":["Al-Infitar was revealed in Makkah, and is one of the three surahs the Prophet ﷺ recommended for seeing the Day of Resurrection 'as if with your own eyes' (Tirmidhi 3333).","It opens with the sky splitting, stars scattering, seas erupting and graves overturned. Then it asks one of the most piercing questions in the Quran: 'O man, what has deceived you about your Lord, the Most Generous, who created you, proportioned you and balanced you?' (verses 6–7).","It reminds us of the noble recording angels (kiraman katibin) who know all we do, and ends: on that Day, the command belongs entirely to Allah."],"s":[[1,5,"When the sky breaks apart","Every soul will know what it sent forward and left behind."],[6,12,"What has deceived you?","Created and balanced by the Generous; noble angels recording."],[13,19,"The righteous and the wicked","The righteous in bliss; a Day when no soul can help another."]],"n":{"19":"The Day no soul will have power to do anything for another; the command that Day is Allah's."},"l":[["Do not be deceived by His generosity",6,"His kindness is not a reason to be careless."],["Your body is a gift",7,"Created and balanced with care."],["Your deeds are noted",11,"By noble angels."]],"r":["What has made me careless about my Lord?","Look at your hands and think of verse 7."],"k":[1,5,6,7,8,10,11,12,13]},"83":{"h":["Al-Mutaffifin is classified by many as the last surah revealed in Makkah or the first in Madinah. Ibn 'Abbas reported that when the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madinah, its people were among the worst in giving short measure, and after this surah was revealed, they improved (Ibn Majah, Nasa'i).","It warns those who take full measure for themselves but give less to others, as if they will never be resurrected. It describes the records of the wicked in Sijjin and the righteous in 'Illiyyun.","It contains a key teaching on the heart: sins form a stain (ran) upon hearts (verse 14). The Prophet ﷺ explained that each sin leaves a black spot; if one repents, it is polished away; if one continues, it spreads until it covers the heart (Tirmidhi 3334)."],"s":[[1,6,"Woe to the cheats","Those who take full measure but give less."],[7,17,"Sijjin and the stain on hearts","The record of the wicked; what they earned has stained their hearts."],[18,28,"'Illiyyun and the pure drink","The record of the righteous; let those who compete, compete for this."],[29,36,"The last laugh","They used to laugh at the believers; today the believers laugh."]],"n":{"14":"'Rather, what they earned has covered their hearts like rust' (Tirmidhi 3334).","26":"Its seal is musk; for this let the competitors compete."},"l":[["Honesty in every measure",3,"Work, trade, time, all must be fair."],["Polish your heart",14,"Repentance removes the stain."],["Compete for what lasts",26,"Not for wealth, but for Paradise."]],"r":["Where do I 'give short measure': at work, in my time, in my promises?","What sins might be staining my heart, and when will I polish it?"],"k":[1,2,3,4,6,15,18,29,34]},"84":{"h":["Al-Inshiqaq was revealed in Makkah, and is the third of the surahs the Prophet ﷺ recommended for seeing the Day of Resurrection vividly (Tirmidhi 3333). Abu Hurayrah recited it in prayer and prostrated at verse 21, saying he had seen the Prophet ﷺ prostrate there (Bukhari 766).","It describes the sky splitting open and the earth stretched out, both 'listening to their Lord and obeying, as they must'.","Then it addresses every human: 'O man, you are labouring toward your Lord with great exertion, and you will meet Him' (verse 6). Some will receive their record in the right hand and return to their families rejoicing; others will receive it behind their backs."],"s":[[1,15,"Labouring toward your Lord","The sky and earth obey; two kinds of record."],[16,25,"Stage after stage","Oaths by twilight and the moon; why do they not believe and prostrate?"]],"n":{"2":"And listens to its Lord, as it must.","9":"And return to his family rejoicing.","13":"He used to be among his family happy, in heedlessness.","19":"You will surely pass from stage to stage."},"l":[["Every effort leads to Him",6,"All life is a journey toward meeting Allah."],["Seek an easy account",8,"Live so your reckoning is easy."],["Life moves in stages",19,"Each stage is a step on the journey."]],"r":["If my whole life is 'labouring toward my Lord', what am I bringing?","Ask Allah today for an easy account."],"k":[1,6,7,8,18,21]},"85":{"h":["Al-Buruj was revealed in Makkah, when believers like Bilal, Khabbab and the family of Yasir were being tortured for their faith. It tells of 'the companions of the trench' (ashab al-ukhdud), believers who were thrown into a fiery trench by a tyrant while he and his men sat watching.","Muslim (3005) records a long hadith about a boy who learned faith from a monk, survived repeated attempts by a king to kill him, and finally told the king the only way to kill him was to say 'In the name of Allah, Lord of the boy' before shooting. When the boy died, the people declared their faith, and the king had trenches dug and set ablaze for them.","The surah consoles the persecuted: the true victory is theirs, and Allah, the Forgiving, the Loving, witnesses everything."],"s":[[1,11,"The companions of the trench","Believers burned for their faith while the tyrants watched; they resented them only for believing."],[12,22,"The Loving Lord","Your Lord's grip is severe; He is the Forgiving, the Loving; Pharaoh and Thamud; a preserved Tablet."]],"n":{"11":"Those who believe and do good: that is the great success.","14":"He is the Forgiving, the Loving (al-Wadud).","16":"Doer of whatever He intends.","22":"In a preserved Tablet."},"l":[["True success is faith",11,"Even martyrdom is 'the great success'."],["Repentance is open even for persecutors",10,"'And then did not repent': the door was open."],["Allah is the Loving",14,"His love surrounds the believers in trial."]],"r":["What does 'the great success' (85:11) mean to me?","Think of believers persecuted today. How can I support them?"],"k":[1,4,7,8,10]},"86":{"h":["At-Tariq was revealed in Makkah, in the early period. Its name means 'the night-comer', explained in the surah as 'the piercing star'.","It states that every soul has a guardian watching over it, then asks humans to look at what they were created from, a fluid emitted from between the backbone and the ribs. He who created you is able to bring you back on the Day when secrets are tested.","It ends by affirming that the Quran is a decisive word, not a jest, and tells the Prophet ﷺ to give the disbelievers respite a little while."],"s":[[1,10,"Every soul has a guardian","The piercing star; created from fluid; secrets tested."],[11,17,"A decisive word","The Quran is decisive, not jest; give the disbelievers a little time."]],"n":{"13":"It is a decisive word.","17":"So allow time for the disbelievers; leave them awhile."},"l":[["You are watched over",4,"No soul is alone."],["Secrets will be tested",9,"Purify your private life."],["Take the Quran seriously",14,"It is decisive, not entertainment."]],"r":["What secret would I least like tested? What should I do about it?","Do I treat the Quran as decisive guidance or as background sound?"],"k":[1,3,4,5,6,8,9,14]},"87":{"h":["Al-A'la was revealed in Makkah, in the early period. The Prophet ﷺ loved it. He recited it with Al-Ghashiyah in the Friday and 'Eid prayers (Muslim 878), and in the Witr prayer. When 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High' was revealed, he said: 'Make it (subhana Rabbiya al-A'la) in your prostration' (Abu Dawud).","It reassures the Prophet ﷺ that he will not forget the revelation except what Allah wills, and that Allah will ease him toward ease.","It declares that the successful one is he who purifies himself and remembers his Lord's name and prays, and that people prefer this worldly life, though the Hereafter is better and more lasting. This, it says, is in the earliest scriptures of Ibrahim and Musa."],"s":[[1,8,"Glorify the Most High","He created and proportioned, measured and guided; We will make you recite so you do not forget."],[9,19,"Remind, and the successful","Remind if reminding benefits; he succeeds who purifies himself; the Hereafter is better."]],"n":{"1":"Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High. Said in every prostration.","3":"Who measured and guided.","19":"The scriptures of Ibrahim and Musa."},"l":[["Glorify in prostration",1,"Every sujud is a fulfilment of this verse."],["Allah eases the way",8,"Ask Him for ease."],["Success is purification",14,"Purify the heart, remember, pray."],["Prefer what lasts",17,"The Hereafter is better and lasting."]],"r":["When I say 'subhana Rabbiya al-A'la' in prostration, do I feel what it means?","What choice today shows whether I prefer this world or the next?"],"k":[2,6,8,9,14,15,16,17]},"88":{"h":["Al-Ghashiyah was revealed in Makkah. The Prophet ﷺ recited it in the Friday and 'Eid prayers alongside Al-A'la (Muslim 878).","It describes 'the Overwhelming Event': on that Day some faces will be humbled, labouring and weary, while others will be joyful, pleased with their efforts, in a lofty garden.","It then turns to the Bedouin's everyday world as proof: 'Do they not look at the camels, how they were created? And at the sky, how it was raised? And at the mountains, how they were set? And at the earth, how it was spread?' (verses 17–20). The Prophet's ﷺ role is to remind; he is not a controller over them."],"s":[[1,16,"Two kinds of faces","Humbled and weary; joyful and pleased."],[17,26,"Look around you","The camel, the sky, the mountains, the earth; you are only a reminder."]],"n":{"3":"Working hard and exhausted: effort in this world that was wasted.","21":"So remind; you are only a reminder."},"l":[["Not all effort is rewarded",3,"Make sure your effort is for the right goal."],["Contentment with effort",9,"Strive so that you will be pleased with it."],["Look and reflect",17,"The ordinary world is full of signs."],["Remind, do not control",22,"Our duty is to convey, not to force."]],"r":["Which of my efforts will I be 'satisfied with' on that Day?","Look up at the sky tonight with verse 18 in mind."],"k":[1,2,8,9,11,17,18,19,20,22]},"89":{"h":["Al-Fajr was revealed in Makkah. It opens with oaths by the dawn, ten nights (understood by many to be the first ten nights of Dhul-Hijjah, or the last ten of Ramadan), the even and the odd, and the night when it passes.","It recalls three powerful civilisations: 'Ad of Iram with lofty pillars, Thamud who carved the rocks in the valley, and Pharaoh of the stakes. All transgressed and were struck by their Lord's whip of punishment.","It corrects a common human mistake: thinking wealth means Allah has honoured you and poverty means He has humiliated you. Both are tests. The real failure is not honouring the orphan, not feeding the poor, devouring inheritance and loving wealth excessively. It ends with the tender call to the soul at peace."],"s":[[1,14,"The dawn and the fallen nations","'Ad of Iram, Thamud, Pharaoh; your Lord is ever watchful."],[15,20,"Wealth and poverty are tests","You do not honour the orphan or urge feeding the poor."],[21,30,"The soul at peace","When the earth is pounded; 'O soul at peace, return to your Lord.'"]],"n":{"14":"Your Lord is ever watchful.","15":"When his Lord tests him and is generous to him, he says: 'My Lord has honoured me.'","16":"When He restricts his provision, he says: 'My Lord has humiliated me.'","17":"No! You do not honour the orphan.","24":"He will say: 'I wish I had sent ahead for my life.'"},"l":[["Both wealth and poverty are tests",15,"Neither proves Allah's love or anger."],["Honour the orphan",17,"A sign of a healthy heart."],["Send ahead for your real life",24,"The Hereafter is the true life."],["Seek a soul at peace",27,"Contentment with Allah is the goal."]],"r":["When I have less, do I feel Allah has abandoned me? Read verses 15–17 again.","What would make my soul 'at peace' (mutma'innah)?"],"k":[1,2,6,7,18,20,27,28,30]},"90":{"h":["Al-Balad was revealed in Makkah. It begins with an oath by 'this city' (Makkah), 'while you, O Prophet, are free (or made lawful) in this city'. Commentators explain this as the Prophet ﷺ being persecuted in the very city that should have been safe, or as a promise that he would one day enter it with authority.","It declares that humans were created into toil and struggle (kabad). Allah gave them two eyes, a tongue and two lips, and showed them the two paths.","Then it explains the 'steep path' (al-'aqabah) that few attempt: freeing a slave, feeding on a day of hunger an orphan relative or a poor person in misery, and being among those who believe and urge one another to patience and compassion."],"s":[[1,10,"Created into toil","An oath by Makkah; does man think no one has power over him? Two eyes, a tongue, two paths."],[11,20,"The steep path","Freeing a slave, feeding the hungry, and urging patience and compassion."]],"n":{"5":"Does he think no one will have power over him?","6":"He says: 'I have spent wealth in abundance.'","11":"But he has not attempted the steep path."},"l":[["Struggle is normal",4,"Hardship is part of human life, not a sign of failure."],["You have the tools",10,"Eyes, tongue and the knowledge of two paths."],["Climb the steep path",11,"Generosity in hard times is the true climb."],["Patience and compassion together",17,"Remind one another of both."]],"r":["What is my 'steep path' that I have been avoiding?","Whom could I feed or free from hardship this week?"],"k":[1,2,4,8,9,10,13,14,15,16,17]},"91":{"h":["Ash-Shams was revealed in Makkah. It contains the longest series of oaths in the Quran: by the sun and its brightness, the moon, the day, the night, the sky and its construction, the earth and its expanse, and the soul and its proportion. All of them lead to a single statement about the human soul.","Allah inspired the soul with its wickedness and its piety. 'He has succeeded who purifies it, and he has failed who corrupts it' (verses 9–10). The Prophet ﷺ would pray: 'O Allah, give my soul its piety and purify it; You are the best to purify it' (Muslim).","The surah illustrates this with Thamud, who were given a she-camel as a sign. The most wretched of them hamstrung her, and the whole people were destroyed because they all approved."],"s":[[1,10,"Eleven oaths, one truth","The soul inspired with wickedness and piety; success in purification."],[11,15,"Thamud and the she-camel","The most wretched one rose; they denied and hamstrung her; Allah levelled them."]],"n":{"13":"The messenger of Allah said: 'This is the she-camel of Allah; let her drink.'"},"l":[["You know good and bad",8,"The soul is inspired with both."],["Purify the soul",9,"Success is inner purification."],["Silence can be complicity",14,"One acted; all approved; all were punished."]],"r":["What is one habit that 'corrupts' my soul? One that purifies it?","Make the Prophet's ﷺ du'a today: 'O Allah, give my soul its piety.'"],"k":[1,7,8,9,10,11,12,14,15]},"92":{"h":["Al-Layl was revealed in Makkah. It contrasts two kinds of people. Whoever gives, fears Allah and affirms the best reward, Allah will ease him toward ease. Whoever is stingy, considers himself free of need and denies the best reward, Allah will ease him toward hardship.","Many commentators link verses 17–21 to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, who spent his wealth freeing persecuted slaves such as Bilal, not to repay any favour but seeking only the face of his Lord, Most High.","The Prophet ﷺ cited verses 5–10 when Companions asked why they should act if destinies are written: 'Act, for each is eased toward what he was created for' (Bukhari, Muslim)."],"s":[[1,11,"Diverse efforts","The giver eased to ease; the stingy eased to hardship."],[12,21,"Guidance and the God-fearing","Upon Us is guidance; the most God-fearing gives to purify himself, seeking only Allah's face."]],"n":{"17":"The most God-fearing will be kept far from it.","19":"Not for any favour to be repaid.","21":"And he will surely be satisfied."},"l":[["Giving opens ease",7,"Generosity smooths the path of life."],["Feeling 'self-sufficient' is dangerous",8,"Need of Allah is the truth."],["Give purely for Allah",20,"Not for return, praise or obligation."],["He will be satisfied",21,"The promise to the sincere giver."]],"r":["Do I give to purify myself, or to be seen?","Where in my life do I feel 'self-sufficient' and forget Allah?"],"k":[4,5,6,7,8,10,11,12,18,20]},"93":{"h":["Ad-Duha was revealed in Makkah, early in the mission. After the first revelations, there was a pause (fatrah). Jundub reported that the Prophet ﷺ fell ill and did not pray at night for a night or two, and a woman (reportedly Umm Jamil, wife of Abu Lahab) mocked him: 'I see your devil has left you' (Bukhari 4950).","Then Allah revealed: 'By the morning brightness, and the night when it grows still, your Lord has not abandoned you, nor has He become displeased.' It is one of the most comforting surahs in the Quran.","It reminds the Prophet ﷺ of his past: an orphan whom Allah sheltered, wandering and guided, in need and enriched. Then it draws the lesson: do not oppress the orphan, do not repel the one who asks, and speak of your Lord's blessings."],"s":[[1,5,"Your Lord has not abandoned you","Morning light after night; the Hereafter is better; you will be satisfied."],[6,11,"Remember and give back","An orphan sheltered, wandering and guided, poor and enriched: so be kind to others."]],"n":{"10":"And as for the one who asks, do not repel him.","11":"And as for the favour of your Lord, speak of it."},"l":[["Allah never abandons",3,"Silence is not abandonment."],["The best is yet to come",4,"Hope for what is ahead."],["Remember where you came from",6,"Then treat others as Allah treated you."],["Speak of blessings",11,"Gratitude spoken aloud."]],"r":["When did I feel abandoned by Allah? What was He preparing?","Which of Allah's blessings could I speak about today?"],"k":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9]},"94":{"h":["Ash-Sharh (also Al-Inshirah) was revealed in Makkah, soon after Ad-Duha, and continues its comfort. Many early scholars read the two as companion surahs.","It asks: 'Did We not expand your chest for you, and remove your burden which weighed down your back, and raise high your mention?' The Prophet's ﷺ name is now mentioned with Allah's in the call to prayer, the testimony of faith and every prayer.","Then comes a repeated promise: 'With hardship comes ease; with hardship comes ease.' Scholars note that 'hardship' (al-'usr) is definite and 'ease' (yusr) indefinite, so one hardship is followed by more than one ease. Ibn Mas'ud is reported to have said: one hardship will never overcome two eases."],"s":[[1,4,"Did We not expand your chest?","Burden lifted, mention raised."],[5,8,"With hardship comes ease","Twice promised; when you finish, stand up in worship; turn to your Lord."]],"n":{"1":"Did We not expand for you your chest?"},"l":[["An open chest",1,"Ask Allah to expand your chest."],["Ease is with hardship",5,"Not only after it, but with it."],["Move from task to worship",7,"Productivity and devotion together."],["Long for your Lord",8,"Make Him the goal."]],"r":["What hardship am I in? What ease is already with it?","When I finish my work today, what worship will I turn to?"],"k":[2,3,4,5,6,7,8]},"95":{"h":["At-Tin was revealed in Makkah. Al-Bara' reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited it in the 'Isha prayer while on a journey, and he never heard anyone with a more beautiful voice (Bukhari 767).","It opens with oaths by the fig, the olive, Mount Sinai and 'this secure city'. Many commentators explain these as places of revelation: the land of the fig and olive, where 'Isa was sent; Sinai, where Musa received the Torah; and Makkah, where Muhammad ﷺ was sent.","Its message is human dignity and its loss: Allah created humans in the best form, then returns them to the lowest of the low, except those who believe and do good."],"s":[[1,3,"Oaths by the lands of revelation","The fig, the olive, Sinai, the secure city."],[4,8,"The best form and the lowest","Created in the best stature; is not Allah the most just of judges?"]],"n":{"3":"And this secure city (Makkah)."},"l":[["Human dignity",4,"Every person is created in the best form."],["Dignity can be lost",5,"Sin lowers what Allah raised."],["Faith and deeds preserve it",6,"The way to stay honoured."]],"r":["How do I treat my body and mind, created 'in the best form'?","What lowers me, and what lifts me?"],"k":[1,2,4,5,6,7,8]},"96":{"h":["The first five verses of Al-'Alaq were the first revelation of the Quran, in 610 CE. 'A'ishah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to retreat to the cave of Hira on Mount Nur. The angel Jibril came and said: 'Read!' He replied: 'I am not a reader.' The angel embraced him tightly three times, then said: 'Read in the name of your Lord who created' (Bukhari 3).","The Prophet ﷺ returned home trembling to Khadijah, who comforted him: 'Allah will never disgrace you. You keep ties of kinship, bear the weak, help the destitute, honour guests and support people in hardship.' She took him to her cousin Waraqah ibn Nawfal, who recognised the same angel that came to Musa.","The rest of the surah (verses 6–19) came later, about Abu Jahl, who threatened to step on the Prophet's ﷺ neck if he saw him prostrating at the Ka'bah. It ends with the command: 'Prostrate and draw near.'"],"s":[[1,5,"Read!","The first revelation: read in your Lord's name; He taught by the pen."],[6,19,"The one who forbids a servant to pray","Man transgresses when he sees himself self-sufficient; prostrate and draw near."]],"n":{"1":"'Read in the name of your Lord who created.' The first word of revelation.","2":"Created man from a clinging substance ('alaq)."},"l":[["Knowledge begins with Allah's name",1,"Learning is worship when done in His name."],["The pen is a gift",4,"Writing and learning are blessings."],["Self-sufficiency breeds arrogance",7,"Remember your need of Allah."],["Draw near through prostration",19,"The closest a servant is to Allah."]],"r":["Do I begin my studies and work 'in the name of my Lord'?","When I prostrate, do I feel I am drawing near?"],"k":[3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,14,19]},"97":{"h":["Al-Qadr is considered Makkan by most scholars. It celebrates the night on which the Quran was first sent down, Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Decree or Power.","The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Seek it in the odd nights of the last ten of Ramadan' (Bukhari 2017). He also said: 'Whoever stands in prayer on Laylat al-Qadr out of faith and seeking reward will have his past sins forgiven' (Bukhari).","'A'ishah asked what she should say if she found that night. He taught her: 'Allahumma innaka 'afuwwun tuhibbu al-'afwa fa'fu 'anni': O Allah, You are Pardoning and love to pardon, so pardon me (Tirmidhi)."],"s":[[1,5,"The Night of Decree","Better than a thousand months; angels and the Spirit descend; peace until dawn."]],"n":{"3":"The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months: more than 83 years.","4":"The angels and the Spirit (Jibril) descend therein by permission of their Lord for every matter."},"l":[["One night can outweigh a lifetime",3,"Never underestimate a moment of worship."],["Seek it in the last ten nights",1,"Prepare for Ramadan's final nights."],["Peace descends",5,"Seek inner peace in worship."]],"r":["How will I prepare for the last ten nights of the next Ramadan?","Memorise the du'a 'Allahumma innaka 'afuwwun...'"],"k":[1,2,5]},"98":{"h":["Al-Bayyinah was revealed in Madinah. Anas reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b: 'Allah has commanded me to recite to you: Lam yakun alladhina kafaru.' Ubayy asked: 'Did He mention me by name?' The Prophet ﷺ said yes, and Ubayy wept (Bukhari 4959).","It explains that the People of the Book and the idolaters would not depart from their ways until the clear evidence (al-bayyinah) came: a Messenger reciting purified pages.","It defines the essence of religion in one verse: to worship Allah sincerely, upright, to establish prayer and give zakah (verse 5). It names the best of creation and the worst, and ends with mutual pleasure between Allah and the righteous."],"s":[[1,5,"The clear evidence","A Messenger reciting purified pages; religion is sincere worship, prayer and zakah."],[6,8,"Best and worst of creation","Those who believe and do good are the best; Allah is pleased with them and they with Him."]],"n":{"5":"They were commanded only to worship Allah, sincere to Him in religion, upright, to pray and give zakah."},"l":[["Religion in one verse",5,"Sincerity, prayer and charity."],["Clarity removes excuses",4,"Division after clear truth is a choice."],["Mutual pleasure",8,"The highest reward: Allah pleased with you and you with Him."]],"r":["Imagine Allah mentioning you by name. How would you feel, like Ubayy?","Am I pleased with Allah, with His decree in my life?"],"k":[1,2,4,6,7,8]},"99":{"h":["Az-Zalzalah is classified as Madinan by some scholars and Makkan by others. It describes the final earthquake, when the earth will bring out its burdens (the dead and buried treasures).","On that Day the earth will 'report its news'. The Prophet ﷺ explained that it will bear witness against every man and woman for what they did upon its surface: 'He did such and such on such and such a day' (Tirmidhi).","It ends with two of the most comprehensive verses in the Quran: 'Whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.' The Prophet ﷺ called them 'the unique, all-encompassing verse' (Bukhari)."],"s":[[1,5,"The earth's report","The earth shakes, brings out its burdens, and tells its news."],[6,8,"Every atom counts","People come out in scattered groups to be shown their deeds."]],"n":{"3":"Man says: 'What is wrong with it?'"},"l":[["The earth is a witness",4,"Every place you stand remembers."],["Small good matters",7,"Never despise a small good deed."],["Small evil matters",8,"Never belittle a small sin."]],"r":["What small good could I do today that I would be happy to see on that Day?","What would the ground under my feet testify about me?"],"k":[1,2,4,5,6,7,8]},"100":{"h":["Al-'Adiyat was revealed in Makkah, according to most scholars. It opens with a vivid scene: war horses charging, panting, striking sparks with their hooves, raiding at dawn, raising dust and penetrating the enemy's centre.","The point of this image is a contrast. The horse is utterly loyal to its master, charging into danger for him, while 'man is ungrateful to his Lord' (verse 6) and witnesses this himself.","It declares that man's love of wealth is intense, and asks: does he not know that when the graves are overturned and what is in the hearts is brought out, his Lord will be fully aware of them?"],"s":[[1,5,"The charging horses","Loyal steeds galloping into battle at dawn."],[6,11,"Man's ingratitude","Ungrateful, a witness to it, and intense in love of wealth; what is in hearts brought out."]],"n":{},"l":[["Loyalty versus ingratitude",6,"Even a horse is loyal to its master."],["We know our own ingratitude",7,"Honesty with oneself."],["Hearts will be laid bare",10,"Purify intentions now."]],"r":["In what way am I less loyal to Allah than a horse to its rider?","What is in my heart that I would not want 'brought out'?"],"k":[1,3,6,7,8,9,10,11]},"101":{"h":["Al-Qari'ah was revealed in Makkah. Its name, 'the Striking Calamity', is a name for the Day of Judgment, which strikes hearts with terror.","It describes people on that Day as scattered moths, confused and helpless, and the mighty mountains as fluffed wool.","Then comes the weighing of deeds: whoever's scales are heavy will be in a pleasant life; whoever's scales are light, his home will be an abyss (hawiyah), a blazing fire."],"s":[[1,5,"The Striking Calamity","People like scattered moths; mountains like fluffed wool."],[6,11,"The scales","Heavy scales: a pleasant life. Light scales: the abyss."]],"n":{},"l":[["Strength means nothing that Day",5,"Even mountains become wool."],["Make your scale heavy",6,"Good character is the heaviest deed on the scale (Tirmidhi)."]],"r":["What deeds am I adding to my scale this week?","The Prophet ﷺ said good character is the heaviest thing on the scale. How is mine?"],"k":[1,2,4,5,6,7,8,9]},"102":{"h":["At-Takathur was revealed in Makkah, according to most scholars. Commentators report it concerns two clans of Quraysh who competed in boasting about their numbers, until they began counting their dead in the graves.","'Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was reciting it, and he said: 'The son of Adam says: my wealth, my wealth! But do you have from your wealth anything except what you ate and consumed, or wore and wore out, or gave in charity and sent ahead?' (Muslim 2958).","It ends with a sobering warning: 'Then you will surely be asked that Day about the pleasures.' The Prophet ﷺ once ate dates and drank cool water with Abu Bakr and 'Umar and said these were among the blessings they would be asked about (Muslim)."],"s":[[1,2,"Distracted by increase","Competition for more distracts you until you visit the graves."],[3,8,"You will know","With certain knowledge you will see Hell; you will be asked about the blessings."]],"n":{"3":"No! You will soon know."},"l":[["More is a distraction",1,"Competing for wealth and status empties life."],["Death ends the race",2,"Everyone visits the grave."],["Every blessing will be asked about",8,"Even cool water and dates."]],"r":["What am I competing for 'more' of?","Which blessing today will I be asked about? Did I thank Allah for it?"],"k":[1,2,5,6,7,8]},"103":{"h":["Al-'Asr was revealed in Makkah. Imam al-Shafi'i said: 'If people reflected on this surah alone, it would be sufficient for them.' Some reports say that when two Companions met, they would not part until one had recited Al-'Asr to the other (Tabarani).","In three verses it contains a complete path to salvation. Allah swears by time, the medium in which every life is spent, that humanity is in loss.","The exception requires four things: faith, righteous deeds, urging one another to truth, and urging one another to patience. The last two are communal: salvation is not only individual."],"s":[[1,3,"By time","Humanity is in loss, except four qualities."]],"n":{"3":"Except those who believe, do righteous deeds, advise one another to truth, and advise one another to patience."},"l":[["Time is life",1,"Every moment wasted is lost forever."],["Loss is the default",2,"Without effort, we lose."],["Four keys",3,"Faith, deeds, truth and patience, shared with others."]],"r":["How did I spend my last 24 hours?","Who do I 'urge to truth and patience', and who urges me?"],"k":[1,2]},"104":{"h":["Al-Humazah was revealed in Makkah. Commentators report it was revealed about Quraysh figures such as al-Akhnas ibn Shariq or Umayyah ibn Khalaf, who mocked and slandered the Prophet ﷺ and the poor believers, though the warning applies to anyone with these traits.","It condemns two sins of the tongue: humazah (backbiting, slandering) and lumazah (mocking, defaming), often with gestures, combined with hoarding wealth and counting it, thinking it will make one immortal.","Their punishment is al-Hutamah, 'the Crusher', a fire that reaches into the hearts."],"s":[[1,4,"The slanderer and hoarder","Woe to every backbiter and mocker who gathers wealth thinking it will make him immortal."],[5,9,"The Crusher","A fire that rises over the hearts, closed in, in extended columns."]],"n":{},"l":[["Guard your tongue",1,"Mocking and slander are grave sins."],["Wealth does not make you immortal",3,"Counting money does not count your days."],["The punishment reaches the heart",7,"Because the sin began there."]],"r":["Have I mocked anyone, in words or with a look, this week?","Do I spend more time counting my wealth or my deeds?"],"k":[1,2,3,4,6,7]},"105":{"h":["Al-Fil was revealed in Makkah about an event the Quraysh knew well. In the year the Prophet ﷺ was born, around 570 CE, Abrahah, the Abyssinian ruler of Yemen, built a great church in San'a' to draw pilgrims away from the Ka'bah. When it was defiled, he marched on Makkah with a large army and at least one war elephant to destroy the Ka'bah.","'Abd al-Muttalib, the Prophet's ﷺ grandfather, asked Abrahah only for his camels, saying: 'I am the lord of the camels; the House has a Lord who will protect it.' The Makkans withdrew to the mountains.","The elephant refused to march toward the Ka'bah. Then Allah sent flocks of birds (ababil) carrying stones of baked clay, and the army was left like eaten straw. The Arabs called it 'the Year of the Elephant' and dated events by it."],"s":[[1,5,"The army of the elephant","Their plan foiled; birds with stones of baked clay; like eaten straw."]],"n":{},"l":[["Allah protects His House",1,"No army can defeat Allah's will."],["Plans of the arrogant fail",2,"Power is nothing against Allah."],["Small things can defeat great ones",3,"Birds defeated an army."]],"r":["What 'elephant' am I afraid of? Who is my protector?","Like 'Abd al-Muttalib, what should I leave to Allah's care?"],"k":[1,2,3,4,5]},"106":{"h":["Quraysh was revealed in Makkah and is closely connected to Al-Fil; some early copies wrote them without separation, though they are two surahs.","The Quraysh lived in a barren valley, yet thrived through two trade caravans: in winter to Yemen and in summer to Syria. Because they were guardians of the Ka'bah, other tribes respected them, and their caravans travelled safely while the rest of Arabia was plagued by raids.","The surah reminds them: Allah protected the House from Abrahah, gave you secure trade, fed you against hunger and made you safe from fear. So worship the Lord of this House."],"s":[[1,4,"For the security of Quraysh","Winter and summer journeys; worship the Lord of this House, who fed and protected you."]],"n":{},"l":[["Security and food are blessings",4,"Two gifts we take for granted."],["Gratitude through worship",3,"The right response to blessings."]],"r":["Am I fed and safe today? Have I thanked the One who provided it?","How can worship be my gratitude?"],"k":[1,2,3,4]},"107":{"h":["Al-Ma'un is considered Makkan by most scholars, though some think its second half was revealed in Madinah because it mentions those who pray only to show off, a trait of the hypocrites.","Commentators link its first part to a Quraysh leader, often said to be Abu Jahl or Abu Sufyan before his Islam, who drove away an orphan asking for help.","It shows that denial of religion appears in conduct: repelling the orphan and not encouraging feeding the poor. It then warns those who pray yet are heedless of their prayer, who show off, and who withhold al-ma'un, small acts of help like lending a pot or a tool."],"s":[[1,3,"The one who denies","He repels the orphan and does not encourage feeding the poor."],[4,7,"Heedless worshippers","Woe to those who are heedless of their prayer, show off, and withhold small kindnesses."]],"n":{"7":"And withhold small kindnesses."},"l":[["Faith shows in kindness",2,"How we treat orphans reveals our belief."],["Prayer needs presence",5,"Heedless prayer is warned against."],["Small help matters",7,"Lending a tool is part of faith."]],"r":["Is there a 'small kindness' (lending, helping) I refused recently?","Am I present in my prayer, or heedless?"],"k":[1,2,3,4,5,6]},"108":{"h":["Al-Kawthar, the shortest surah, was revealed in Makkah according to most scholars. Anas reported that the Prophet ﷺ dozed, then raised his head smiling and recited it, saying: 'Al-Kawthar is a river my Lord has promised me in Paradise, with much good' (Muslim 400).","It was revealed when the Prophet's ﷺ sons died in infancy, and enemies such as al-'As ibn Wa'il mocked him as 'abtar', cut off, with no son to carry his name.","Allah answered: We have given you al-Kawthar (abundant good), so pray and sacrifice; it is your enemy who is cut off. Today, his name is mentioned in every call to prayer around the world, while the mocker's name is remembered only through this surah."],"s":[[1,3,"Abundance","Al-Kawthar given; pray and sacrifice; your enemy is the one cut off."]],"n":{},"l":[["Allah's gift outweighs loss",1,"Abundance came at the moment of grief."],["Gratitude through prayer and sacrifice",2,"The response to blessing."],["Mockers fade",3,"Truth endures."]],"r":["When have I felt 'cut off'? What abundance did Allah give me instead?","How is my prayer an act of gratitude?"],"k":[1,2,3]},"109":{"h":["Al-Kafirun was revealed in Makkah. Commentators report that the Quraysh leaders, including al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah and al-'As ibn Wa'il, proposed a compromise: 'Worship our gods for a year, and we will worship your God for a year.'","The answer came in this surah: 'I do not worship what you worship, nor do you worship what I worship... For you is your religion, and for me is mine.' It is a clear rejection of compromise in worship, while leaving each to their choice.","The Prophet ﷺ recited it with Al-Ikhlas in the two units before Fajr (Muslim 726), after Tawaf and in the Witr prayer. He also advised reciting it before sleep as a declaration of freedom from shirk (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi)."],"s":[[1,6,"To you your religion","No compromise in worship; each to their own."]],"n":{"1":"Say: O disbelievers."},"l":[["Worship is not negotiable",2,"Some things cannot be compromised."],["Respect without compromise",6,"Clarity about faith, without coercion."]],"r":["In what ways am I tempted to 'compromise' my worship for others' approval?","How can I be clear about my faith while respecting others?"],"k":[2,3,4,5,6]},"110":{"h":["An-Nasr was revealed in Madinah, among the very last revelations; Ibn 'Abbas called it the last complete surah revealed. Many report it was revealed at Mina during the Farewell Pilgrimage (10 AH), a few months before the Prophet's ﷺ death.","'Umar asked senior Companions what it meant, and they said it commands praise and forgiveness after victory. Then he asked the young Ibn 'Abbas, who said: 'It is the sign of the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ death, which Allah informed him of.' 'Umar said: 'I know of it only what you know' (Bukhari 4970).","'A'ishah reported that after it was revealed, the Prophet ﷺ frequently said in his bowing and prostration: 'Subhanaka Allahumma Rabbana wa bihamdika, Allahumma ighfir li' (Bukhari)."],"s":[[1,3,"When victory comes","People enter in crowds; glorify, praise and seek forgiveness."]],"n":{"3":"Then exalt with praise of your Lord and ask His forgiveness. He is ever Accepting of repentance."},"l":[["Victory calls for humility",3,"Success is a moment for praise and repentance, not pride."],["Endings are reminders",1,"Every completion points to the final return."],["Keep seeking forgiveness",3,"Even the Prophet ﷺ increased istighfar at the height of success."]],"r":["After my last success, did I praise Allah or myself?","Add 'Subhanaka Allahumma Rabbana wa bihamdika, Allahumma ighfir li' to your rukuʻ and sujud."],"k":[1,2]},"111":{"h":["Al-Masad was revealed in Makkah, early in the public mission. When 26:214 commanded 'Warn your closest kindred', the Prophet ﷺ climbed Mount Safa and called the clans of Quraysh. When they gathered, he warned them of a severe punishment. His uncle Abu Lahab said: 'May you perish! Is it for this you gathered us?' Then this surah was revealed (Bukhari 4770, 4971).","Abu Lahab, whose real name was 'Abd al-'Uzza ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, was a wealthy uncle of the Prophet ﷺ who followed him around markets calling him a liar. His wife, Umm Jamil, reportedly placed thorns in the Prophet's ﷺ path.","The surah is a prophecy as well as a judgment: it declared that Abu Lahab and his wife would die in disbelief. Though they lived for years afterwards and could have proven it false by simply professing Islam, they never did."],"s":[[1,5,"Perish the hands of Abu Lahab","Wealth will not help him; he and his wife in the Fire."]],"n":{},"l":[["Family ties do not save",1,"Even the Prophet's ﷺ uncle was judged by his choice."],["Wealth cannot buy salvation",2,"What he gained did not help him."],["Truth fulfils its prophecies",3,"Abu Lahab never believed, as foretold."]],"r":["Do I rely on my family background or identity instead of my own deeds?","How do I respond when someone close opposes what is right?"],"k":[1,2,3,4,5]},"112":{"h":["Al-Ikhlas was revealed in Makkah according to most scholars. Reports say the idolaters (and in other reports, Jews of Madinah) asked the Prophet ﷺ: 'Describe your Lord to us: what is His lineage?' This surah was the answer (Tirmidhi).","The Prophet ﷺ said it equals a third of the Quran (Bukhari 5013), because the Quran's themes can be grouped into three: rulings, stories and the description of Allah, and this surah contains the pure description of Allah.","A Companion who led prayers would end every recitation with Al-Ikhlas. When asked why, he said: 'Because it is the description of the Most Merciful, and I love to recite it.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Tell him that Allah loves him' (Bukhari 7375)."],"s":[[1,4,"He is Allah, the One","One, Eternal Refuge, not begetting nor begotten, without equal."]],"n":{"1":"Say: He is Allah, the One (Ahad): uniquely one, with no division and no partner.","2":"Allah, al-Samad: the Eternal Refuge, whom all creation needs and who needs no one."},"l":[["Know your Lord",1,"Tawhid is the foundation of everything."],["Turn to al-Samad",2,"All needs end with Him."],["Love what describes Allah",4,"Love for this surah is love for Allah."]],"r":["When I am in need, do I turn first to al-Samad?","Recite Al-Ikhlas three times and reflect: what does it mean that it equals a third of the Quran?"],"k":[3,4]},"113":{"h":["Al-Falaq and An-Nas are known together as al-Mu'awwidhatayn, the two surahs of seeking refuge. Scholars differ on whether they are Makkan or Madinan.","Reports in tafsir connect them to an incident in Madinah when a man named Labid ibn al-A'sam performed magic on the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ felt its effect, and the magic was found in a well. The basic incident is recorded in Bukhari and Muslim, while the link to these two surahs comes through further reports.","'Uqbah ibn 'Amir reported the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Have you not seen verses revealed tonight, the like of which has never been seen? Qul a'udhu bi-Rabbi al-falaq and Qul a'udhu bi-Rabbi al-nas' (Muslim 814). The Prophet ﷺ recited them every night before sleeping, blowing into his hands and wiping his body (Bukhari 5017)."],"s":[[1,5,"Refuge in the Lord of daybreak","From the evil of creation, darkness, magic and envy."]],"n":{"1":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak: the One who splits the darkness with dawn.","4":"And from the evil of those who blow on knots: witchcraft."},"l":[["Refuge in Allah alone",1,"Not in charms or amulets."],["Envy is real",5,"Protect yourself with remembrance, and protect others from your own envy."]],"r":["Do I recite Al-Falaq and An-Nas in the morning, evening and before sleeping?","Is there anyone I envy? Ask Allah to bless them instead."],"k":[2,3,5]},"114":{"h":["An-Nas is the last surah of the Quran and the second of the two surahs of refuge. Its circumstances are linked to Al-Falaq.","It seeks refuge in Allah through three of His attributes: Lord of mankind (Rabb), King of mankind (Malik) and God of mankind (Ilah). It seeks refuge from one evil only: the whisperer who withdraws (al-waswas al-khannas), who whispers into human hearts, from among jinn and people.","Al-Khannas means 'the one who retreats'. Ibn 'Abbas explained that Satan sits on the heart of a person: when the person remembers Allah, he retreats; when the person is heedless, he whispers. The Quran thus ends with protection of the heart, the place where guidance begins."],"s":[[1,6,"Refuge in the Lord of mankind","Lord, King and God of mankind; from the retreating whisperer among jinn and people."]],"n":{"1":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind.","2":"The King of mankind."},"l":[["Three names, complete protection",3,"Lord, King and God: He controls everything."],["Remembrance makes the whisperer retreat",4,"Dhikr is the shield of the heart."],["Whisperers can be human too",6,"Choose your influences carefully."]],"r":["What whispers do I hear most often? What dhikr could drive them away?","The Quran begins with guidance (Al-Fatihah) and ends with protection of the heart (An-Nas). What does that teach me?"],"k":[3,4,5,6]}};